   393	### Coordinator rulings (binding)
   394	- **Entry seam with Movement 3 ratified** (Sol plan audit): day 281 going down off the rise; six and no mule; the loads; deployment thirty-two; ten fragments; purse 138; bodies; the papers with Karis (the flat, the hide, the copied page in oilcloth, the dead surveyor's ledger, her notebook).
   395	- **The road hold lifts at the first hedge** below the rise: on a town's ground a person may speak and **nobody declares** (there a declaration draws a clerk, not a hound). **The Line at Treswick is the river; the sill lies under the outer gate.** Wayposts at nine, six and three miles; the six slept at the three-mile pull-in on the 281st.
   396	- **The reading inward (the 281st): the fourth and fifth self-readings**, with Tide, ROUND the still place and never in; **not counted** ("Myself, twice: not counted, as before"); a palm on a gatepost while it was open is a trial. Finding (protected): "He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*." — the tenth feels only what flows; where nothing flows it returns nothing, and that nothing is the instrument's limit, not a finding about the place (a gatepost and the middle of himself come back alike). **He needs an instrument for what does not move.** He read Oryn's order for the leave he wanted, has logged that, and **owes her the telling at the mill before anything else is said**. Lira asked and is entered as having asked. **Deployment since the Line: thirty-two; ten fragments; none acquired.**
   397	- **Vastin and Cael at the three-mile post.** Vastin SAT for the first time since the cut, of his own choosing, where he could not rise without Cael's arm. "I read your file" (no span of years). Cael: "You were the instrument," … "I was the error." (protected, Cael's). **Why he declined** (the author's, amended to locked Book 6): the case shown him at Ostrand is the true and causal reason — the office had shown him what it did with honest readings; and privately part of him was relieved never to learn what a plate would say (a second silence would have been turned on Cael; a plate that lit would have been turned on nine thousand of his own readings). A sound act, and glad of it for a bad reason. **What Cael gave him:** that the place has been read twice that day with "the one thing I've got that reads", and nothing came back because that thing only feels what moves. NOT what reads, nor fragments, the mechanism, the date. Vastin did not ask. "It is a place where two people can stand": ground, not friendship. The S7 log verbatim ("color" stands).
   398	- **Treswick (the 282nd).** The grey bridge on five piers is of dark, dry-laid stone with no mortar and is OLDER than the walls (Karis, from the masons' fitting of the pale stone to it); the same dark stone is the registry's three lowest courses, the undercroft's end and the frame of the iron door. The outer gate: one keeper counts feet on a bead-frame ("Six"); another writes only loads that come on wheels to be sold. **No name of the six was written.** The outer district works by brokers' slate boards and counted labour: cards with a coloured edge (blue to the second wall, buff to the third) carry a figure, are counted in with a pen and torn at each gate, and are good for the day named. **Iron passes the second wall by right, Bronze the third** — on a certificate that is read out and WRITTEN. The smith's two forges (charcoal; an Ember's held bed for a horse with a blue band): Brom, "They shoe by the tier." (protected). The rope-walk: sixty yards of four-strand priced (no figure printed) and to be bought going out; the Lowmarch debt stands. **The inn with two doors** (the painted door has a book; the yard half-door takes money): the long loft over the stable for a week from the 282nd, the pot at dusk, seven bowls; two marks paid; **purse 136**. Lira hung a blanket over the loft's uphill window.
   399	- **Vastin:** "I have papers I will not show. I want that said once, so that nobody asks twice." (three sheets under one seal, in oilskin, beside Karis's receipt). He has certified Treswick on four occasions. His cutaway: "the first honest district". He has sat three times; no age; no wall; **the right knee is day five of thirty on the 284th**; he came down twenty-two steps and caught one slip with his left hand.
   400	- **Seln's report (the 282nd night):** the old town stands on a quarry cut from inside; the lower archive is behind one iron door in the undercroft beneath the registry's yard; a practitioner registered under historical studies has standing entry by the week and may have matter brought down; **"Rune Path. Bronze."**; one clerk keeps the provision's book, which has had one name in it; the form has a line for the matter, a line for its bearer (one), and **a line for hands, entered by count**. Protected: Karis "Four hands." / Seln "Four… By count." / Lira "Four hands," … "I've two." / Cael wrote: *Entered by count. That's the door.* The S8 close verbatim (Cael; Karis), with OWNER-pending #53 ("four years and a hearing") as Cael's rough spoken figure, never repeated.
   401	- **Cleon at noon (the 283rd)**, at the cook-house against the second wall: he looked at one sheet from its foot upward. Protected: "Off floors," (Cleon) / "Fused. Grey. No join. Three of them. I copied every mark on all three. Fifty-two sheets," (Karis) / "Fifty-two sheets," (Cleon). His terms: not on a counter; all the sheets, in their places; they come down to him; no names (his is on the card and is not said aloud); **not a word of what she thinks they are until he has looked**; one lamp goes down with them, his, held where he says; no other carried light and no fire of anybody's. He gave a buff-edged card for the 284th at the second bell. He never looked at Cael. He stopped a step late on the paving. **His Path has not been shown.**
   402	- **The 284th.** Seln is NOT on the count (a clerk might know his face; somebody must watch the door). **Vastin said "Yes" before a mouth was open** and is the fourth hand. Karis is the card's unnamed bearer; **Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin are the four hands**. Both upper keepers counted ("One, and four") and tore a corner; the way to the yard goes by the load lane under the third wall and a cut of **twenty-two steps in five flights, a stride and a half wide, walled three men high with no door or window** — never across the square. **Cael did not set foot on the square or its steps and did not look at the crown; Lira kept the same discipline unasked** and walked between him and each alley's end. **Lira's ladder did not pull** ("It's a hill with a tax on it"); protected: "Four hands," (Lira) / "Four." (Cael) / "Right." (Lira); the S9 log verbatim. Her registry paper stayed in her coat. **The yard clerk (unnamed) asked nothing and wrote *four*.** None of the four restricted names has been written; the asset-restriction is still pending.
   403	- **The iron door** is low, of plates laid like slates, with no lock or handle on the outside; the clerk knocks; Cleon opened it from within, counted loads, not people, and shut it behind them. Inside: Cael, Brom, Lira, Vastin and Karis with three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide; the clerk and Seln outside. "The tiers closed, and the dark opened."
   404	- **Oryn (cutaways):** at her first gate north (unnamed) she re-broke and set, with his leave, the wrist of a carter of Oxhollow that had knit wrong over the cold term (spent to greyness; a day lost); at her second (unnamed) she turned a child lying across and a daughter was born alive at first light on the 284th. No patient died. She has heard of no leaf. She still owes Cael the reading and is a day behind her round.
   405	- **Names:** none minted (the clerk, keepers, porters, innkeeper and both holds unnamed). **Length:** about 35,200 against 34,000, accepted.
