   604	
   605	- **The carts' street and the beam.** (r1: in the yard Seln names the risk before the ruling — "a bar at the foot of it, and a beam, and a man who writes" — and Cael answers that he would "sooner be weighed in front of fifty carters than shut in where nobody's looking"; the card is spelled out as one bearer, Karis, and a figure of four meaning Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin by count, with Seln on no card.) From the lane's end a broader way opens to the left between the last house and the top wall: a shelf cut into the bluff on its outer side, paved with grooved setts, wide enough for two drays; up-traffic keeps the wall, down-traffic the kerb; it curls in under an archway to **a banded pole let down between two posts**, with the weigh-beam beside it (a pan as broad as a cartwheel on three chains; a man in a leather cap with a ruled slate, eleven years at that beam). His rule: "What goes by the slot's the hatch's. What comes on wheels is mine." **The honest way:** Karis lays the card on his ledge and says that it is matter for the lower archive on its practitioner's card; he reads the card's head with his lips and chalks four weights against that name. No name is spoken; none of the four restricted names is written. He will not weigh a man ("There's no column"). Vastin had given Cael the rule beforehand in the yard ("a load that travels on a practitioner's card is entered to the head of the card… it has an article, which I do not give you") and says one sentence at the beam and no article. Karis tells Cleon that his name is on a slate ("It's been on worse").
   606	- **The handcart.** The stable's own, two wheels and a pair of shafts, lent by the woman at the half-door while the loft is theirs, "if it comes back swept". Brom goes between the shafts (leaning up; walking backward as the brake down). Vastin rides on the boxes, facing backward, and is got off the wheels at the dock's corner.
   607	- **Vastin from the 291st.** Let down the loft ladder on a box-rope, with a turn round the ladder-post under Brom's weight; he does not go up it again and sleeps in the end stall of the stable, on straw, at no charge. He goes below because the card written the evening before says *four*. He comes down the inner stair on his seat, Cael taking his heel at each step, and goes up it backward on his hands. On the 293rd he takes the whole stair alone, a quarter of an hour early, and is three steps from the iron at the bell. He uses the road's hand-signs (*seen*; *nothing?*) from his straw.
   608	- **The three things the clerk has not asked** (the packet gives the fact; the three are mine): the name, rank and office laid on his ledge on the 285th; the same matter arriving by the bottom gate on wheels with another man's chalk against it; a bearer's hand and a hand's leg changed overnight, with a tread out of its bed and two bolts found in his cut. He writes *The same* and *four*.
   609	- **What Cleon learns of the 290th:** that Karis cut her hand on a stair ("Which lays?" / "The other."), and no more.
   610	- **Cleon's speech at the board (r1; the three plan pieces fixed, the rest mine).** Of the tally-book: "A load came in at a gate. It was paid on, and when the day was done a clerk put it down. He might have begun at the head of his column or the foot of it; it'd be the same tally. There's not a mark on that leaf that's waiting for anything." Of the Stair's sheet: "Whoever cut this set each sign where it is for the sake of the one after it. Take them out of their order and you've a pile of chippings. Leave them in it, and you're handed on, sign to sign, whether you can read a letter of it or not. I don't read it. I'm walked down it." The word "bearer" is not used of signs. **"There" is Cleon's**, said as his finger stops above the last sign of the fifth row. **The acquisition as re-ordered:** Cael first sees the board's four rows go aslant at the tail of his eye and takes it for the lamp; checks the flame; then looks at the fifth row (*Goes*); then knows it and whose it is. The comparison with how the earlier fragments came is gone from this scene.
   611	- **Rune from inside, as Cael has it.** The twelfth does not ask; it leans, and gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*). A made thing is looked at from where it stands on the ground toward where it is going, which is why the light belongs low and to the left. A run's lean "comes down flat" where it ends; Cleon can therefore mark where each run stops (**"red"** squares of tile between signs), which gives Karis the lengths of the words.
   612	- **The decipherment, 291st–293rd.** Fifteen and sixteen on the 291st from a five-sign word and a second (the sixteenth is the old word for twenty, a score); seventeen at the bell. Eighteen to twenty-one on the 292nd. **Her first word that is not a number is *and*.** **The six-sign word that she had covered is *set*** ("as a stone is set, or a stake"); she had wished for a name. The twenty-second and twenty-third come from the leaf itself on the 293rd ("It spells them for me"). **(r1) Before she says "I can read it" she makes three unlike checks, not three passes:** one of the two new signs laid against a word in the last tall tally-book; a question to Cleon ("The fourth line… Does it sit, or does it go?" / "Sits. The lot of it sits. There's not an order on that skin from the head to the tail… Somebody's telling you what was done. He's not telling you to do it."); and a word near the leaf's foot covered with her glove, written out beforehand from the letters' use at the head, and then uncovered. She shows Vastin's copied page to Cleon only after the reading.
   613	- **The refusal to turn it inward (r1).** One present choice, near the bell, after he has seen that he must tell Cleon: Oryn's order (*Write it down and stop touching it.*), which he "had got round… once already, at a waypost" and does not get round now. Cleon's limit is not recited beside it; it stands in the notice (*Not a man.*).
   614	- **The telling (35.3).** Cael's own account of why Cleon may be told and Vastin still may not: the taking from Cleon would go on every hour at his elbow, and his own term was that he be told what a thing is once he has looked; the eleventh sits unused and cannot be told without the whole. Brom: "Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours." Seln, that night: "I should have said yes… I should have been a good deal longer about it."
   615	- **The cup of water.** Cleon takes down the cup and the jar, pours, and sets the cup on the bare corner of the third table an inch from Cael's hand, without a word. Cael drinks half. **(r1) The warning's joins:** he speaks to the oak from the table's corner; squares the reed with one finger between the first and second pieces; and at "He stopped" the finger comes off the reed.
   616	- **How Cleon looks away.** At the board his eyes come as far as Cael's sleeve and wrist and he turns his whole head to the oak; on the 292nd they stop at the edge of the lamp's brass ("There's tar on my lamp"); on the stair he passes with his face to the wall; at the fire he keeps them off the boy for an hour.
   617	- **The tail.** Out by the stable's muck-door and a barrow-wide lane. Seln's three rules (look where he will be in twenty strides; never be the nearest thing that moves; when he stops, you do not). The watch: two men in leather caps, a handcart, a lantern on a pole (one is telling the other about a goose). The hood drinks at the trough's pipe; walks the brokers' street; **stands eleven minutes inside the rope-walk** (Cael counts seven hundred between two tar barrels); walks the quay past six boats and the ferry's cable; goes a quarter of the ring road, where the watch nods to him; warms his hands at the keepers' brazier; shows something low in his palm; is let through **a small door in the foot of the grating**, and turns in it for a breath. The blue gate at night: the grating down, a brazier, two keepers (a pike; a board on a cord). A keeper to Cael: "First bell… And you'll want your card." The groove is "a hand wide". Seln's finding: "It was worth one night to learn that it is not worth two"; the man was showing how much time they have. No face; no name; nothing located beyond "he may go through the blue gate by night".
   618	- **The parcel.** The carrier with his hat cut away on one side lies the night of the 292nd at the inn on his way to the western landing with the bag; he knows Brom's back from that landing and breaks the bag "for a thumb that the old man knows" (Brom's thumb, in hub-grease, on his way-slip; a copper of Cael's own). The dividers: two brass legs a hand long, flat on the inner side, points of blued steel in a round of cork, a broad flat rivet, a turned stalk. Karis asks for the loan of them "when I have a hide in front of me again". Cael does not open them on any distance (his ruling of the 168th).
   619	- **The meal.** Brom's ruling ("A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on"); four marks at the half-door; the lower room; a goose, a pie, a white loaf, baked apples, cider; seven cups poured, two kept under saucers (Karis, above with her letters; Oryn). **Purse 130.** **(r1) The cups line is Cael's:** Vastin says of the two absent women, "I have kept the count at a great many boards. I should have struck both"; Cael answers with the protected line, across the table; Vastin: "I see that it is… I was never shown the book." Brom fills his cup. (Vastin's remark over the dividers and Brom's "That's so" are cut.)
   620	- **Seln's slip (r1: entered late).** Cael smells paper burning; a curl of black lies in the candle's dish under Seln's fingers, with one white corner and a line and a half of close writing on it before the flame has that too; "Closed." Lira: "What is?" / Seln: "It is in the dish." Cael did not see it come out of the sleeve or be read. **What was on it is not given.**
   621	- **The light.** A thread of the crown's light has come in each night by a gap at the left of the blanket and lain on the loft's boards like a chalk line. On the night of the 292nd it thickens and thins for a count of one. Cael feels "the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him" (r1: the comparison with the eleven seconds in a chalk ring is cut by the coordinator's fix 37; nothing likens the light to a plate, a ring or a thing that attends) and answers himself ("A needle swings to iron. Nobody says that the needle has seen the iron"; a draught; a lamp-trimmer; his own tired eyes). He does not cross three strides of boards and does not look again. Lira crosses, without lifting the corner, and draws the blanket to the frame.
   622	- **Oryn's fourth gate** (unnamed; r1: her scene now opens "On the evening of that same day, the two hundred and ninety-second, and four gates north of a fork-stone"; nine on the bench, the third of them a grandfather): a larger hold, stone to a man's head with split oak above. The hold's old woman, who keeps its days, rings the fourteen-year-olds on a threshing floor of beaten clay "at the first light after the moon's full", with a hazel stick. No office, plate or clerk. The girl (unnamed; a plait; a sheepskin too big) steps in; her eyes go to a place a hand's breadth from her cheek; she listens; her knees give a little; she tells her grandmother in her ear. Oryn sits forty yards off with her hands in her sleeves and lays a hand on nobody. "Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without." She has watched forty such. **Her decision:** the fifth gate has its day, the ford an hour, and she will not come late to the mill; and the reading she owes will go "as deep as she went", asked first. She is two days behind.
   623	- **The leaf as a thing.** In a bundle between two thin boards of beech. A single leaf of skin the colour of old horn, stiff, curled at one corner, the lamp showing through it; written with a point and rubbed dark; twenty-six lines on its first face, in the floors' letters; a list on its back, not gone into. A little below the middle a place four or five letters wide scraped pale and thin, with one upright stroke left at its left side. Cleon: "One hand. One sitting. He ruled it first… a fair copy… scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom." Its head gives a year in the count that the old town's books keep. **Beneath the lower board: a dozen or more leaves of paper, a year-book of the town in its old ink hand** (floods, a murrain, who was reeve), which Karis reads at sight and puts later than the skin ("It speaks of the walls"). The chronicle's line stands on its first leaf beside a worm-hole or damp-hole a thumb wide.
   624	- **The reading's joins (r1).** In the first half Karis's breath gives out "upon a comma" after "the rivers ran wrong" and she takes another without lifting her finger; nothing of the record is missing there. At the scraped place her finger stops above the one stroke and she says, in her own voice: "It is scraped. A word as wide as four of his letters, or five. I have the first stroke of it, and nothing after." After *The Architect* (said once, heard by Cael for the first time) the pencil's butt goes from the head of the skin to the pale place, and she says the title clause. **"Older. Made. Us." is Karis's, said aloud**, shutting three fingers into her palm "as a big man had counted findings at a spring". Vastin's reach is the restored line from the box.
   625	- **Who heard it.** Karis, Cleon, Cael, Vastin. Lira and Brom were in the eastern gallery with the carried lamp, putting twelve tally-books back (Cleon would not have the tape touched while a book was out of its bay). Karis reads it once, asks that nobody say it back ("A thing that is said twice begins to be said differently"), and makes two copies; the fair one goes into Cael's coat.
   626	- **The page the right way up (the §13 lock).** At the head of the inner stair on the evening of the 293rd, by the lamp in Lira's hand, Cael sees the first line of Karis's copy lying the right way up: "There was no desk on the other side of this."
   627	- **Cleon's day over.** At the bell of the 293rd: "They lie tonight… I said a week. I'll be a day over it. There's the back of that skin to do." (To meet the next packet's 294th.)
   628	- **The street (r1).** Both protected lines are Seln's. Cael, asked not to say the leaf over, gives two sentences "as a carter gives a load": "It's one man's work. All of it; the tiers, and the rest. He says so himself." / "Made," / "And he says who it was built against. It wasn't a house. It was a sort of people." / "For a kind." Seln says nothing for ten paces, with his left hand on the strap; then, "I have had two sentences, which is as much as I trust from any mouth."
   629	- **The fire, as re-joined (r1).** Lira reads once; Cael turns his face away and watches eight bowls cool; she folds the page and says her line **to the fire**, before anything else; then the hole and the ruled box ("There's a stroke in the hole." / "Yes."); then she puts her thumb on the place through the paper and looks across at him. What her line does to him is told while Brom reads. **Seln (only "I'll keep it a while longer." fixed):** "There is a house on that hill which would give a great deal to have it laid upon its ledge; and I have asked myself on a good many nights what it was that I kept it out of… I had only ever seen that house from the inside of its own books. It stands upon this. I know now what the office is for, which I did not know at noon; and I had rather know it and carry the thing, than carry it blind." No count of years; the case on his chest, shut; nothing in it named. **Cael sits** on the fifth bucket when Seln hands the page back, so that Vastin's "Who's sitting across the fire" is so, and **gets up off it** for pole two. **Vastin's joins:** "I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." He lifts his eyes from the fire only for the last piece. Before "I would have.": "He could have left it there, and it would have been true." **Pole two's joins:** "He had breath left for three words." / "It was Lira, and she had not taken her eyes off him since he got up." / "He stopped there, since the rest of it was not a plan."
   630	- **The yard fire.** The stable's iron fire-basket on three legs, by the stall door; Brom's flint; upturned oak buckets; eight bowls ("I'd laid one for a mule before now"). Cleon comes in by the yard, having asked a keeper which street; he sits with his back half to the flame and eats half a bowl. Asked by Lira whether it is true: "I read how a thing's made… it's as old as it says it is… Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say." His undertaking, to which Brom says "Good.": "That skin stays where it's lain… they'll not learn it from me." Brom's price: whatever was built was built over the whole country and down to every child; "Nobody spends so on a thing that he thinks is small… A price isn't a verdict… I'd want to hear the other side." After Lira's "You would have." she empties the old man's cold bowl into the pot and fills it hot. Cael does not write what he said.
   631	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the beam-man; the two watchmen; the keepers of the blue gate by night; the carrier with the cut hat (from Book 7); the hold's old woman, the girl in her year and her small brother; a girl in a red cap and a boy who laughed (Vastin's memory of kindlings he signed).
   632	
   633	### State at movement end (author; governed by the rulings above and by the surviving prose)
   634	*(Refreshed after repair r1, 2026-10-12. No fact of the calendar, bodies, money, fragments or deployment changed in repair. The sixteen markers are gone; the speakers of three restored units are as the coordinator ruled.)*
   635	
   636	**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and ninety-third day past the Line**, in the long loft at Treswick. Days on the page: 291 (the ladder; the carts' street; the beam; the hatch; the gallery; the inside; the twelfth; the telling; the stair), the night of 291–292 (the tail), 292 (below; the parcel; the meal; the inventory; the light), Oryn at her fourth gate from dusk on the 292nd to first light on the 293rd, 293 (the leaf; the chronicle; the copy; Vastin's afternoon and stair; the street; the yard fire). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   637	
   638	**Where everyone is.** Five in the loft (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln at the ladder's head) and Vastin in the end stall beneath. Cleon has gone back up through the blue gate; the leaf, the chronicle and the board are under the registry; the fifty-two sheets lie on their tables one night more. Oryn and the brown mule are between her fourth gate and her fifth. Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
   639	
   640	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Twelve fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** The twelfth is acquired, read, told to Cleon, and held shut; three unmeant glances, not counted; never on a person, the crown, the leaf or himself. He keeps Cleon's warning and has told nobody. He has ruled that the twelfth is not turned inward until Oryn has been asked. He has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; one unsteady moment was seen on the loft's boards and entered fourth on the page of the inventory, ruling *hold*. He has stood at the blue gate's groove by night and gone back down. He has heard the leaf, carried its copy, and said pole two aloud on his feet at the fire; it is not in the log. **Vastin is still not told of the eleventh**; the debt is entered as heavier. He owes Oryn two tellings at the mill (the readings round the place; the twelfth). Body: sound; tar on his palms; eyes recovered.
   641	
   642	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. Arm: day twenty-eight out of the wool on the 293rd; it steadied a page and drew a blanket along two nails. Right shoulder still bruised. She was told first and read the page first. She has twice been refused something Cael had no leave to give (the warning; the leaf before the fire) and has said so. She closed the gap by the blanket without looking. Her registry paper is untouched.
   643	
   644	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. Neither arm above the elbow; the box on the bound right shoulder; he was the handcart's horse and brake. He put his thumb to a carrier's slip. **The purse: a hundred and thirty.** He means to buy the rope before the gate. Owed at the mill to two healers.
   645	
   646	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork (she did not light the yard fire). **All twenty-three letters.** She has read the first face of the leaf aloud once, given one account of it, shown Vastin's copied page to Cleon, marked one line of the chronicle and not read on, and made two copies with the gap ruled as an empty box and the one stroke drawn. She has kept two pages in the chamber and at the fire. She has not gone into the leaf's back (a list). Left palm: stitches in, under linen; she has begun to use the hand without noticing.
   647	
   648	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He taught a second person (Cael: three rules of following). He has read the page. The case is on him, **shut**; at the fire he said that a house on the hill would give a great deal to have it laid on its ledge, that he knows now what the office is for, and "I'll keep it a while longer." He was told on the street in two sentences and answered "Made." and "For a kind." He burned a slip and said "Closed." He will not follow the boots again. He expects them on the road east.
   649	
   650	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**; slat-bound; he rides the cart, sleeps in a stall, and takes the inner stair on his seat. He kept silent at the beam after one sentence. He heard the leaf; reached once aloud and was stopped; has stopped the same sentence in his own head; has said his piece at the fire (imperfect; the fault meant, which he called corrupt; aimed) and answered Lira ("I would have."). He was told across a dinner table that out here one counts who is owed a seat. He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the sum, or what is under the bar. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it.
   651	
   652	**Cleon.** Rune, Bronze. He has shown the inside of the Path to Karis; has been told by Cael how the twelfth came; refused to finish a sentence; gave a cup of water and a warning; looks away from the boy on purpose and has kept it for two days and an hour at a fire. He has heard the leaf that lay on his shelf ten years. He has been above ground with the crew once. He has said that the leaf stays under his hand and goes to no counter. Tomorrow: the list of parts.
   653	
   654	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. Four gates done of five; two days behind. She watched a Kindling from a bench and laid hands on nobody. Nobody has died under her hands. She has heard of no leaf. She has decided not to come late to the mill and to read Cael as deep as she goes, asked first.
   655	
   656	**The clerk.** Unnamed. *The same* and *four* on ten days. Three things not asked.
   657	
   658	**The faction.** One watcher (the cut boots), walked for three hours on the night of the 291st and seen through the blue gate; not on the kerb on the 293rd. Faceless, unnamed, unlocated. It has been shown a porter twice.
   659	
   660	**Objects and money.** **130 marks** (134 less four for the dinner). The reserve sealed in the third box. Hesk's dividers are shut in Hesk's book; his note with them. The fair copy of the leaf is in Cael's hand; the other in Karis's notebook. The stable's handcart is theirs while the loft is (to the 295th). Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
   661	
   662	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; **Cleon knows how one fragment came**; not Vastin or the clerk. The twelfth: the six (Oryn not yet) and Cleon. The warning: Cael and Cleon. The leaf: Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin by ear; Lira, Brom and Seln from the page; not Oryn, Teague or Pike. The unsteady light: Cael and Lira (from the log). The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged.
   663	
   664	**Open threads.** The back of a leaf. A line with a hole in it, under a slip. A second page with nothing on it. A warning that is in no book. A debt to a man in a stall. Two tellings owed at a mill. A light that was not steady once. Boots that may be on the road east. A case kept a while longer. A rope to buy before the gate.
   665	
   666	### Next-movement inheritance (Sol plan audit §8; also written into the next packet)
   667	Movement 7 already carries the 294th, all-day list, twelve fragments, Karis's bound hand, Vastin day fifteen and no mule (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:28–30`). Its author must additionally inherit:
   668	
   669	- **Leaf audience and wording discipline.** Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin heard the record once; Lira, Brom and Seln read Karis's page; Oryn has not heard it (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:3–29,95–105,251–269`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:3–37,53–103`). Nobody who heard it quotes it. The surviving public reductions are *made*, *for a kind*, Lira's existence conclusion, Vastin's *aimed/category/person*, and Cael's built-to-prevent conclusion (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:179–189`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:37,135,187` after fixes 10, 12 and 14).
   670	- **Fragments and knowledge.** Twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two. The eleventh remains unused and known to Lira through the log but not Vastin. The twelfth is known to Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and Cleon; Oryn and Vastin have not been told. Cleon knows how this one fragment came but no other acquisition history. The warning is known only to Cael and Cleon and is in no book (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:197–239,279–339`; `manuscript/chapter-36.md:15–63`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:219–227`). **Coordinator ruling (overrides the audit here):** the twelfth leans at made things UNBIDDEN and OUTWARD in Movement 6 (the board, the rope-walk, the bridge, the dividers, the inn table), and Cael is learning not to look; a few unmeant outward glances are on the page and are not counted as deployments. It has never been turned INWARD, and he refused that turn once, deliberately, until Oryn has been asked. Movement 7's beat is the first INWARD turn, and it comes unbidden.
   671	- **Cleon.** He has shown the Path's inside, been told the twelfth's mechanism, declined a dangerous sentence, given the warning, deliberately looked away for two days, promised the leaf stays under his hand and above no counter, and said “Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts.” (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:15–239`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:115–125,227–239`). He let the fifty-two sheets lie one extra night and has written the 294th card (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–125`).
   672	- **Object positions at night on the 293rd.** The original skin leaf is between its beech boards under Cleon's jar at his desk; the paper chronicle remains under the registry with its hole slipped/marked; the shelf bundle is untied and the leaf's back/list is unread (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:31–43,163–193`; `manuscript/chapter-39.md:137`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:289`). Karis's fair copy is in Cael's hand/coat after Seln returns it; Karis retains the second copy in her notebook (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:123–127`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249–251`). The fifty-two sheets lie on their three tables one night more, and the black-oak plank remains against the third table (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–121`; inherited location `STATE_LEDGER.md:552`). The three boxes, flat and hide are back in the loft; the bearing is on the calfskin in the second box, the reserve stays sealed in the third, and Cael sits against the third (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`). Vastin is in the end stall; the tarred kit case is last explicit in his hands on the returning cart, and the prose does not state whether it finishes in the stall or loft (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:173–205`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–265`). M7 must not silently choose a different custody if the case matters.
   673	- **Bodies.** Vastin's right knee is day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd, still slat-bound, with stick/no kneeling/no running/no Wall; he sleeps in the end stall and can take only the inner stair on his seat/hands (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:53–113`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–263`). Karis's left palm remains stitched and bound; she writes right-handed but has begun to steady/use the left without noticing (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:227–247`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249`). Brom still cannot raise either arm above the elbow; Lira is day twenty-eight out of wool on the 293rd (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:71–79`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:281–285`).
   674	- **Money, lodging and tools.** Purse 130; reserve sealed in the third box; loft/handcart through the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed. Hesk's dividers and note are shut in Hesk's book with Cael (`manuscript/chapter-37.md:95–99,141–143,267–273`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`).
   675	- **Faction and route.** Last sighting: the altered boots pass the blue gate on the night of the 291st; no face, name or base learned, and Seln will not follow again. No watcher is on the kerb/doorways on the 293rd; Seln expects the road east watched (`manuscript/chapter-36.md:285–379`; `manuscript/chapter-39.md:191–221`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:219–223`). Cael still will not cross the square/steps or take the dusk cut.
   676	- **Seln and the case.** The case remains on him and shut; its contents remain unnamed. He has read the leaf copy and decided the case is worth keeping “a while longer” (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:91–105` after fix 11). His cache decision is not a declaration or disclosure.
