   139	## Changes made while drafting
   140	
   141	(Written after the draft.)
   142	
   143	- **Decision 5, the list.** Karis says on the 294th that the first run of the fourth line is "the people at a kindling, as the face has them" (41:135). That much of the line's content is on the page four days before the grade; the second run stays three signs of six until the 298th.
   144	- **Decision 12.** The factor's offer has two parts: twenty at each first frost to the five, and a seat at the long house's fire for Vastin for the whole of any cold term, "within a shout of my road". Vastin does not answer it; Brom declines for the purse.
   145	- **Decision 14.** Vastin lies the night of the 297th on the keeper's settle (no stair). The hearth's coppers are not shown.
   146	- **Decision 17.** Teague's heel goes against a root of the great ash, not a trough's kerb. Brom names the Force and recalls one seen at a quarry.
   147	- **Decision 18.** Karis is not in the meadow (she is at the table from first light); eight walk the file before Teague, and her place is told to Brom for her.
   148	- **Decision 19.** The office's point of the compass is never said aloud: Karis lays it against the hide and reports only that it does not differ from the carter's line by the width of the brass. The hide's scale is taken in days (the hall to the iron: three), and the office's figure in lengths is turned into days by her knowledge of the cord. No figure in lengths is printed.
   149	- **New, 45.4.** Vastin rides to the fork above the mill on the salt cart that Teague put out of the mud: "It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one." It is the first cost of the field's ruling, and he takes it without a clause (the first order kept without walking it back is still Oryn's "Stand there." that evening). He gets down at the notch's head and walks down, so that eleven come off the ridge.
   150	- **Decision 23.** Oryn's answer to the two tellings is given in my words and ends "But not on my plank." The protected "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." stays inside the marker of 46.4.
   151	- **Decision 24.** Oryn says, late in the reading, that "something turned over … out at the edge … one of yours, in its sleep", and dismisses it. Lira, on the bench, saw him go stiff, and is told in the dark only that something came, unasked, and that he has no name for it that he would put his hand to.
   152	- **Decision 10.** At the western landing the horse is seen tied in the alders above the last bend, with no rider on it.
   153	- **Rhythm.** Chapters 41, 44, 45 and 46 first measured under 13.3 on the sentence mean (13.1, 12.6, 12.2 and 10.9); chapter 42 first measured over (14.7, with 5.9% long). Each was brought into range by reading: clipped runs that were one thought were joined, by individually written replacements applied as exact strings; chapter 46 was rewritten whole. No rule or script split or joined sentences.
   154	- **Overlap.** The tool's first run listed seven unprotected runs of eight words. Five were mine and were re-composed (41: the forearm; 43: the noon; 44 and 46: the first light, twice; 46: the quarter-hour). One was my own wording after a plan-quoted fragment inside a marker and was re-worded. One stands: the plan's own line for Lira's arm, quoted inside its marker (45:329).

