    78	## New canon minted
    79	
    80	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
    81	
    82	- **The back of the leaf (41:29–49; 41:55–59; 41:113–135).** Paler than the face, with no dark stuff rubbed in; nine short lines in a column down the left side, ruled first; each a noun and a number written out in words. The first four begin with the four things that the face names, in the face's order; the other five are nouns that Karis can spell and cannot turn. No number is said aloud. The fourth line (the mark's) crosses the thin place behind the scraping: one bearer (the face's word for the mark), two runs hung level from it (five signs; six signs), and the number *three*. On the 294th the first run is "the people at a kindling"; of the second, three signs of six are sure. Nobody asks whether the back sits or goes; Cleon says only that it is a list, that the two runs hang level, and that it ends ("There's no tenth that's been cut away").
    83	- **Cleon turns the skin** between two boards, a third brought for it, without touching it (41:27–31). **Cleon's copy:** a square of his card-stock, drawn sign for sign and not read, with a red tick under each place where a run ends; he strikes his own drawing of the fourth line through and has Karis put hers in (41:145–151); he gives it to her at the iron, rolled, with a turn of the bundle's brown tape (41:187). He takes his own lamp back from Cael's fingers (41:169–173).
    84	- **The fifty-two sheets go up** counted into three boxes by a tally kept by Vastin at Karis's asking, and signed K. and V. (41:83–99; 41:163). **The clerk's last entry:** *Withdrawn by its bearer*, and *four*, with a line ruled beneath across both columns (41:221).
    85	- **The rope (42:13–23).** The same coil that Brom looked at on the 282nd, kept by for him; **two marks**. **The rope-walk's dray (42:31–63):** four wheels, two brown cobs, forty coils of four-strand for the western landing's broker, who sent for it a fortnight since because "a big man from over the water" called his rope the wrong kind; "the wrong kind of excellent" is a saying on that landing. The carter: a lean man with a straw in his mouth, unnamed. The coil rides free; Vastin rides the tail-board at a copper a day of his own, and gets down for every hill.
    86	- **The outer gate going out (42:65–77):** the keeper moves two beads for the boots on the tail-board when Lira holds him to his own count: "Six out. Six in, the day I had you."
    87	- **The bridge (42:79–119).** The twelfth is refused by counting the piers; Karis lays her bound hand on the parapet and draws nothing; Cael finds that he is free to turn and does not, because he ruled *hold* on the light.
    88	- **The road east.** The wind has gone round to the east (42:187; 42:229; 42:245). Vastin walks the rise, turns once at its top, and lifts a hand toward a pen that is not there (42:201–205). Brom touches Oryn's bowl at the fork-stone (42:213). At the spring the slab is open to the wind and no paper is laid out; the camp is under the outcrop's western face (42:239–251). Seln stands at the basin with his back to the slab (42:261–271).
    89	- **Cael to Vastin, on the causeway (42:149–159):** Vastin says that he has noticed that he is not told, and does not ask; Cael answers only "It's six people's. That's why."
    90	- **Sheep on the high moor,** turned out since the cut was cleared (43:3–5); thirty-one carts up the cut in a fortnight (43:15).
    91	- **The factor's offer (43:19–43):** twenty at each first frost to the five who walk the cut before a wheel goes on, with the long house's fire and the pot for the week; and for Vastin a seat at that fire for the whole of any cold term, "within a shout of my road". Brom declines. **"He" is Teague,** who brought the hold's salt up behind the first carts with three of his own, asked what the factor saw, asked it again, and said that the five were for the east. He asked how long the wall stood and whether its maker lifted a foot.
    92	- **The cut on the 296th (43:47–95).** The seep runs as water; the glass and the white crack are gone. The line is forty strides below the head and three above Brom's flat stone. Vastin lifts the stick a hand's breadth off the gravel and stands on the bound leg for a breath and a half. The three wrecks have been broken up; one wheel is left (43:113–115).
    93	- **Why nothing came at the wall on the 279th (Cael's own reckoning, 43:107):** a thing comes when somebody means it, with the whole of himself, at something that can mean it back.
    94	- **The horseman (43:117–135; 43:157–165; 43:245).** A man on a bay or brown horse, in something dark, on the skyline above the cut; he keeps a mile behind the dray all the afternoon of the 296th, does not enter the cut while the six are in it, and comes through afterward at a walk. On the 297th a horse is tied in the alders above the western landing's last bend, with no rider on it. No face; no boots seen. Seln: "somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher."
    95	- **The halting-ground (43:139–187).** Vastin sits before the six; no copper is laid on a stone ("I am told that a road has no keeper."); Lira's flat hand, and he does it bent.
    96	- **The western landing and the ferry (43:199–245).** The river has fallen two feet. The broker has "forty coils, and more laying". The ferryman kneels to open the lay, drags the coil to his rope-house himself, and waives the fare: "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid." He has crossed forty times since the thaw on a hauling rope with two yarns gone.
    97	- **Lowmarch on the 297th:** more than forty fires in the meadow (43:249).
    98	- **Teague's window (44:3–97).** He has been home from the shoulder hold a week. He has never seen an Iron Wall; he knows that they belong to tribunals, and does not ask who the old man was. What he took from the factor: the wall cannot come to the trouble, and wants a count of who is behind it. Why he goes: "I mean to be at the front of the thing. I'll not dress it." The bow's objection (it pays nothing; salt work on four holds; the weir at the mill wants three stakes yet) and his answer ("I'd like to see you find the mill without me"). The older healer comes for the wrist, uncounted ("There's a valley up the north water that breaks its legs every dry spell"). The young one asks whether he is on the count: "You've two legs, and a Path, and you do what you're told a little too soon. That's a man. I can count as far as four." He nods with his whole head. Teague can read a slip, a ledger and a man's hands (44:91). Why "contract" and no more: two crews that become one bury each other's dead as kin, and a captain turns back on a ledge for a man who is not his; "He had four, and he meant to walk four home."
    99	- **Pike (44:101–157).** The twelfth line, said twice: *Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* The thirteenth is the protected line, with its price and outcome columns empty and said to be empty. **The season line is dated:** in the ruled space he writes "the day, by his own book's reckoning, and no figure after it" (no date is printed); "That's when I left off looking… Eleventh. And dated." He has had "a hundred and sixty-eight days to look". Cael reads the thirteenth line from Pike's own side of the bench (44:133–147). What is said in the room: "We read a survey… An office has measured four places beyond this river for six years" (Karis, 44:123); "A bearing" (Cael).
   100	- **The contract's four heads, in my words as Teague's (44:175–187):** the count (neither healer on it: "A healer goes where she's wanted, and I'll not have one sent"); shares by the count, "and what it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be"; the field ("On a road, with steel out, there's one voice"); the reading ("I'll take that from her as I'd take a ford from a man who'd crossed it"). Karis: "I shall want to be asked before anybody steps over a thing." Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it." Teague on the wall: "I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." No hand is offered.
   101	- **The meadow cart (44:215–257).** A salt cart sunk to its axle by the great ash; Teague's right heel against the ash's root, his right palm on the cart's bed; "He lined himself up": heel, knee, hip, shoulder, palm in one straight line, held three breaths; no light and no sound; the cart goes the length of a body and stops dead; the arm jars from palm to shoulder; the wrist clicks, heard at twenty strides; the young one's cloth is round it before the arm drops. Brom names the Path ("It comes up out of the ground, through a man, and out at the hand") and has seen one at a quarry as a child. The second cart is shifted with three iron bars and a baulk in the better part of an hour.
   102	- **The file (44:261–307).** Teague's reason: ten who have never walked together walk like two crews, "and a thing that hunts will count two". Order: Seln out front; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left hand a stride back ("he sees a thing before he knows that he's seen it, and I want to be told"); Lira; the young one behind her; the papers in the middle with whoever is healing, and a beast when there is one; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. The four hands are taught by Lira. Teague and Vastin exchange their first words ("I'm told that's where you've stood." / "It is."). Nobody asks to see the wall.
   103	- **How the grade comes off the sheets (45:3–177).**
   104	  - With Cleon's drawing and her own laid one on the other by a window, Karis has four signs of the six, and they are the six-sign word that she uncovered on the Stair's sheets on her ninth day under the town: *set* (45:13–25). The fourth line: the mark, and hung from it, level, "those who are kindled" and "what is set"; then *three* (45:29). She lays stake thirty-two, the dead man's first page and the Stair's abstract together (45:35–41).
   105	  - Three boxes ruled on a clean sheet (45:55). The middle: a stroke with a bar (the four places of the hide). The right: a stroke with two bars — the unmatched sheets of the spring, which are **one group, six sheets, one to a year**; she saw their square at the spring and said nothing of it (45:57–63). The left is ruled and empty: no sheet in six rows carries a stroke alone (45:65–71). Lira: "It's tiers. First, second, third."
   106	  - **The running number (45:77–99):** small, in the right-hand corner at each sheet's foot, after a clerk's flourish; it falls by one with every row (the Stair: 94 to 89). Vastin: "It is a series number… each sheet is numbered from the first that the office holds… I say what the form is; I do not say who drew the first." The newest row: the Fallow 61; the Stair 94; the hall 138; the place behind the iron 206. Seln: the six rows "were only as much of it as had been kept from the people who ought to have received it."
   107	  - **The two-bar place (45:101–109):** its corner has no figure, only two signs out of the index; Vastin: "A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style." Karis: older than two hundred and six, and no figure given.
   108	  - **The office's distance (45:113–151):** the two-bar sheets carry one line above their eight stations: the fourth place's four signs, a point of the compass, a measure in lengths. Neither the point nor the figure is printed. Karis turns the lengths into days by what she knows of the cord: "three such days, less a little". **The dividers** are opened for the first time on a distance, by Karis, on Cael's loan ("They're lent. I'll not open them."); set on the hall's ring and the fourth, closed by a hair, and swung along the brass, the point comes down a hand's breadth off the skin, in the Ternhall strip's shadow. "Three days past the iron." / "As we walk. A little less." (Teague; Karis.)
   109	- **The south road (45:181–231).** Vastin rides the salt cart to the fork above the mill on Teague's ruling ("It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."); the carter will not take his copper ("the captain's fare, and paid"). The well at the fork has its house and a new rope. Lira reads the log at the flat rock: "Your grandmother." / "Hesk's wife, and I never knew her."
   110	- **Millrace (45:235–291).** Three stakes still gone from the weir. Oryn is thinner. The two healers look once at each other and then at Brom's right shoulder. Teague speaks to Oryn for the first time. **A mark's worth of meal and a flitch** at the mill (45:283). Brom sets Oryn's bowl, carried seventeen days under his lashing, on the bench's end before an upturned keg; she sits.
   111	- **The readings (45:295–379).** Brom: the older healer unties her own knot; Oryn shuts the wrench in the left shoulder in a quarter of an hour (the arm goes to the height of his chin); the right is "no worse. I can't think why"; the older healer re-ties it in two yards with the turn behind the arm; Oryn: "That'll do." Lira: "It's an arm." Karis: the seven stitches drawn with a small knife, a little spent "where the thumb pulls", new linen; "Write. Don't lean." Vastin: he has stood at the post the better part of an hour ("I was told to stand there. I was not told that I might leave off."); he tells her of the wall himself; she takes the slat off, binds the joint in her own linen with two new laths, and says that the month stands as it was counted.
   112	- **The two tellings (46:3–63).** Oryn's judgment of the readings of the 281st: "You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field… And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so… It's entered." She is told the eleventh as well as the twelfth, and that Vastin does not know. Her term, in my words: "for as long as my hands are in you, nothing's turned"; "After, you're on a road, with your own eyes to spend… I don't keep people. But not on my plank."
   113	- **The deep reading (46:67–175; 46:179–205).** After the miller shuts the sluice; Lira on the bench's end. Forty minutes: four hundred and ninety breaths at twelve to the minute. She names the ten by what her hands meet, finds the eleventh ("it stands in the stream like a post, and everything parts for it") and the twelfth ("a little flat thing behind your eyes, laid by, like a rule folded on a shelf"). At the place her current parts as a river parts at a cutwater; she goes round it left, right, above and beneath ("It's not small. I'd thought that it was small."). At about the three hundredth breath the twelfth leans in: *Still.* and, a round later, *Set.* His heart strikes once; he keeps his jaw and his hands. He feels a next word and does not let it come. Oryn: "Something turned over… Out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep. Leave it. It's nothing to me." She comes out grey and counts to fifty twice.
   114	- **After (46:207–261).** Cael marks that the two findings do not say the same thing: "Hers said that there was no flow in that place for a Tide to feel. His said that something stood there which had been put." Lira saw him go stiff and is told: "Something came. I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to." / "You'll tell me when it's a thing." / "On the road." / "If there's road." The eyes are paid in the cart-shed. The four reasons (46:269–277). The warning "had begun to lean" (46:291).
   115	- **Dawn (46:295–339).** Seln: "Twelve. And a mule." The mule carries three boxes, the flat and a sack of meal. Brom lifts each box with both hands. "Twenty." / "Of thirty." Karis pins a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge with two thorns, pricks the divider's point, and rules from the fourth ring over the join and a hand's breadth past the prick, with the bound left hand: "I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me."
   116	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the woman of the half-door; the woman with the tarred hands; the rope-walk's carter; the keeper with the bead-frame; the factor of the shoulder hold (no pronoun); the horseman; the landing's broker and his clerk; the ferryman; the salt carter; the miller and the miller's wife; the woman with the net; Teague's tall man, bow, boy (the young one) and old woman.
   117	
   118	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   119	
   120	**Calendar.** First light on the **two hundred and ninety-ninth day past the Line**, at the head of the notch above Millrace. Days on the page: 294 (the Deepstacks; the hatch; the yard), 295 (the rope-walk; the outer gate; the bridge; the posts; the rise; the fork-stone; the spring), 296 (the cut's head; the cut; the cairn; the halting-ground), 297 (the western landing; the ferry; Lowmarch at dusk; Teague's window; the long room), 298 (the meadow; the back room; the south road; the fork above the mill; Millrace; the readings; the tellings; the deep reading; the cart-shed), 299 (the ford). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no date printed for Pike's season line; no count of years since the Kindling.
   121	
   122	**Where everyone is.** Twelve and a mule, in file, leaving the notch's head by the ruts that do not go back to Lowmarch: Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left shoulder a stride back; Lira; the young one; Karis, the two healers and the brown mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. Cleon is under Treswick with the leaf, the chronicle and the board. Pike is at Lowmarch.
   123	
   124	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Twelve fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He was a lamp for ten days and has entered it. He did not look back at the crown from the bridge; the promise is kept and done, and Oryn has not been told in so many words that it was kept (he told her the two things he had written that he owed). He has told Oryn: the two self-readings of the 281st and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. He has not told her, or anybody: **the two words (*still*; *set*)**; the warning; the date. He holds Oryn's finding word for word and has not written it. He has written that one thing came under her hands, unasked, and four reasons for not writing what (the fourth "not mine to write"). He has told Lira that something came and that he has no name for it. He walks at Teague's left by the field's ruling and has said aloud that the field is Teague's. He lent the dividers twice and did not open them (the ruling of the 168th stands, a hundred and thirty days old on the 298th). He has told Vastin that what is kept from him is "six people's". The eleventh's debt stands. Body: sound; the eyes paid on the night of the 298th.
   125	
   126	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **The arm is "an arm"** (day thirty-three out of the wool on the 298th; marker 13 holds the exchange). She taught the four hands to Teague's four and corrects the young one. She offered the leaf to Oryn (marker 16) and was declined. She saw Cael go stiff on the plank and has agreed to wait. She has read the 298th's noon log. Her registry paper is not mentioned.
   127	
