   159	6. **Money.** The rope at two marks (42:21–23) and the mill's meal at one (45:283) are my sums, chosen to land the calendar's 127 on the 298th; the ferry's fare is waived (43:239); the hearth at Lowmarch on the 297th is not paid for on the page. Should that night cost anything?
   160	7. **What the list says (41:57–59; 41:125–135; 45:29).** Nine lines; the first four are the face's four things; the fourth puts one word, the mark, over "those who are kindled" and "what is set", with the number three. That is the whole of the list's content on the page, and the word *set* is the one this edition uncovered in Movement 6. Is that more, or other, than the plan's "Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line"?
   161	8. **Ages "by first sheet" (45:77–109).** I have made them running series numbers at the foot of each abstract (61, 94, 138, 206), a sheet a year, with Vastin giving the form and nothing of who drew the first. It follows on the page that an office has had a yearly sheet of the fenced place for two hundred and six years, and that the six rows were only what had been held back (Seln, 45:99). Does that say "they knew" earlier than the plan means it said (the crest, the 303rd)?
   162	9. **The fifth place on the 298th (45:57–63; 45:101–109).** One group, six sheets; a stroke and two bars; no figure in its corner; "older than two hundred and six" and no more. Karis says that she saw its square at the spring and kept silent. Is that an acceptable account of Movement 3's silence, and is the withheld figure right for the 305th's "documented four hundred years back"?
   163	10. **The office's distance and the dividers (45:113–151; 46:321–335).** A run on the two-bar sheets from the fourth place: a compass point and a measure in lengths, neither printed; "three days past the iron, as we walk, a little less". Hesk's dividers are opened for the first time on a distance by Karis, on Cael's loan, at Lowmarch, and again at the ford. Movement 8's "by the office's distance" and the Span-to-court days should be checked against "three days… a little less".
   164	11. **Stake thirty-two** is taken from the kit case and laid on the table (45:35–39). It has still never been driven.
   165	12. **The horseman (43:117–135; 43:157–165; 43:245).** One distant rider on the western bank and a riderless horse at the landing; nothing identified. If no watcher is wanted before the Span's, the three passages lift out, and the second half of the 296th's "Known" (43:193) goes with them.
   166	13. **Teague's window (44:3–97).** He does not know who Vastin was and declines to ask; he reads slips and ledgers; "the boy" is the young one in his head; the young one "has a Path", unnamed. The bow speaks to Teague (not to the six). Is anything here more than Teague may know?
   167	14. **Pike (44:115; 44:153–157).** The twelfth line's wording is mine. "Dated" is rendered as a day in the ruled space by his own book's reckoning, with nothing printed. "A hundred and sixty-eight days" counts from first snow on the 129th.
   168	15. **The four heads after marker 3 (44:175–187)** are in my words, as Teague's. When the protected term is restored they may double it; they can be cut back to Brom's question and Cael's answer.
   169	16. **Force seen (44:225–255).** A root of the ash for the ground behind him; "a ton and more of oak and salt"; Brom's memory of a Force at a quarry. Cael's log of the line and its cost is in my words and keeps clear of the thirteenth's.
   170	17. **The file (44:283–291)** fixes a march order that Movement 8 inherits, with Cael at Teague's left a stride back (as that packet has him at the Span).
   171	18. **Millrace's outcomes (45:305–317; 45:349; 45:377).** Brom's left shoulder mended and both arms to his shoulders by morning; the stitches drawn on the eighth day; the slat replaced by two laths and "the month stood as it had been counted". The book's end has Karis's hand "bound" and scarring, and Vastin on day twenty-nine: both still hold.
