   272	
   273	He carried the pot back round the rock.
   274	
   275	Karis had not opened the flat. But she had the reader's roll of card out of her coat, with its turn of brown tape; and beside it, on her knee, her own notebook at the fourth line. She sat bent between the two, in what was left of the light, with her bound hand holding the one flat and the pencil going in the other; and her lips were moving.
   276	
   277	"Six," she said, under her breath, as he went by. It was not said to him. "The first. The fourth. And the last."
   278	
   279	"You said that you'd not guess."
   280	
   281	"I am not guessing. I am looking at how sure I am of three." She did not lift her head. "It is another kind of work, and it is the only kind that I am allowed tonight. Go away."
   282	
   283	He went, and sat down against the third box, and took out Hesk's book. The brass legs slid from it into his lap, shut, with the cork upon their points. He had carried them for three days and had not once opened them upon a distance. A ruling of his own stood in the way, and it was a hundred and twenty-seven days old. Whatever measured had read one way in four hands and another in his.
