    22	
    23	"What's the offer?" said Brom.
    24	
    25	"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot." The crook's foot came round, without haste, and pointed at the tail-board. "And for him, a seat at that fire for the whole of a cold term, every year that he cares to come, with nothing asked of him but to be within a shout of my road. I'll not say what for. I was on the lip."
    26	
    27	The old man had not stirred. He looked at the crook's foot as he had once looked at a milking stool, with grave attention, and it was plain that he did not mean to answer for a thing which had been put to another person's purse.
    28	
    29	Brom priced it. Cael watched him do it, with his head a little on one side and his weight on both feet, as he priced any wall.
    30	
    31	"It's a fair wage," he said. "It's a better one than the first, though it's the same twenty; you didn't know the first time that it could be done." He rubbed his jaw with the back of his wrist. "We're walking east. There's a line that we've undertaken to follow, and it goes clean away from your frost, and I can't tell you which side of a cold term it ends on. I'll not take a hold's silver against a week that I can't swear to be standing in."
    32	
    33	The factor nodded, once, like a person who has heard a figure read back correctly.
