     3	Vastin had been given three words in his life for the thing that he had served, and he set them out in their order, since he had never been able to think about a matter until it was set out.
     4	
     5	He did it on a box, by a bay of bundles, in the afternoon of the two hundred and ninety-third day. Nobody wanted him. The lamp had come back to the long table, and the boy knelt beside it, a hand to the left, as he had knelt on every other afternoon. The small pale woman was making a second copy. The man in the dusty coat sat in his chair with his eyes shut. It was a room with one office in it, and Vastin had not once that day felt his boot begin to move.
     6	
     7	The first word was *imperfect*.
     8	
     9	He had held that one for very nearly the whole of his service, and he had held it comfortably. A scale has an error. A careful officer learns the error, and allows for it, and does not throw away the scale. He had sat through eleven fields and nine thousand readings on that footing. Where a plate gave a wrong answer he had called it a wrong answer, and had written so, and had been thought rather too scrupulous by people who were not obliged to sign.
    10	
