   167	"Somebody laid them down in order," Karis said, flatly, as she said everything she was sure of, and then she said the rest, which mattered more to her than the finding, because she had been a year at the Stair's foot learning that it did. "That's what the sheets say. They say somebody graded these, a long time ago, and the grade runs oldest-last along the line, into the deep edge. That's *all* they say. They don't say who. They don't say why. They don't say what a grade is for, or what the oldest one is, or whether anything's still doing anything out there, and I'll not have anyone at this table say it for them." She looked at Vastin, because Vastin was the one at the table who would. "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means."
   168	
   169	"Noted," Vastin said, from the stick, and it was the first time Cael had heard him use the word, and he entered it.
   170	
   171	Teague, at the door, who did not read, said: "Oldest last."
   172	
   173	"Oldest last."
   174	
   175	"Then that's where we're going." He said it as a man says where the job is. "Past the fourth. On the line." He looked at Karis once, as he had looked at her map across a plank. "You'll pace it."
   176	
   177	"I'll pace it."
   178	
   179	"Good." He went out, and the tall woman in the passage behind him, who had not been in the room and had heard all of it, had the jug.
   180	
   181	Karis put her hand flat on the sheet, and the reading closed.
   182	
   183	He stayed when the others went, because the edge from Denvash was still on the map and he had been asked to bring it and had not been told to take it away. He sighted along it against the window, and there was no light under the line, as there had not been against a fire in the snow, and he folded it into the length of his palm and put it inside his coat where it went. Then he opened the log on the table where the map had been, because that was where a thing went when he had read it, and wrote.
   184	
   185	*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. Lowmarch, the back room. A hundred and twenty-seven in hand; nothing owed; nothing earned. Nineteen. Twelve. Eleventh on the wall; Teague's four at the top. Millrace tonight, by the count; Oryn there by hers.*
   186	
   187	*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came for spring like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
     1	# Chapter 18 — The Route North
     2	
     3	Millrace was a ford with a wheel on it, and he heard the wheel before he saw the roofs, as he had heard it in the autumn, and he catalogued the difference, because there was one.
     4	
     5	The Stair road went south out of Lowmarch along the river's high bank for a morning and then left the river and climbed, and from the top of the climb the country opened, and the road ran along the ridge's shoulder with a stream coming down beside it, and where the stream met a second one there was the race cut in stone and the wheel turning in it, slow, and the eleven roofs, and the ford below them shallow over gravel. The same. But there had been five and a mule coming down off the shoulder in the autumn, and there were eleven now and no mule, because the mule was hers since a fork and was in the ford-keeper's shed where she had left it, and the ford-keeper at the mill's door with flour to the elbow counted them as he had counted five, and arrived at a number he had not had to arrive at before, and said it to the healer's back.
     6	
     7	"Eleven off the ridge."
     8	
     9	"I know," Oryn said, without turning.
    10	
    11	She had a woman's hand between her palms. A hold-woman's, by the coat — the fingers gone stiff and the wrist with it, an old thing and not a wheel's work — and she did not look up at eleven people coming down onto her ford, because a healer with a hand between her palms does not look up, and Cael, at the bank's edge, a stride and a half off, which was her distance and had become his, did not expect her to, and stood, and catalogued the lean-to while he waited, because that was what the distance was for.
    12	
    13	The turf roof on poles. The plank table scrubbed white. The roll open on it in her order. The pan of water. The stool she was not using. And the line, which was six people when he counted it and five by the time he had, because she had done one while he counted, as she always had. Nothing in it had changed since the autumn except the season and the count of the crew on the bank, and he entered that, because a place that does not change is a fact, and there were going to be more of those on this road than he had been used to.
    14	
    15	She let the hand go. The fingers opened, slowly, all the way, which they had not been going to do when they came in, and the hold-woman looked at them as people looked at their own hands on Oryn's table, and Oryn said, "Work them. Every morning. In warm water, not hot. They'll go stiff again if you don't and I'll not be here to do it twice," and the woman said something Cael did not hear, and went, and Oryn drank from the pan, not much, and put it down, and looked up.
    16	
    17	She looked at the bank. She counted. He watched her do it, because he had watched her count a crew on a bank before and knew the order she took them in: Lira, whose arm she had priced at a fork; Brom, whose shoulder she had not been allowed to bind; Karis, whose hand was bound and had not been when she left; Seln; and then the man with the stick, and the stick, and the knee under the trouser, and the look went to the knee and stayed there a moment longer than it had on anything else, and then came to Cael, and stopped.
    18	
    19	"Six," she said, and he said it back.
    20	
    21	"And five that aren't." The look went past him, to the road, where Teague's four stood with the tall woman's spear grounded and the young one looking at the ford as the young look at everything, and to the fifth, the older woman with the cloth bag, who was looking at the lean-to as a woman looks at another woman's table. Oryn looked at her. The older woman looked back. It lasted as long as it took, and neither of them nodded, and neither of them looked away first, and then the older woman looked at Brom's shoulder, once, as she had looked at it in a marsh and across a room, and Oryn looked at it too, and then they had both looked at the same thing and priced it the same, and that was the whole of the exchange between the two healers on the bearing, then or after, and Cael entered it as complete.
    22	
    23	"The rest," Oryn said, to the bank, to the six of them, in the register, "can wait till I've done the line. Sit down. Not you." That was to Vastin. "You stand there and don't put the stick down, because you've been putting it down, I can see it from here, and I said a month."
    24	
    25	"Nineteen days," Vastin said.
    26	
    27	"Then it's eleven more. Stand there."
    28	
    29	He stood there. It was, Cael thought, the first order the man had taken since a cut without walking it back, and he took it from a woman at a plank table with her hands in a pan, and stood, and did not say anything about it, and Cael noted the date.
    30	
    31	---
    32	
    33	She did the line. Five, then four — the old woman's knee among them, done again as she had said at this ford in the autumn she would, the ladder order repeated and, by the woman's face, refused again — then the small ones, in the register, one hand each for the small ones and the current for none of them, because none needed it and she did not have it to spend, and Cael sat on the bank with the log and did not open it, and watched a woman do the thing she owned.
    34	
    35	Then she came to the bank.
    36	
    37	She did not sit. She stood at the edge of it with the roll on the table behind her and the ford at her back, square to the six of them as she stood square to everything, and said the terms, and he had known she would, because she had said them at a fork and at a table and by post, and a woman who says a thing three times at three sizes is a woman who has decided that it needs saying until nobody can mistake it.
    38	
    39	"I'm on my route." She said it to the six of them and past them to the road, where Teague stood, because Teague was the one on the road who had not heard it said. "Seven holds north of here owe me a visit and I've walked four of them since the fork and there are three I haven't, and there's an old woman at Thornwater with a knee that held through the winter and a ladder she'll not stop climbing. That's mine. Nobody at this ford gets a share of it." A breath. "Your bearing runs east. So does the road to the last three holds, for two days, and then it doesn't. I'll walk the two days because they're on my way. I'll walk further than that because there's a man on this bank with ribs I've been owed a reading of since a marsh, and because ten people are going into two places that stop Paths at a stride, and I've seen what that costs, and I'll not have it said I was a day north when it cost it." She did not look at Teague when she said the next thing, and it was for him. "I'm not a recruit. I've never been one. I'm a stop on my own route, and the route's mine, and the day I have to turn north I'll turn north, and nobody at this ford will ask me not to, because the one who'd have the right to has already said he won't."
    40	
    41	She looked at Cael, then.
    42	
    43	"On the road, if there's road," he said. "Not before."
    44	
    45	"Good," she said.
    46	
    47	Teague, on the road, said nothing, which was the right answer, and Cael watched him hear it and file it, because the man filed things too, in his way — a healer of no Path and a healer of Tide, and both of them on their own terms, and neither of them his to move — and the tall woman moved the spear a hand's width, and that was Teague's crew hearing it, and it was done.
    48	
    49	Lira, from the bank: "Sixth chair."
    50	
    51	"Sixth chair," Oryn said. "On my terms. Which are the only kind." And then, because the terms were said and the rest was work: "Brom. Shoulder."
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	She read them in order, on the plank table, with the light going, and asked each of them first, as she asked every time — "May I?" — and each said yes in their own register: Brom with a grunt that was a word, Lira with a nod she turned into one, Karis with "Yes" and no look up from the notebook, Vastin with "You may," which was the form's word and the only one he had; and he catalogued each one, because each was a cost he had carried in the log since the autumn and a healer's hand on it was the entry that closed the row.
    56	
    57	Brom's shoulder. She put the flat hand on it, as she had wanted to at a marsh and had been told *binding, not hands* by a woman with a cloth bag, and she held it there, and came out of it, and said, "She was right. It wanted leaving alone and it was left alone and it's a shoulder. Carry with the other one another month. You will anyway." Brom said, "Aye," which was his whole speech on the subject of his shoulder for the year.
    58	
    59	Lira's arm. Two fingers on the wrist, the flat hand on the forearm, the count: "Fifteen days at the fork. It's had thirty-three. It's an arm." She looked at Lira's two hands, both at her sides and neither in her coat. "You listened when it told you the second time."
    60	
    61	"Once," Lira said.
    62	
    63	"Once is once more than I priced." That was all of it, and Lira took it as praise, because from Oryn it was.
    64	
    65	Karis's hand. The bound one, the cut across the back of the left hand from a step's edge in a cut, eight days old, bound by Brom with thread and a strip of shirt and his teeth, and Oryn unbound it and looked at it a long time in the going light and said, "Who sewed this."
    66	
    67	"Brom."
    68	
    69	"With what."
    70	
    71	"Thread. His teeth for the knot."
    72	
    73	Oryn looked at Brom, once, and said nothing, and rebound the hand with her own linen, tighter and flatter, and said to Karis, "It'll close. It'll leave a line. Don't write with it for a week — the other hand. You've got one." And Karis, who had been writing with it for eight days and would not stop for a week, said "Noted," which from Karis was not agreement, and Oryn heard that it was not and let it go, because a healer picks the orders she enforces.
    74	
    75	Vastin's knee. He came to the table with the stick and she said, "Sit," and he sat, and she put both hands on the knee through the trouser and then said, "Trouser," and he rolled it, and the knee was a knee with a binding on it that had been put on at a cut nineteen days ago by the hands that were on it now, and she read it. It took longer than the others. He watched her face, because her face did the reading's work in the register her voice did not, and it did not change, and then it did, a little, at the corner, and she took her hands off.
    76	
    77	"It held in a cut," she said.
    78	
    79	Vastin did not say anything.
    80	
    81	"You stood on it. With the stick off the ground. You've done it once, and it was in a cut, and you did it because a cut was where it went and you wanted to stand on the place." She did not ask him. She had read it. "It held. That's not the same as the month being up. The month's the month. Eleven more days on the stick and then I'll say, and if you put it down before I say I'll know, because I'll read it, and it'll tell me."
    82	
    83	"Noted," Vastin said, and she looked at him. "That's new."
    84	
    85	"It's a useful word."
    86	
    87	"It's his." She tilted her head at the bank, at Cael, and rebound the knee, and it was done, and the man with the stick went back to standing where he had been told to stand, and stood.
    88	
    89	Then she wiped her hands, and looked at the pan, and did not drink from it, and said, to the ford, "Cael. Bench. Lie still when I say."
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	He lay still.
    94	
    95	He had lain still for nobody, in five years, except her, and for her three times, on a sack and on a table and at a marsh's edge, and each of those had been a minute at her count, and this was not going to be a minute, and he knew it before she put her hands on him, because she had said *the long one* at a fork and had meant it, and because she had carried this reading on her route since the autumn and was going to have it whole.

codex
The source resolves two important ambiguities: the four dated sites are Ring, Stair, Hall, and the fenced fourth site—not the Fallow as the draft currently says—and the source gives the protected speaker order for the cut, contract, Millrace arrivals, and knee exchange. I’m mapping every marker and deciding which departures require line repair versus an audit-only ruling.
