    96	- **The western landing and the ferry (43:199–245).** The river has fallen two feet. The broker has "forty coils, and more laying". The ferryman kneels to open the lay, drags the coil to his rope-house himself, and waives the fare: "She's a copper a head. Not today. Today she's paid." He has crossed forty times since the thaw on a hauling rope with two yarns gone.
    97	- **Lowmarch on the 297th:** more than forty fires in the meadow (43:249).
    98	- **Teague's window (44:3–97).** He has been home from the shoulder hold a week. He has never seen an Iron Wall; he knows that they belong to tribunals, and does not ask who the old man was. What he took from the factor: the wall cannot come to the trouble, and wants a count of who is behind it. Why he goes: "I mean to be at the front of the thing. I'll not dress it." The bow's objection (it pays nothing; salt work on four holds; the weir at the mill wants three stakes yet) and his answer ("I'd like to see you find the mill without me"). The older healer comes for the wrist, uncounted ("There's a valley up the north water that breaks its legs every dry spell"). The young one asks whether he is on the count: "You've two legs, and a Path, and you do what you're told a little too soon. That's a man. I can count as far as four." He nods with his whole head. Teague can read a slip, a ledger and a man's hands (44:91). Why "contract" and no more: two crews that become one bury each other's dead as kin, and a captain turns back on a ledge for a man who is not his; "He had four, and he meant to walk four home."
    99	- **Pike (44:101–157).** The twelfth line, said twice: *Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* The thirteenth is the protected line, with its price and outcome columns empty and said to be empty. **The season line is dated:** in the ruled space he writes "the day, by his own book's reckoning, and no figure after it" (no date is printed); "That's when I left off looking… Eleventh. And dated." He has had "a hundred and sixty-eight days to look". Cael reads the thirteenth line from Pike's own side of the bench (44:133–147). What is said in the room: "We read a survey… An office has measured four places beyond this river for six years" (Karis, 44:123); "A bearing" (Cael).
   100	- **The contract's four heads, in my words as Teague's (44:175–187):** the count (neither healer on it: "A healer goes where she's wanted, and I'll not have one sent"); shares by the count, "and what it costs is carried the same, so far as a cost can be"; the field ("On a road, with steel out, there's one voice"); the reading ("I'll take that from her as I'd take a ford from a man who'd crossed it"). Karis: "I shall want to be asked before anybody steps over a thing." Cael: "It's yours. I'd have asked you to take it." Teague on the wall: "I'd not ask a thing with a month on it to be shown for a count." No hand is offered.
   101	- **The meadow cart (44:215–257).** A salt cart sunk to its axle by the great ash; Teague's right heel against the ash's root, his right palm on the cart's bed; "He lined himself up": heel, knee, hip, shoulder, palm in one straight line, held three breaths; no light and no sound; the cart goes the length of a body and stops dead; the arm jars from palm to shoulder; the wrist clicks, heard at twenty strides; the young one's cloth is round it before the arm drops. Brom names the Path ("It comes up out of the ground, through a man, and out at the hand") and has seen one at a quarry as a child. The second cart is shifted with three iron bars and a baulk in the better part of an hour.
   102	- **The file (44:261–307).** Teague's reason: ten who have never walked together walk like two crews, "and a thing that hunts will count two". Order: Seln out front; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left hand a stride back ("he sees a thing before he knows that he's seen it, and I want to be told"); Lira; the young one behind her; the papers in the middle with whoever is healing, and a beast when there is one; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. The four hands are taught by Lira. Teague and Vastin exchange their first words ("I'm told that's where you've stood." / "It is."). Nobody asks to see the wall.
   103	- **How the grade comes off the sheets (45:3–177).**
   104	  - With Cleon's drawing and her own laid one on the other by a window, Karis has four signs of the six, and they are the six-sign word that she uncovered on the Stair's sheets on her ninth day under the town: *set* (45:13–25). The fourth line: the mark, and hung from it, level, "those who are kindled" and "what is set"; then *three* (45:29). She lays stake thirty-two, the dead man's first page and the Stair's abstract together (45:35–41).
   105	  - Three boxes ruled on a clean sheet (45:55). The middle: a stroke with a bar (the four places of the hide). The right: a stroke with two bars — the unmatched sheets of the spring, which are **one group, six sheets, one to a year**; she saw their square at the spring and said nothing of it (45:57–63). The left is ruled and empty: no sheet in six rows carries a stroke alone (45:65–71). Lira: "It's tiers. First, second, third."
   106	  - **The running number (45:77–99):** small, in the right-hand corner at each sheet's foot, after a clerk's flourish; it falls by one with every row (the Stair: 94 to 89). Vastin: "It is a series number… each sheet is numbered from the first that the office holds… I say what the form is; I do not say who drew the first." The newest row: the Fallow 61; the Stair 94; the hall 138; the place behind the iron 206. Seln: the six rows "were only as much of it as had been kept from the people who ought to have received it."
   107	  - **The two-bar place (45:101–109):** its corner has no figure, only two signs out of the index; Vastin: "A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style." Karis: older than two hundred and six, and no figure given.
   108	  - **The office's distance (45:113–151):** the two-bar sheets carry one line above their eight stations: the fourth place's four signs, a point of the compass, a measure in lengths. Neither the point nor the figure is printed. Karis turns the lengths into days by what she knows of the cord: "three such days, less a little". **The dividers** are opened for the first time on a distance, by Karis, on Cael's loan ("They're lent. I'll not open them."); set on the hall's ring and the fourth, closed by a hair, and swung along the brass, the point comes down a hand's breadth off the skin, in the Ternhall strip's shadow. "Three days past the iron." / "As we walk. A little less." (Teague; Karis.)
   109	- **The south road (45:181–231).** Vastin rides the salt cart to the fork above the mill on Teague's ruling ("It's owed a seat. For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."); the carter will not take his copper ("the captain's fare, and paid"). The well at the fork has its house and a new rope. Lira reads the log at the flat rock: "Your grandmother." / "Hesk's wife, and I never knew her."
   110	- **Millrace (45:235–291).** Three stakes still gone from the weir. Oryn is thinner. The two healers look once at each other and then at Brom's right shoulder. Teague speaks to Oryn for the first time. **A mark's worth of meal and a flitch** at the mill (45:283). Brom sets Oryn's bowl, carried seventeen days under his lashing, on the bench's end before an upturned keg; she sits.
   111	- **The readings (45:295–379).** Brom: the older healer unties her own knot; Oryn shuts the wrench in the left shoulder in a quarter of an hour (the arm goes to the height of his chin); the right is "no worse. I can't think why"; the older healer re-ties it in two yards with the turn behind the arm; Oryn: "That'll do." Lira: "It's an arm." Karis: the seven stitches drawn with a small knife, a little spent "where the thumb pulls", new linen; "Write. Don't lean." Vastin: he has stood at the post the better part of an hour ("I was told to stand there. I was not told that I might leave off."); he tells her of the wall himself; she takes the slat off, binds the joint in her own linen with two new laths, and says that the month stands as it was counted.
   112	- **The two tellings (46:3–63).** Oryn's judgment of the readings of the 281st: "You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field… And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so… It's entered." She is told the eleventh as well as the twelfth, and that Vastin does not know. Her term, in my words: "for as long as my hands are in you, nothing's turned"; "After, you're on a road, with your own eyes to spend… I don't keep people. But not on my plank."
   113	- **The deep reading (46:67–175; 46:179–205).** After the miller shuts the sluice; Lira on the bench's end. Forty minutes: four hundred and ninety breaths at twelve to the minute. She names the ten by what her hands meet, finds the eleventh ("it stands in the stream like a post, and everything parts for it") and the twelfth ("a little flat thing behind your eyes, laid by, like a rule folded on a shelf"). At the place her current parts as a river parts at a cutwater; she goes round it left, right, above and beneath ("It's not small. I'd thought that it was small."). At about the three hundredth breath the twelfth leans in: *Still.* and, a round later, *Set.* His heart strikes once; he keeps his jaw and his hands. He feels a next word and does not let it come. Oryn: "Something turned over… Out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep. Leave it. It's nothing to me." She comes out grey and counts to fifty twice.
   114	- **After (46:207–261).** Cael marks that the two findings do not say the same thing: "Hers said that there was no flow in that place for a Tide to feel. His said that something stood there which had been put." Lira saw him go stiff and is told: "Something came. I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to." / "You'll tell me when it's a thing." / "On the road." / "If there's road." The eyes are paid in the cart-shed. The four reasons (46:269–277). The warning "had begun to lean" (46:291).
   115	- **Dawn (46:295–339).** Seln: "Twelve. And a mule." The mule carries three boxes, the flat and a sack of meal. Brom lifts each box with both hands. "Twenty." / "Of thirty." Karis pins a clean sheet to the hide's eastern edge with two thorns, pricks the divider's point, and rules from the fourth ring over the join and a hand's breadth past the prick, with the bound left hand: "I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me."
   116	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the woman of the half-door; the woman with the tarred hands; the rope-walk's carter; the keeper with the bead-frame; the factor of the shoulder hold (no pronoun); the horseman; the landing's broker and his clerk; the ferryman; the salt carter; the miller and the miller's wife; the woman with the net; Teague's tall man, bow, boy (the young one) and old woman.
   117	
   118	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
   119	
   120	**Calendar.** First light on the **two hundred and ninety-ninth day past the Line**, at the head of the notch above Millrace. Days on the page: 294 (the Deepstacks; the hatch; the yard), 295 (the rope-walk; the outer gate; the bridge; the posts; the rise; the fork-stone; the spring), 296 (the cut's head; the cut; the cairn; the halting-ground), 297 (the western landing; the ferry; Lowmarch at dusk; Teague's window; the long room), 298 (the meadow; the back room; the south road; the fork above the mill; Millrace; the readings; the tellings; the deep reading; the cart-shed), 299 (the ford). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no date printed for Pike's season line; no count of years since the Kindling.
   121	
   122	**Where everyone is.** Twelve and a mule, in file, leaving the notch's head by the ruts that do not go back to Lowmarch: Seln ahead; the tall man; Teague, with Cael at his left shoulder a stride back; Lira; the young one; Karis, the two healers and the brown mule in the middle; Brom; Vastin a stride behind him; the bow last. Cleon is under Treswick with the leaf, the chronicle and the board. Pike is at Lowmarch.
   123	
   124	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Twelve fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He was a lamp for ten days and has entered it. He did not look back at the crown from the bridge; the promise is kept and done, and Oryn has not been told in so many words that it was kept (he told her the two things he had written that he owed). He has told Oryn: the two self-readings of the 281st and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. He has not told her, or anybody: **the two words (*still*; *set*)**; the warning; the date. He holds Oryn's finding word for word and has not written it. He has written that one thing came under her hands, unasked, and four reasons for not writing what (the fourth "not mine to write"). He has told Lira that something came and that he has no name for it. He walks at Teague's left by the field's ruling and has said aloud that the field is Teague's. He lent the dividers twice and did not open them (the ruling of the 168th stands, a hundred and thirty days old on the 298th). He has told Vastin that what is kept from him is "six people's". The eleventh's debt stands. Body: sound; the eyes paid on the night of the 298th.
   125	
   126	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **The arm is "an arm"** (day thirty-three out of the wool on the 298th; marker 13 holds the exchange). She taught the four hands to Teague's four and corrects the young one. She offered the leaf to Oryn (marker 16) and was declined. She saw Cael go stiff on the plank and has agreed to wait. She has read the 298th's noon log. Her registry paper is not mentioned.
   127	
   128	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. **Left shoulder mended by Oryn** on the 298th; **right re-tied by the older healer** in two yards; both arms come up to his shoulders on the 299th. He paid the rope debt and gave Pike the thumbed slip. **The purse: a hundred and twenty-seven.** Oryn's bowl is back with her.
   129	
   130	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck. She has copied the leaf's back (her notebook) and holds Cleon's drawn copy (a roll of card in brown tape). She has the fourth line: the mark, over those who are kindled and what is set; three. She has the grade as three marks in three ruled boxes, the first empty; the four running numbers; a fifth place of the two-bar mark, older than two hundred and six, with no figure; the office's run from the fourth place to it. She has not said the office's compass point or its figure in lengths aloud. Five lines of the list are spelled and unturned; she has said none of its numbers aloud. Left palm: stitches drawn, in Oryn's linen; she writes and draws with it; she is not to lean on it. The hide has a clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge, with a prick at the divider's point and a pencil line ruled through and a hand's breadth past it.
   131	
   132	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Out front. He has seen a horseman who behaves as a watcher would, and a riderless horse in the alders; he puts it no higher. He stood again at the spring's basin with his back to the slab. The case is on him, shut; not mentioned.
   133	
   134	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day twenty of thirty on the 299th**; Oryn's linen and two new laths; the slat is off; the month stands as counted. He stood on the leg once, with the stick up, at the line in the cut (the 296th). He rode the dray's tail-board for three days at a copper a day, and the salt cart on the 298th at Teague's ruling; he walks every hill. He kept the tally of the fifty-two sheets and signed it V. He sat before the six at the halting-ground and laid no copper. He took Oryn's "Stand there." without a clause and stood an hour. He has said "Noted." once (45:165; marker 15 may hold a second). He told Oryn of the wall of the eleventh day himself. He is on Teague's count of ten as one of "your six"; that is a count for a contract; he has not asked to join the six and nobody has spoken of it. He has been offered a seat at the shoulder hold's fire and has not answered. **He does not know** the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the two words, the date, or what is under the bar; he knows that he is not told, and that it is "six people's". He stopped the sentence about the mark twice more, without a word.
   135	
   136	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. At Millrace on the 298th, thinner; nobody has died under her hands. She has read Brom's shoulders, Lira's arm, Karis's hand and Vastin's knee, each asked first; and Cael, asked first, for forty minutes, to her own floor. **Her finding is given** (46:197). She knows of twelve, and whose the eleventh and twelfth were (the twelfth's source as "a man under the registry"); she knows what the twelfth reads and that it has not been turned inward by Cael's will; she felt "something turn over, out at the edge" and thought nothing of it. **She has not heard the leaf.** She has not been shown the grade on the page. She walks east with the mule in the middle of the file, on her own terms (marker 11), and has sat in the sixth place (marker 12).
   137	
   138	**Teague and his crew.** The term is taken as a contract: ten on the count (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln, Vastin; Teague, the tall man, the bow, the young one); shares by the count; the field his; the reading Karis's. His wrist was used once, on a cart, clicked, and is wrapped in linen under the leather. He has not said "Noted." The tall man, the bow and the older healer have said nothing to the six. **What his four have been told:** that an office's survey was read at Treswick and gave a bearing (the long room); that the old man's wall has a month on it and is not stood before the month is out (Cael); the four numbers on the hide and "three days past the iron" (Teague, at the door; he came for the distance and did not look at the marks). **Not told:** the leaf or any word of it; the list; the mechanism; any fragment; the date; who Vastin was. Nobody in his crew is named.
   139	
   140	**Pike's book and the cloth.** **Thirteen lines:** the twelfth is the clearance (*twenty; paid*); the thirteenth is *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.* with its last two columns empty. **The season line is dated** (a day in its ruled space; no figure). Eleventh on the cloth.
   141	
   142	**Cleon.** Rune, Bronze. He turned the skin, drew its back, gave Karis his copy, took his lamp back, and said his two lines at the iron. He did not look at Cael. He is not above ground.
   143	
   144	**The faction.** One horseman, unidentified, a mile behind on the 296th; a riderless horse on the western bank on the 297th. Nothing east of the river in these chapters.
   145	
   146	**Objects and money.** **130 → 128** (the 295th: sixty yards of four-strand, two marks) **→ 127** (the 298th: meal and a flitch at the mill, one mark). Coppers, which the closed text keeps apart from the purse: Vastin's three to the dray's carter, his own; the ferry's fare waived; the hearth at Lowmarch and the salt carter's seat not charged on the page. The reserve sealed in the third box. **The rope debt is paid.** The handcart went back swept. On the mule: three boxes (the fifty-two sheets in their order, with the tally), the flat, a sack of the mill's meal, Oryn's pannier and canvas. With Karis: the hide with its pinned sheet, the Ternhall brass, her notebook, Cleon's roll, the sheet of three boxes, Vastin's copied page. With Cael: Hesk's book, the dividers shut in it, the fair copy of the leaf. With Vastin: the tarred kit case (stake thirty-two was taken out on the 298th and laid on the table; its return to the case is not stated). The skin leaf, the chronicle and the black oak board are under Treswick.
   147	
   148	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; Cleon knows how one fragment came. The twelfth: the six and Cleon, **Oryn now among them**. The eleventh: Cael, Lira (the log), **and Oryn**; not Vastin. The warning: Cael and Cleon; Cael has written that a fourth reason exists and is not his to write. **The two words: Cael alone.** That something came on the plank: Cael, Lira; Oryn only as "one of yours, in its sleep". The leaf: unchanged (not Oryn, Teague or Pike). The list's fourth line and the grade: the six and Vastin; Teague in part. The date: Cael and Seln, each alone.
   149	
   150	**Open threads.** Sixteen markers. Two findings that do not agree, and one of them in nobody's book. A warning that has begun to lean. A box ruled for a mark that no sheet carries. A fifth place with no figure for its age and two signs unread in its corner. Five lines of a list spelled and not turned. A compass point and a measure that Karis has not said aloud. A line ruled past a point. A debt to a man who walks a stride behind Brom. A seat at a hold's fire, offered and not answered. A horse in the alders. A wrist in linen. Oryn's road, which runs with theirs for a day and a half.
   151	
   152	## Owner flags
   153	
   154	1. **Sixteen markers** (table above). Three carry choices that change a scene's sense and want a ruling before the joins are smoothed: 43:71 and 43:87 (who speaks at the line in the cut); 44:195 (who says that the wall is still the wall); 45:379 (who owns the four lines that close the knee's reading and the chapter). **One unprotected overlap run stands** inside marker 13 (45:329), being the packet's own line.
   155	2. **Units placed on inference, not on a pinned speaker:** 45:243 / 45:247 ("Eleven off the ridge." to the woman with the net; "I know." to Oryn, on Book 7 chapter 47's form); 45:263 ("Stand there." to Oryn); 45:343 ("Who sewed this." to Oryn). Are these as the plan has them?
   156	3. **"He" is Teague (43:35–43).** The factor says that the man first on the one-armed man's cloth brought the salt up and foretold the answer. Is that the plan's "he"? If it is Pike or another, 43:39–43 and Teague's window at 44:13–27 (where he hears of the wall from the factor) both need re-staging, and "a wall's count" loses the reason I have given it.
   157	4. **The factor's offer (43:25).** A standing wage for the five and a seat at the hold's fire for Vastin, which he does not answer. Does the seat sit too near Book 9's question of where Vastin belongs? It can be cut to the wage alone without touching the protected line.
   158	5. **How Vastin gets east (42:31–63; 45:189–193).** He rides the rope-walk's dray for three days at his own copper, and the salt cart to the fork above the mill on Teague's ruling; he walks every hill, the cut, and the notch. The boxes stay on backs (§13). The packet's "the road and the cut walked again on a bad knee" is met at the rise and the cut only. Is a ridden road acceptable?
   159	6. **Money.** The rope at two marks (42:21–23) and the mill's meal at one (45:283) are my sums, chosen to land the calendar's 127 on the 298th; the ferry's fare is waived (43:239); the hearth at Lowmarch on the 297th is not paid for on the page. Should that night cost anything?
   160	7. **What the list says (41:57–59; 41:125–135; 45:29).** Nine lines; the first four are the face's four things; the fourth puts one word, the mark, over "those who are kindled" and "what is set", with the number three. That is the whole of the list's content on the page, and the word *set* is the one this edition uncovered in Movement 6. Is that more, or other, than the plan's "Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line"?
   161	8. **Ages "by first sheet" (45:77–109).** I have made them running series numbers at the foot of each abstract (61, 94, 138, 206), a sheet a year, with Vastin giving the form and nothing of who drew the first. It follows on the page that an office has had a yearly sheet of the fenced place for two hundred and six years, and that the six rows were only what had been held back (Seln, 45:99). Does that say "they knew" earlier than the plan means it said (the crest, the 303rd)?
   162	9. **The fifth place on the 298th (45:57–63; 45:101–109).** One group, six sheets; a stroke and two bars; no figure in its corner; "older than two hundred and six" and no more. Karis says that she saw its square at the spring and kept silent. Is that an acceptable account of Movement 3's silence, and is the withheld figure right for the 305th's "documented four hundred years back"?
   163	10. **The office's distance and the dividers (45:113–151; 46:321–335).** A run on the two-bar sheets from the fourth place: a compass point and a measure in lengths, neither printed; "three days past the iron, as we walk, a little less". Hesk's dividers are opened for the first time on a distance by Karis, on Cael's loan, at Lowmarch, and again at the ford. Movement 8's "by the office's distance" and the Span-to-court days should be checked against "three days… a little less".
   164	11. **Stake thirty-two** is taken from the kit case and laid on the table (45:35–39). It has still never been driven.
   165	12. **The horseman (43:117–135; 43:157–165; 43:245).** One distant rider on the western bank and a riderless horse at the landing; nothing identified. If no watcher is wanted before the Span's, the three passages lift out, and the second half of the 296th's "Known" (43:193) goes with them.
   166	13. **Teague's window (44:3–97).** He does not know who Vastin was and declines to ask; he reads slips and ledgers; "the boy" is the young one in his head; the young one "has a Path", unnamed. The bow speaks to Teague (not to the six). Is anything here more than Teague may know?
   167	14. **Pike (44:115; 44:153–157).** The twelfth line's wording is mine. "Dated" is rendered as a day in the ruled space by his own book's reckoning, with nothing printed. "A hundred and sixty-eight days" counts from first snow on the 129th.
   168	15. **The four heads after marker 3 (44:175–187)** are in my words, as Teague's. When the protected term is restored they may double it; they can be cut back to Brom's question and Cael's answer.
   169	16. **Force seen (44:225–255).** A root of the ash for the ground behind him; "a ton and more of oak and salt"; Brom's memory of a Force at a quarry. Cael's log of the line and its cost is in my words and keeps clear of the thirteenth's.
   170	17. **The file (44:283–291)** fixes a march order that Movement 8 inherits, with Cael at Teague's left a stride back (as that packet has him at the Span).
   171	18. **Millrace's outcomes (45:305–317; 45:349; 45:377).** Brom's left shoulder mended and both arms to his shoulders by morning; the stitches drawn on the eighth day; the slat replaced by two laths and "the month stood as it had been counted". The book's end has Karis's hand "bound" and scarring, and Vastin on day twenty-nine: both still hold.
   172	19. **Oryn is told the eleventh (46:35)** as well as the twelfth, since she would have found it under her hands. She is asked nothing about Vastin's debt.
   173	20. **Oryn's answer on the inward turn (46:59–63)** forbids it only while her hands are in him, and leaves the road to him. Movement 9's log has "Cleon said don't" and no word of hers; is her leave here too wide, or too narrow?
   174	21. **Oryn half-notices (46:171–175), and Lira sees (46:247–259).** "Something turned over… one of yours, in its sleep"; and "You went stiff." The two words are told to nobody, and the page says so; but two people now know that something happened. Is that inside "told to no one" and "keeps the change out of his face"? Either beat can be cut alone.
   175	22. **The reasons are written in the book (46:267–279)** without the thing itself, and the fourth is "not mine to write". Lira reads that book. If the entry should be kept out of the book altogether (as pole two was), it can become a thing that he sets in order in his head.
   176	23. **"Twelve. And a mule." is Seln's (46:297),** by my choice; the plan gives the count and no speaker.
   177	24. **"Known"** is entered under the 295th, 296th and 297th (42:289; 43:193; 44:209). The 298th has the fixed log and no such line.
   178	25. **Cael to Vastin, "It's six people's. That's why." (42:157).** He confirms that something is withheld and whose leave it wants. Is that more than Vastin should have before the debt is paid?
   179	26. **The promise.** Cael does not tell Oryn in so many words that he kept it (he tells her what he had written that he owed). If the plan wants it said at the mill, one line in 46.1 will carry it.

