editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:They had learned the road. Nothing that had ever declared at the top of that glass had stayed there. It had gone down the hill backward, with a man hanging on its tailboard and a Path blazing in him, and it had come to rest at the foot; and three times the grey things had run to the head of the cut, and found the sound already sixty strides below them. They did not go any longer to where a declaration was made. They went to where it ended.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:11:They came down the ice as he had never seen a beast go on ice. They did not gallop. They ran low, with their legs spread and their claws out; and where the runnel bent a hand's breadth they let their hindquarters swing wide, and caught themselves, as a boy does on a slide of his own making. They were as high as calves at the shoulder. There were eight of them, strung out over ten strides of glass, and the sound was like a handful of nails drawn down a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:19:Vastin had not looked at the hounds since the first of them came over the bank. He was looking down the cut. Cael, who was two feet from him, saw exactly where. A mule's long ears stood against the light, sixty strides off, beside a small figure with something bright in its hand, and a square one with its arms bare. Then the grey head turned, and he looked up the hill at what was coming. His face did not change at all. It was the face with which he had read a case through to its last sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:29:It came out of the road. Cael felt it through his own soles, since for that one instant the ground under him had been told something and had answered. From the place where the stick stood, a line went across the cut. He saw it run. It went over the frozen ruts and split the runnel's glass from side to side, as straight as a thing ruled, and struck the foot of the north bank. All along that line the ice was white, in a crack no wider than a hair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:33:He had no other words for the thing, then or later. There was no light. Nothing was thrown, and nothing went anywhere. One moment there was a road going up a hill, and eight hounds on it. The next there was a face across the road from bank to bank, as tall as the scree. He could see it only as he might have seen a pane of clean glass, by what was on it. The frost that the pack had kicked up was drifting down the cut ahead of them. It came to that place and stopped, and lay flat on nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:41:It struck with its whole weight, shoulder first, and the sound was that of a sack of wet meal thrown against a barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:51:Vastin had not moved. That was the first cost, and Cael read it off a pair of boots. They stood on the frozen road exactly as they had been set, and they looked as though they had been let into it. The line that crossed the cut ran under both soles. Each time that a body struck the plane, forty feet off or four, something came down through that brown coat like a blow on a post. The stiff leg took it and shook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:That was the second cost, and it was arriving already. It was going out of him as heat goes out of a forge when the door is opened, steadily, with nothing kept back. Cael's own Arbiter had been dark since the day that it woke, and yet he felt it from two feet away. He had stood beside Brom when the grey came up, and beside Karis with nine points alight, and neither had been a tenth of this. Every hound in that cut could feel it from the far side of the hill. It was the loudest thing that had been done on that road in the memory of the den.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:91:The one that had gone wide came over the top of the bank's shoulder, above the place where the plane ended, and dropped onto the road inside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:101:Everything that he owned came to him then at once, and each of the ten had its reason. *Edge?* said the ninth, of the steel in his fist, and it would have gone through that grey neck like a wire through cheese. Pressure had been gathering since the first hound came over the bank. It lay under his breastbone as tight as a held breath, narrow and deep, and he had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It would go through the hound, and on, into a man's knee. *There?* asked Anchor, of the place where the plane met the scree, in its thin note. A point held there would have shut the bank like a gate, and it would have taken every other thing that he had while he held it. Storm showed him the air of the cut as a lane already half laid, from the head to the foot, and what a lane would do to six hounds; and after it he would be ten counts late at everything, in the one place where a beat mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:103:He could have used any of them. The road's rule was broken already, by a better man than himself, for a better reason than he had ever had. No hound in that cut would have turned its head for one point of Ember more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:117:She had come down the bank. He never knew how; there was a rush of plates, and she was on the road beside him with the long knife in her right hand. She did not strike at once. She put out her left hand first, the thin one, which had been out of grey wool for fourteen days. She took the hound by the loose skin behind its skull.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:121:It was done in one stroke, close in, with the whole of her quick shoulder behind it, and it was not a thing to look at. The jaws came open. The hound went down across the old man's boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:137:It was behind him, on the north side. The low bank ran down the cut there, inside the plane, with its top crusted in loose plates; and ten strides off, where the bank stood highest, there was a place on it as far from Vastin's end of the wall as the cut allowed. A straight line drawn from the south scree to that place went through the plane a little north of its middle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:145:There was a crack like a wet sheet shaken out, and the cut jumped. He had left the road and come to the bank, with the ten strides taken out of the middle. The plates slid under his boots, and held. Then the lock came down on him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:165:The ice went in the same breath. The crack that the wall had made lay across the runnel like the cut of a knife. Under a heap of that weight, the glass on the uphill side of it broke up in plates of its own, and what was beneath came up black. There was water under it. The seep had been running there all the cold term, a stride wide and a hand deep, under its own roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:185:They had been last into it, and so uppermost. They got clear of the plates on the uphill side with their coats full of grit, and they stood for a breath on the frozen ruts with their heads low. Not one of them looked at the wall. Karis had it in her fifth line. Five of their kind had been killed in that place in less time than it takes to say so, and by nothing that made a sound. The only sound in the cut was a thing that could not be bitten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:209:Vastin shut his eyes. He did not do anything that Cael could see. The frost that had been lying on nothing fell, in a thin straight line, across the road and the broken ice. There was a hill with a cut through it, and cold air. Up on the scree a plate shifted and ran a yard, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:229:Down at the foot of the cut a mule had begun to walk up the road, on the dry edge, with a small woman at her head. In front of them both, coming a good deal faster, there was a square figure with her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:15:It was the two hundred and seventy-ninth day. They were on the road in the grey before the sun, which is dusk turned round, with a ground mist lying in the hollows of the moor as high as a man's knee. The country had risen all the day before. It was heather now, and bent, and outcrops of a dark stone that broke into plates, and the ruts climbed through it toward a long shoulder of hill that stood against the west like the back of a beast lying down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:17:Seln's right fist came up at the second mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:31:He had gone to it without looking at his feet, at the flat of a hand thirty yards ahead of him. He stood in the wet bents at the road's edge with the stick's foot on turf, and he was not upright; he had let his shoulders come forward and his head hang a little, like a man who has stopped to get his breath on a hill. It was not well done. Anybody who knew him would have seen the effort in it. But there was nothing in that mist that knew him, and nothing in his two wrapped hands, which lay on the stick's head as quietly as they had lain on a trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:37:They went on, and it went with them. It kept its distance for the better part of half a mile, drifting up the slope where the ruts bent toward it and coming down where they bent away, and in all that time nobody on the road made a sound that a Path would have made. The mist thinned. A beck came down across the road in a spread of wet stones, and the mule's feet rattled in it. At the edge of those stones the grey thing stopped, as if it had come to a wall, and stood; and when Cael next turned his head there was only heather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:41:Nobody spoke for another furlong. It was Vastin who did it first, and he had waited until the road let him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:53:He said no more, and nobody told him that he had done it well. Cael saw Lira turn her head a little, on the whole of her foot, and look at a ditch that was now a furlong behind them, and face the road again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:57:The road came up under the shoulder at noon, and there it divided.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:61:"That'll be the cut, where the wheels go," said Brom. "And that's the hold. I'd sooner be told what's in a thing before I walk the mule into it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:67:"Pike's five, for the cut. I asked for five, and I see five and two over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:"I've had no salt up since the first frost. I've had no wheel up of any kind. They're stood on your landing, I don't doubt, thirty of them, saying that they'll come when it's fit." The crook pointed down over the wall, to where the spur fell away. "That's my road. It goes through the rock there by a cut that my grandfather's people made, a hundred strides and a cart's width, and it's the only way a wheel can come to this gate. Since the frost it's had three carts off me. And a carter, and two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:"They all know what a road is. They come up from the river quiet as a burying, two days, and never a finger lit; I've had Iron men walk through that gate who'd let a beast die in the shafts on the moor sooner than bring up the grey. They know. Then they come into my cut." A hand went out flat, and tilted. "There's a seep at the head of it. It comes down the middle of the road, a stride wide, and from the first frost till the last it's glass from top to bottom, in a place the sun never finds. A wheel goes onto that. Then the cart begins to go, backward, down a hill, with a year's goods on her and a man's own two beasts in front. What does the man do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:91:"He's Iron, or he's Stone, or he's some small thing that he was given at fourteen and has kept in his pocket for two days. He's had it out before he's thought. Carters declare, on the ice." The factor spat over the wall. "And then they come up out of the sink."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:103:"Twenty, on this bar, when I've led a beast of my own up that cut and down it again with my hand on her, and come home. Not when you tell me it's done." The crook was taken up. "There's a pot for a healer whether she's with a crew or not. There always was. And I'll thank the rest of you not to ask me what you're called."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:111:Cael lay on his stomach there with Lira on his left and Seln on his right, and looked down thirty feet of scree into the cut. He had learned in a library, from a list of other people's bouts, that a place is fought in before it is stood in, and he had never had a better seat to learn one from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:117:The hollow was round, like a bowl, and it lay in the spur's shadow, where the day never came. There was ice on it. The ice was grey and dull, with water standing on it at the edges, and Cael knew that grey: the shelf at Lowmarch had gone that colour a week before the river took it. At one side, under a lip of rock, there was a black place where it had not shut, and he could hear water going down there, a long way, into the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:127:"Five is what can be seen from here," said Seln. "There is a strip under the north bank that this lip hides, perhaps four yards of it, the whole length of the cut. I cannot tell you what lies in it. I should not care to say five to a factor who has just told me five."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:131:He went over the cut itself, a stride at a time, with his eyes. Down the middle of the road ran the thing that the factor had held out a flat hand for. It began at a wet place under the rock at the head, away to his right, and it came down the whole hundred strides as straight as a rule: a stride of blue-white ice, with the dark road on either side of it. In that shade it had never softened. He knew what such ice did to a weight. He had lain on a river with his face against two hands of it, and felt a cart come up out of a hole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:141:He let that go by, since she had not said it to be answered. He was looking at the foot of the cut, away to his left, where the banks ran out and the road came into the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:145:"All three," he said. "Right at the foot. Not one of them's in the cut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:167:"They don't come for a cart that keeps its feet. They come for a declaration. So we give them one: small, and short, and a long way from anybody. At the top of the cut, eighty strides up. One point of Ember on a twist of tow, on the ice. It's out in a breath. And whoever sets it doesn't stay there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:185:"Then here is the whole of the doctrine, for anybody who's not heard it in one piece," he said, and he did not look at the well's far side. "Nobody declares unless they're meant to be loud. One of us, once, in a place that we've chosen beforehand. Everybody else is dark from the foot of that cut to its head, whatever they see. If the loud one can't do it, then nobody's loud, and we walk out by the foot as we came in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:197:"You'll have the foot of the cut," Cael said to Karis. "With the clock, and the mule, and the boxes. It's eighty strides from the mark and sixty from Brom, and it's the last place in that road that anything will come to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:215:They went into the cut from its foot an hour after noon, when the factor judged that the shadow lay deepest and the beasts below were most asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:217:Cael walked by the three carts. He had not meant to look at them, and he looked: a wheel with two spokes gone, and a barrel-head with a hold's mark burned in it, and under the nearest bed a mule's collar, with the straw coming out. Somebody had driven that cart for two days up a silent road with a Path in his pocket. He had kept it there until the very last hundred strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:221:The cold came up out of the cut to meet him like the air from a cellar. He kept to the south edge, where a strip of dead turf and gravel ran beside the ice, a little wider than his two boots. The read asked at every stride what the stuff weighed, and Anchor asked *There?* of the wheel-ruts frozen into it, in its thin held note, as it asked at every sill; he told the one that he knew, and the other no. He did not look at the north bank, which was no higher there than his belt, or think of the strip under it that a lip of rock had hidden.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:229:It was twenty strides, and they took him longer than the sixty had. At the eightieth the cut's head stood above him, black and wet, with the seep coming out under it in a skin over the stones. He stooped, and laid a twist of tarred tow on the ice, in the middle, and stepped back two paces onto the turf. His mouth was dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:239:Wind had his feet before the first stride was down. It leaned him at the foot of the cut with everything that it had, and he told it no, and found that his own two legs had something to offer in its place: a walk, a little bent, close under the scree, such as a girl on a bench had beaten into him with the two halves of a count. He did not run. A thing that ran on that road would be looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:241:He heard them at the tenth stride. It was a sound like gravel poured from a cart, away up on his left, beyond the bank; and it did not come from three places. It came from the whole upper half of the cut together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:She did not touch her coat. Cael marked that, since he had been listening for it. At a board in Lowmarch she had laid her hand on the pocket, and said what was in it, and that she had almost forgotten which side; she had spoken then of a certificate, and she spoke now of a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:31:"The man below counts, since feet are all that he is asked for; the men above do not count those who come by right. They read. A certificate is a name, and the district that issued it, and a number, and it is entered at the gate, with the hour, and goes up to a counter before night to be laid against a list." He did not raise his voice. "There are four names in this loft which must not be written in any book inside that wall. I told you why on the ninth day, and a man who used to certify such papers told you again at a board. One hour is what the paper allows; you would not be at the third gate before it was served."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:63:He had not meant to. He had lain on a pallet with his hands behind his head and listened to the street, and he had found that he could not lie under a town which he had not looked at. He had looked at the foot of it for a day. He wished to see how it stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:She saw it at once. With the wall on his left, the hill would be on his left as well; and whatever stood on the crown of the hill would be behind his shoulder for the whole of the walk, where he need do nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:73:A road ran round the foot of the bluff, under the second wall, and he found it by going uphill until he could go no farther. It was cobbled, and it was narrow, and the houses on its upper side had been built straight against the wall's foot, so that their back rooms were the town's own masonry. He walked with one hand almost on their doors. The wall went up above the roofs on his left, pale and sheer, as high as the scree in a cut that he remembered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:77:The road opened there into a small paved place, where the street from the trough came up to meet the arch. There was a cook-house built against the wall on the near hand, with smoke going up from it, and a water-butt at its corner. Cael put his shoulder against the butt and stood. Nobody looks twice at a person who is waiting beside water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:79:*There?* said Anchor, of the arch. It was a tall one, with a groove cut down each jamb where something heavy could be let fall. He told it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:85:There were people whom the keepers knew. They were the greater number: a woman with a basket and a good cloak, and a pair of clerks, and an old man with a dog. They went by with a nod, and the pen did not move. They lived on the other side of that arch, and they had gone through it twice a day since they were children.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:103:Cael took his shoulder off the butt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:It came out above the river on the bluff's far side, where the town wall ran along a quay, and there was a landing. He had not seen a flat boat since Lowmarch. There were six of them here, tied nose to tail, and a great wooden wheel on the quay with a man walking inside it, round and round, so that a sack went up on a rope at every dozen steps. A ferry lay at the landing's lower end, on a cable. He looked at the cable's blocks for Brom's sake, and could not have said what lay the rope was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:171:He had written two sentences in a book, at a fire, for a healer who would not take his word. They had been easy to write. The square had been two days off, in a town that he had never seen, and a thing which is two days off costs nothing to forgo. He had not known that it would stand at the head of the only street; that the record he had walked here for would be kept behind a door on its far side; or that the two of them would face one another across forty yards of paving, like a pair of people who have been set to keep each other honest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:177:It was not a hard thing to refrain from, today. He had no card, and a blanket hung over a window for him in a hayloft. He thought that it would be harder on the day when he had a card. He turned his back on the street, and went down past the trough, where a brown mule that was not theirs lifted her head and looked through him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:187:He turned that over. In forty years he had entered some hundreds of houses on the seat's business, and he could not call to mind one in which he had seen the back of the kitchen door. He saw this one now. It had been mended with a board of a different wood, and somebody had cut a tally into the frame with a knife, in fives.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:191:He had known it, of course. An officer of his grade did not arrive; he was announced, by a form, four days before, and the form went to the very people whom he came to look at. So he had ridden for forty years through streets that had been swept the day before, past whitewashed kerbs, to offices where every book lay squared on its desk and a man stood at the sill with his cap in his hand. He had allowed for it, as a careful officer allows for the known error of a scale, and had supposed that he could subtract the whitewash and arrive at the street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:199:He had certified this town on four occasions. He had put his name to the soundness of its station, and the order of its gates, and the keeping of its books. He had ridden over a bridge without knowing that it had piers. It had taken a stick, and a brown coat, and a place in the count of a keeper's thumb, to show him what any carter saw from his seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:211:A small grey man had told him at a spring what that hand was. He had not much cared to be told, and it had been true. He laid the hand on his knee again, palm down, and he looked at the room and did nothing to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:219:A boy had given it to him on a bank, at dusk, across a yard of turf. He had it by heart, as he had always had a finding: there was a place, which a healer had found; it had been read twice in a day; nothing had come back. He did not know what had read it. A man of his training could set out the possibilities, in their order, and on the road that morning he had begun to; and he had reached the second, and seen that it was a draft, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:231:"I had not known," he said, "that a man could be fed for four days and still be owed nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:239:Brom had begged a lantern of the woman at the half-door, and it hung from a rafter in the middle of the loft, lit at the kitchen fire with a spill. The six sat or lay in the round of it, and nobody had gone to sleep. Cael had Hesk's book open on his knee, with the day at its head and nothing under it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:247:"What stone?" said Karis. "Did he say what stone the galleries are cut in?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:265:"He comes up at noon to eat," said Seln, "at the cook-house which is built against the wall, beside the blue gate; he stands at its counter, and goes down again within the hour. He has done so on every day that my porter can remember, and he is said to dislike being spoken to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:11:He had known that day. He had written it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:17:He had not been certain. That was the reason that he had given himself, and it had been a good one for two days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:19:A date read once, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, in another person's pencil, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages to find it otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. So might she, for all that she never did. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where it could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:23:He had the sheet itself this time, and daylight, and nobody at his shoulder. He read the technician's date a figure at a time, with his thumb moving under it, as he had not read anything since he was a junior: the year first, and then the day. He turned the sheet over, and looked at the brush's work on its back, and turned it again, and read the date a second time from its last figure to its first. Then he shut his eyes. He set the other date beside it, the one that he had copied twice, and went along the two of them together, as a clerk goes down a copy against its original.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:29:He did not ask himself what it meant. He had a rule for that. It was older than the one that Karis kept, and a good deal less handsome. A clerk who guesses at a file in front of its subject has written the subject's finding for him. He had seen it done to this boy since his Kindling, by men who were better paid than himself and no wiser, each of whom had looked at an empty column and found it unbearable, and had put a word in it. He did not know what a sequence was. He could not have said what it was for one to be initiated, or what lay under a plate, and neither could the man who had carried the sheet three hundred miles. He knew that two papers agreed about a day. He had no more than that, and he did not intend to have more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:37:A fact could be filed. One kept it, and said nothing, and let it lie against the day when it should be useful; and he had kept a great many so. It was what a desk did. He had cut a hundred and forty-seven notches in two hazel wands to count what a desk would do with a clerk's packet, and had been wrong about the manner of it; but he had not been wrong about desks. There was an office somewhere that had held six years of sheets where nobody would receive them. He had been reading its work all morning. He did not care to find, at his age, that he had become a small copy of it, with a locked case for a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:He considered it with care, since he distrusted his own distaste. If he said it at the slab, seven people would have it in the same breath. Karis would be obliged to enter it; she entered everything. She would then be obliged to refuse the sentence that followed from it, aloud, with the boy standing there, and he had watched what that refusal cost her once already today. A man who had certified readings for forty years would hear a day named, and his mouth would open on an order before he could prevent it. A healer would hear it from the basin. And the boy would be told one more thing about himself, in public, by a clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:51:He looked at that squarely, standing over a spring on a moor with a sheet in his sleeve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:75:Seln had been wrong about a desk, after a hundred and forty-seven days of counting it. He had been wrong about this by the width of a slab of rock, and it had taken him less than an hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:He had heard it read, by a lamp, in Lira's voice, from the far end of a table. There was the printed head. There were four short lines in a quick hand that leaned to the right, as a man writes on his knee. He knew what the first of them said, and the third, and where the fourth broke off. They had gone round in him for three days, with the stripe that lay across the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:97:The second line he had never seen. It was a date. There was a year of the registry's in it, and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:103:There was one date that he knew as he knew his own name. He set it beside the technician's, in his head, and went along the two of them a figure at a time: the year, and then the day. He did it without moving his lips, with his two hands flat on the rock. He did not believe what he had, and he did it again from the far end, backward, from the last figure to the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:109:He had been waiting for it, he found, without knowing that he waited. A quiet man had stopped like a clock over that date, by a lamp. There was only one day in the registry's whole count that could have stopped him so on Cael's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:111:*Deep layer*, the sheet said, in the first of its lines; and then two words; and then that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:121:A great many people were classed in a year. On that same morning, in other districts, other children had stood on other plates; a station's man who went below one, somewhere, might have found a thing beginning and written down the day. He did not know that it was his plate. He did not know what a sequence was, or what it meant for one to be running. The sheet did not say what it went with. It had begun to say, and somebody had laid a brush across the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:127:It had Hesk's book half out before he knew what it was about. He had written down everything that had ever happened to him. It was how an old man had taught him to keep from being told what he was: one noted the day, and the count, and the trend. He had the pencil. He had the place, under yesterday's line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:131:If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:147:He had entered every day that had happened to him since an old man put a pencil into his hand, and he supposed that he would go on doing so. There was one line now that Hesk's book would never have. He would always know the place where it was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:155:Vastin had stood through the whole of the morning. He was standing now, a yard from the rock, with both hands on the head of his stick and his eyes on that flat of waxed leather. Cael, who had had reason to watch faces that day, saw something gather in his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:He heard it then. Cael saw him hear it: the four words, in a voice that had said them from a desk every working day for forty years. He stood with his mouth a little open. His right hand had left the stick's head, and it hung in the air a foot from his coat, on its way to a pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:Cael had known that voice for a long while. He had heard it give an inventory, and a warning, and a count of days; he had heard it refuse a good many things. He went back over all of that, standing by a rock on a moor, and he could not find one occasion on which it had told a person how a thing was done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:215:"Treswick," Vastin said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:223:"The only historical-Path practitioner registered west of the Line. If the language is anywhere, it's under his hands. A day and a half."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:239:"He's not been asked," said Lira. "He took a slip." She had come round the slab, and she stood over the small pale woman with her left hand out of her pocket. "You said *ours* at that plank before any of us. I never heard you want a thing before. I'd sooner walk a day and a half behind somebody who wants something than sit at Lowmarch with somebody who's being careful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:249:"The fork by dark," he said. "Oryn, how far is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:251:"Six miles," said the healer. She was drying her hands a finger at a time, and she did not look at him. "There's a fold there with a hearth in its corner, where a fire has walls round it. I'll have something to say when we're sat at it. I've put it off since a board at Lowmarch, and I've run out of road to put it off along."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:3:Oryn came up the cut on the dry edge, at the pace that she kept for a gate with a bell ringing, and she stopped at her stride and a half and looked the whole of it over before she spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:"I'll say when it's my turn to be told." She did not go nearer. "Two days ago, at a board, I opened my mouth about that leg, and you answered a thing that I'd not yet put. You said that you offered it to nobody. So I said that I still hadn't asked, and I let it lie, since that's my rule and I keep it." She pushed her left sleeve a little higher. "This is the asking. There are teeth in that, and under the teeth a joint that's been walked three hundred miles and then stood on by eight of those. I want my hands on it. And I want two fingers on your wrist while I'm there, to learn what's left in you. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:35:"Then stand," said Oryn, "and you'll stand still, and you'll put your weight on the big one's shoulder and not on that stick. I'm not kneeling on a frozen road to have you fall on me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:37:He did as much of that as he could bear to, which was to lay two fingers on Brom's shoulder, as a man touches a banister that he does not mean to use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:57:She got to her feet by Brom's other shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:59:"Thirty days in that binding, and on that stick. You'll not kneel on it. You'll not run. And you'll not stand another of those on it, for anybody, until I've had my hands on it again and said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:67:"I didn't ask you to undertake it. I told you what it costs. Stand a wall on that leg inside the month, and you'll keep the stick for the rest of your days." She had turned to Brom already. "Boot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:75:The upper had been torn across from the lacing to the welt, and two of his toes had gone the colour of a plum. Nothing was broken. She bound them together, and told him that he would know about it for ten days and walk on it all the same; and then she lowered herself onto the stone at his side, with her head down, and her lips moved to fifty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:81:It was a hold's mule, old and sour, with a sack over its eyes, and the factor took it by the cheek-strap and walked it into the cut at the foot, between the dead carts, on the dry edge. Cael understood that he was watching the only test that such a person trusted. They came up the whole hundred strides, past six people and a healer, with a pair of pale eyes going to each grey shape in turn. They went as far as the black wet rock at the head. They turned there, and came down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:99:"Empty's what I posted. I never asked anybody for safe. I've lived on this shoulder since I was born, and that seep'll have its glass back by morning." A hand went into the apron. "There's another den'll find it, in a year, or in five. They'll have to learn it from the beginning, as these did; and by then I'll have had thirty carts of salt up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:119:"That a plane stood across a cut, from a line that cracked the ice. That it stood for as long as he did not move, and went when he let it. That eight struck it and none passed." Her pencil went on. "And I have added a sixth line to my five. *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* I had the three wrecks under my eyes from the foot for the better part of an hour, and I did not read them either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:129:There was no rule against a fire under a roof, behind a wall as thick as a cart. Cael sat with his back to it and felt the cold of the cut go out of him a little at a time. Shadow had come up to his knees with the dark, as it did at every dusk, and he held it there; the man whom it had come from sat two places down, and had not spoken since the ice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:"He's not sat since the cut," she said, under her breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:189:The others were down already in the hay, where he could hear Brom turning his foot about to find a way that it would lie. He began as he began every entry, with the day, and with what could be counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:191:*Two hundred and seventy-ninth day. The shoulder hold. The cut is clear of eight: five killed, by a rock twice, by a knife twice, and once by a thing that I shall come to; three through the ice of the sink. Twenty paid at the gate. In hand, a hundred and thirty-eight. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days. Vastin's right knee: four holes shut by Oryn, the joint bound, on a stick for a month by her word.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:197:He had gone back over it a dozen times since the ice. Eight hounds would have come down to the foot of that cut, and found three quiet things, and stood. They would have tried one of the three with their teeth. He believed that it would have held; a healer and a researcher knew how to be posts, and a mule answered with nothing that a hound hunted. He believed it. He had not been asked to watch it tried, since a man with a stick had put a road's width of refusal in front of it, on his own judgment, with a knee that he knew would not carry him. The rule had been broken, and he had broken a rule himself, twice, for a calf and for a carter. He could not find it in him to enter the breaking as a fault.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:201:*Vastin fought. He rooted the road and nearly got eaten for it — a wall's no good if you're the only thing making noise behind it. He knows fields. He doesn't know packs. He'll learn or he won't. Watched an Iron Wall go up two feet from me. Filed it. Note the day: an Archmarshal took a wound out here and there was no one to report it to. He looked almost free. Almost.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:205:The man had come out of the long house with his copper spent. He had stood under the lantern at the byre's door, looking down at the linen on his knee, and his right hand had gone to the breast of the brown coat, where a pen would have been. Cael knew the gesture by now. An officer hurt on duty wrote it up within the day, no doubt, under two hands, with the circumstances; there would be a sheet for it, and a tray. The hand had found a receipt in a small woman's writing, and nothing else. It had stayed there a moment. Then it had come away empty, and Vastin had looked up at the black shoulder of the hill, and for the space of three breaths his face had had nothing in it that Cael had ever seen there: no room that he was being fair to, and no file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:233:On the two hundred and eightieth day they went up through the cut as soon as it was light, on the dry edge, in file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:241:"The mule's carried worse than you," said Oryn. She had the cheek-strap, and the three boxes sat on that brown back as they had sat since the landing. "Two of those would shift to the other side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:Beyond the cut's head the ruts came out onto the open shoulder, and the whole country changed. It was high moor, bare and brown, rolling away westward in long swells like a sea that had stopped. The wind came over it steadily from the west, with nothing in it but cold, and it had not left off since the first of them topped the rise. There was no tree. Far off to the north a line of blue hills stood up, with snow on them still. Somewhere under that western sky, not two days off, the country that kept no books came to its end; Cael had crossed that edge once, going the other way, by another road, with his name in every ledger behind him, and he had not turned his head to look at it then either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:251:Lira walked with her left hand out of her pocket. It was the fifteenth day, and she was carrying her own knife-belt in it, coiled, for no better reason than that it was something to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:253:They had gone perhaps four miles when Seln's two fingers went up, and then his whole hand, pointing off the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:255:There was an outcrop of the dark plated stone a furlong to the south, as big as a barn, and under its eastern face the ground was green, where a spring came out. It rose in a basin that somebody had built round with stones, a long while ago, and ran over the basin's lip and away down the moor in a thread of rushes. Beside it a single slab of the rock had fallen flat from the face. It lay level, waist-high on its own rubble, twelve feet long and half as broad, with the outcrop standing between it and the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:259:She looked at that slab as Cael had seen her look at a wall of cut courses in a marsh. It was off the road by a furlong; a person might speak there. It was in daylight, and dry. And there was not a bench near it, or a meal-sack, or anybody's elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:265:"It is the table that I have been looking for since the night before last. I said that I should count in daylight. I have carried six years of an office's paper for two days up a road and through a fight, and I have not yet turned a sheet of it." She had the flat out from under her arm already. "I want the forenoon. I want pebbles, a great many, about the size of a hen's egg, since there is a wind. And I want every person here who can read to wash his hands in that basin first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:273:Cael looked at the road, which went on westward over the next swell and out of sight. Somewhere beyond that swell there was a fork, and beyond the fork a rise. He had ruled on a job, and the job was done and paid, and nobody had asked him this morning which way they were walking. They had simply walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:3:The fork-stone stood where the ruts parted, a slab of the dark rock as tall as Brom, set on end by people who had left no other mark on that moor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:They came to it at dusk on the two hundred and eightieth day. One pair of ruts went on past it to the west, and began at once to climb a long bare rise that shut out the sky on that side. The other turned north, along the moor's edge, toward the blue hills. Somebody had cut a hand into each face of the stone, with the fingers pointing, and a hundred cold terms had worn both hands nearly smooth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:7:The fold lay a little way down the northern branch, in a dip. It was a drovers' pen, round, of dry stone laid shoulder-high without a grain of mortar, and three parts of it were open to the sky. The fourth had been roofed with slabs. In that corner there was a hearth built into the wall, with a flue, and beside the hearth a stack of cut peat as high as a man's waist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:"You burn what you want, and you cut as much again before you go, from the hag behind," said Oryn. "It's been the rule here since before the stone was stood up. A fire with a wall round it and a roof on it isn't on a road. I've slept here on thirty rounds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:11:Brom built it, laying the peats on end as she showed him, and Karis set one point among them without being asked; and the thing caught slowly, with a sour brown smell that Cael had never met. It was the first flame that the seven of them had sat by since a hold on a shoulder, and only the second since the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:13:They did not all sit. Vastin stood in the gap of the fold, where the hurdle went, with his back against the stone and the bound leg out before him. He had come six miles from the spring on that stick, at the tail of the column, and had been offered a mule twice; and he had the grey look of a man who is counting what is left in him and finding it sufficient by a very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:"There's a thing that none of you has asked me in two hundred and eighty days, and I've been glad of it. I'll say it once, since it bears on what I want of him." She did not look at Cael. "I'd my rank off a board at the foot of a bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:41:"Not for the healer's. For theirs. I filled that column in for a long while before I asked what it was for; I thought that it was like the name. Then there was a week of fever in the low town, and the house was full to the stairs, and I found out." She spoke as she spoke a finding. "We'd so much in us, the six of us together, as I've so much in me tonight, and it had to be spent on somebody. It went down the column from the top. There was a Gold in a bed by the window with a wrenched wrist. He had what he wanted on the first day. Then the Silvers had theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:65:She had turned from the fire, and her face was as level as he had ever seen it, and a good deal less tired than it had been at the cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:79:"A provision of that kind is granted by the week. I have seen the form. In the first week the keeper of a place decides whether he believes what he has been shown. In the second there is work. And nobody who has waited years for a thing has ever left it at the end of a second week." His hands were in his sleeves. "I should not put my name to it. I should be surprised to be wrong by more than four days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:81:"Three weeks is five gates and a ford, at my pace, with a day in hand," said Oryn. "That'll put me at Millrace. I'll be at the mill's bench when you come down off that road, and if you're late I'll hear of it from a carter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:103:He took the book out of his coat, and opened it on his knee, toward the fire. He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it. Then he wrote what he had been asked for, and it took him a good deal longer than the length of it deserved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:109:*The fork. Oryn north; five holds; routing, not the other verb. Her term, given at the fire, taken in the log because that's the form she'll take it in: inside Treswick I do not go near the station. Not the steps. Not the square. Written here so she can read it, and so I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:113:She did not take it; she bent, with her hands on her knees, and read it where it lay in his, from the day to the last word, with her lips moving. He had written *inside*, and he had not seen that until he heard himself say it. There was no slip any longer between himself and that word; he had put it down in pencil, in the plain hand, as a place where he would be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:129:"Fifteen days. It held a hound yesterday, by what I'm told, and it's none the worse. It's slow, and it'll be slow for as long again. They've stone floors where you're going, and stairs. Don't fall on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:131:"I've been not falling on it for fifteen days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:137:"That's a foot. Ten days, I said, and it's nine now. The shoulder's another woman's knot, and I can't read through it, and she's the far side of a river. You'll be a month and more from her." She put one finger on the drover's turn, as the old woman had, and took it away. "If it tells you anything, you put down whatever you're carrying. You don't undo that. You find somebody with a bench."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:149:"You've never had my hands on you, and you've never offered, and I'll not ask; you'd say no, and I'd sooner not make you say it." Seln inclined his head by a very little. "I'll price what I can see from here. You've carried that on the one shoulder for longer than I've known you, and it's pulled you a finger out of true. Change shoulders."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:165:"This is the first day. There are twenty-nine after it. It's stood a spring and six miles of road, and it's no worse than I left it, which is more than you deserved." She sat back. "Stick; binding; no kneeling. No walls. You'll want to carry something tomorrow, when those boxes come off my mule. You're not to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:197:The boxes came off the mule on the two hundred and eighty-first day, as soon as there was light to rope by, and the frost was white on the fork-stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:199:Brom unroped them himself. He had lashed them on the brown back at a ferry's shed with the beckets outward, so that they might go up any road; he had not said then that the last of that road would be on people's shoulders, and he did not say it now. He took the heaviest on his left side, where the webbing was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:There was not a great deal else to be done, since Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had carried them off a landing. The tarred case that held a dead man's cord had come off the mule last of all, and it leaned against the foot of the fork-stone, where Brom had set it. The healer's pannier stayed on the mule, with her linen in it, and a bow hung beside it with its one bolt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:He went back down to the stone at his own pace. He did not kneel to the thing. He hooked its strap with the head of his stick, and brought it up to his hand, and slung it on the shoulder above his sound leg; and then he came up after them, a step at a time, and stopped where Cael stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:263:The box rode badly on Cael's shoulders for the first furlong, and then he found the way of it, as he had on a stair with something heavier. At a quarter of a mile he turned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:265:He had not meant to; but the road had begun to climb, and from where he stood the whole of the fork lay below him in the early light, with the stone at the parting, and the round pen, and a thread of blue going up from its corner where the peats still smouldered. There was nobody on the northern rut for a long way. Then he found them again, very small, beyond the bend of the moor's edge: a dark upright speck and a lower one. They were not hurrying, and neither had turned its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:273:It was a long bare swell of the moor, a mile and more from its foot to its head. The ruts went up it aslant, as ruts do when they have been cut by loaded carts, and the wind came over the top of it into their faces. Nobody spoke, since it was a road, and the hold lay on all of them; and there was a box on three backs, and a man on a stick behind, who would not be waited for and could not be left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:279:And there was a day. He had not written that, and he did not go over it. He only knew where it lay, as a person knows without looking where the stair is in his own house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:283:The ruts went over a lip of bare rock and began to go down; and the whole country opened in front of him, as it had opened once from a shelf, on the second day past the Line, when it was the east that he was looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:285:This was the west. The moor fell away in long brown folds to a plain, and the plain was green. There were fields on it, with hedges, and he had not seen a hedge for two hundred and eighty days. A river came down out of the north and went across that green in loops, shining, and in the nearest of the loops the land stood up in a bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:289:He counted it, since he counted anything: it went up in three steps. A wall ran round the foot of the bluff, grey and long, with a great many roofs behind it. Higher, where the ground steepened, there was a second wall, and the roofs inside that were of slate. The third was at the top, close and pale, about the crown of the rock. It was a day's walk off still, and he could have covered the whole of it with his thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:291:"Three tiers," said Lira, at his shoulder. She had set her box down. "They've built the ladder out of stone. I'd wondered what it would look like from outside."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:293:Nobody else said anything, and Karis had stopped a yard in front of them all. She stood with a calfskin under her arm and the wind pulling at her coat, and looked at a place whose name she had read once on the spine of a catalogue that she was not let to open. Seln was counting gates, with his lips. Brom lowered his box to the rock and rolled his left shoulder, and studied those three walls as he would have studied any wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:305:Vastin stood with both hands on its head, getting his breath, with the bound leg a little out and a tarred case on his shoulder. He looked at the town for some time. He had gone in at those gates, no doubt, on a horse, behind a clerk, with a seal; and he looked now as a man looks at a house that he once had the keys of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:He had kept his hand a hand's width from that page for the whole of a lamp, so that he might still say no to it. He had ruled on a slip instead, at a plank, in daylight. He had walked three days up a road behind a job. Somewhere on that road the no had gone, without his having marked the stride; it had gone at a spring, perhaps, between two hands laid flat on a rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:331:He got the box onto his shoulders again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:337:The stick was on his left. It came down on the turf at the rut's edge a little after each of his own strides, and a dead man's cord swung beside it in its tarred case. Nobody had told that man where to walk, on the first day of this road, and he had walked last, and a little too far back. Nobody had told him today. He had only not dropped behind when the file made up; and he was there, a shoulder off, on the ditch side, an old man who had been a marshal and was learning to be nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:5:He had slept beside it, after a fashion, on the meal-sacks. It was one line out of an old register, copied by a man who had then walked three hundred miles to lay it on that table, and it sent whoever read it to a town. He had got up twice in the dark to see that it was there, which was foolish, since a sheet of desk paper does not walk; and each time he had stood over it, keeping both hands by him, and had gone back to the sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:27:*Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:33:Cael read the slip a second time, as he read anything, and it was the last five words that he read. Two roads left the western landing. One ran away to the south and west for three hundred miles, to a ford and a station and a house with a seal, and an old man had come down it on a stick. The other was the border road. It went up over a moor to the Line by the short way, and Cael knew what stood at the end of it, since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and told him; and last night he had read it again, in a square black copy, under a title.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:35:"Two days west," said Lira, with her lips. She did not say the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:"They're for a slip. There's a cut two days up a road, with something in it that takes carts, and a hold that'll pay twenty to have it out. I can price that. I know what a clearance costs; each of you has a mark on you from the last one." He kept his eyes on the bark. "I'm not ruling on a town. I said that I'd not rule over a thing I hadn't touched, and I've still not touched it. I can't price a town. Every figure that I've got for it is somebody else's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:"It's the same road, and I can read. I'm not going to stand at this post and pretend that I took a job without seeing where it lay, and I'd not ask any of you to pretend it either." He made himself look at them then: a big man with four yards of webbing on him, and a girl with her left hand in her coat pocket, and a small pale woman holding six years of somebody's counting under her arm. "The boxes come. So do the papers, and the kit. I'll not leave a thing of ours in a store for a week with a river between. When the cut's done we'll be two days nearer than we are this morning, with the road going on in front of us, and I'll know more about what I'm walking with than I do now. That's all that I can honestly say I've decided. It's a job. I'll not make it a vow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:79:"Five," said Pike. He made the strokes at the slip's foot, and then he did not dip his pen again. He turned his shoulder to them. "And who's your healer?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:83:"It's a clearance. I've written a good many of those taken, and I've written some of them closed with a line drawn under a name. In my second year here a crew of four went up the north water, and one of them bled for two days on a hurdle for want of anybody who knew where to put a thumb. Since then I don't write a clearance taken until I've a healer said. It's not the hold's rule. It's mine." He scratched his stump. "Teague's old woman goes up the cold road with him. So who's yours?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:"I've a bench here that's done by noon, and it's had six mornings of me. The mill's my second stop. It's waited a whole cold term, and it can wait four days more while I go round by the top and come down on it from above; I've five gates up that way that I'd have had to climb to in any case." She threw the cloth over her shoulder. "I said once that somebody ought to stand at an edge with her sleeves up. I'm not having a girl with a fortnight's arm go into a cut behind something that takes carts, and hearing about it afterward from a carter. Write me. I'm a line on that slip. I'm not a name under theirs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:Pike considered her in his double fashion. Six days ago he had had no word in his book for a woman who did what nobody had posted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:101:They were on the landing an hour before noon, with the mist gone and the river a hand lower than it had been at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:105:Cael left the word with Pike, as he had promised a better captain that he would: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever lay beyond it, with no day put on the coming back. Pike wrote nothing. He said that he had a season line of theirs with no number against it, and had stopped expecting to fill it by the week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:Teague's salt was going down the planks. There were forty sacks of it in a grey heap at the landing's head, and the ferryman would have no cart on his boat yet, or any bale that a man could not carry; and so it went aboard on backs. The bow took a sack at a time, and came up again easing his weight onto the right leg. The boy took two, and was told by nobody to take one. The tall man carried his on the shoulder that the spear did not use, with seven feet of ash in his other hand, shod with its steel again, and he set each sack down in the scow's waist square upon the last, as though he were laying a course of stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:"West. There's a cut below a hold, two days up the border road, that's been taking carts. It's ours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:"The shoulder hold. I've carried salt up to them. It's a bad piece of road in the cold; it lies in its own shadow, and there's water comes down the middle of it. I never lost a mule there, since I went through at noon, on foot, with their heads held." His eyes had gone past Cael's shoulder, up the bank, to where a brown coat was coming down a stride at a time. They stayed there a stride longer than a crew looks, as they had the morning before, on a pair of hands on the head of a stick, wrapped now in strips of the keeper's linen. "He's walking with you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:It was as near as he had ever come to giving advice, and Cael took it as he would have taken a touch called. He did not say that he knew exactly what was at his back. He had sat across four feet of table from it, once, for three days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:The chin came up by the width of a finger. Then he turned to his sacks. The tall man went by with another on his shoulder, and gave five people and a healer the length of a stride, and not a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:143:Vastin had his copper ready. He gave it with the short stiff bow, and stepped over the rail bad leg first, and went aft without being told, to the post where a new strap of stern line had been rove. He stood there through the whole of the crossing with his hands on his stick, a foot from the thing that he had held two days before with his back, and did not touch it. The ferryman, at the sweep, looked once at him and once at the strap. Cael thought that it was the nearest that man had ever come to thanking anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:147:Above the western landing a long stack lay under tarred sheets, as it had lain since the last boat of the old year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:It was a road again as soon as the landing was out of hearing, and the hold came down on all of them as it had on every road in that country. The sigils stayed in their pockets. Cael's ten lay shut; the read asked at the first bend, civilly, what the wet ruts weighed, and was told that it need not know. Seln went ahead by thirty yards with his hands a little away from his sides. Lira came next, with the bow on her back. Brom had the mule's cheek-strap, with Karis at the off side of the load and Oryn at the near, and Cael walked behind the mule, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:175:"There's a rule out here, and you've been inside it since that landing. I'd sooner you had it from me than from the thing that it's about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:177:"I should be glad of it. I have noticed for twelve days that people on this road do not speak, and I took it for manners."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:179:"It isn't manners. There's a thing that lives along the roads, grey, as high as a calf at the shoulder. The holds call it a stillhound. It'll walk beside you for a mile at forty yards, and you can talk, or drop a pot, and it only follows. What it's waiting for is a Path." He kept his voice at the pitch that the road allowed by day. "It hunts whatever goes out of a person when a Path's used. Karis has a word for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:205:They made eleven miles that day, by Brom's reckoning, which was a mile more than a certain knee could do, and nobody remarked on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:213:He was in the middle of the road, upright between the two ruts, with his weight laid evenly on both feet and the stick grounded, as he had stood on a landing in Ostrand and at the head of a ferry's planks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:255:With the dark, the quietest of the ten came up in Cael to the height of his knees, and he held it there, as he did at every dusk. A healer sat on the next stone with her sleeves down. She had looked at a stiff leg four times since the bread was cut, and Tide, the tenth, lay as still in him as she sat, and for the same reason: nobody had been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:He had not known where else to stand. He had touched neither the cord nor either strip of brass for a hundred and twelve days, by his own ruling, since a thing that measured had read one way in four hands and another in his. Paper was not a cord. A sheet said what it said, whoever turned it. He told himself so, and kept his hands on the rock's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:"There are five columns, and they are the same five on every sheet in six rows. The first is the day, in the registry's reckoning. The second is the station: he measures from eight points round the edge, by the compass and its halves, as the man did whose book I have. The third is the measure. The fourth is headed *Instruments*. And the fifth is the narrow one, which is headed in plain letters for the anomaly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:111:She had said it twice in an inn yard, a hundred and twelve days ago, to him. She said it now to herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:121:Seln had read all six rows while she was at the fourth column, standing at her shoulder and then at the next heap, and he had not touched a pebble that he did not put back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:127:"There is no routing head on any sheet. That you knew. There is no surveyor's name; a field book is signed, and an abstract ought to carry the name that the book carried, and these carry a number in its place. There is nothing to say who ordered a man to walk round the same places for six years. And there is no column for a cause." He let that stand a moment. "A form is a list of the questions that its maker expected to be asked. Somebody expected to be asked the day, and the measure, and whether the cord was true. Nobody expected to be asked why the thing happens. I have never before seen an office measure a matter so carefully and show so little wish to understand it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"I found that on two of our places, in the field, and not on the others," said Karis. "I have turned it over for a hundred and sixty-five days. I could make nothing of two with it and two without. And here it is on all four." Her pencil touched each square in turn. "The Fallow has it. The hall has it. A man with a punch did not trouble to strike it on their iron, I suppose, or struck it on a bar that we never dug for. The office has never once left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:195:"It has a square. A printer cut a block with a ruled place in it, and a heading over the place, for a clerk to fill. Nobody rules a place on a form for a thing that is always the same." She was speaking very fast now, and very small. "It is a class. It is the kind of thing that a certificate has, at its head, where it says what its bearer is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:255:A sleeve came past his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:9:Nobody did. They had come two hundred and eighty-one days through a country where a road was a place for hands and not for mouths, and the habit had gone deeper into them than any of the six had known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:13:"The hedge does. A hound does not lie up where a plough goes, and a thing that hunts what a Path gives off wants a mile of nothing round its den." Seln nodded at the bank. It had been laid within the year, with the stems cut half through and bent over and woven between stakes. "Somebody tends this. That town works these fields to the foot of the moor, and it will keep a watch that rides as far as its fields go. There will be posts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:23:Lira laughed. It was one short breath through the nose, and it was the first sound of the kind that Cael had heard on a road since a meadow at Lowmarch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:31:They went on between the hedges, and for half a mile nobody used the leave that he had been given. Cael found that he had a great deal to say and no notion of how a person began. He had not been silent all those days; they had talked at every fire, behind every wall, and through a whole cold term in a back room. It was only on a road that the mouth was shut, with the sigils in their pockets and his ten lying closed, and he had left off noticing it as a carter leaves off noticing his own wheels. He noticed it now, as it lifted. It was like setting down the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:The first post stood where a lane came in from the north. It was a dressed stone as high as Cael's waist, squared, with a figure cut deep in its eastern face, and under the figure three short bars, one above another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:*What does it weigh?* asked the read, civilly, of the post, as it had asked of every rut for three days. Cael told it that it need not know. Brom lowered his box to the verge, and rolled the left shoulder, and looked at his right boot as a man looks at a debtor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:53:Cael would not have marked it, had he not been doing the same thing himself for three miles. Since the foot of the rise the town had dropped behind the hedges, and come up again, and dropped; and each time that it rose he had put his eyes on the lowest wall and held them there, since there was a small pale light above the highest roofs that did not flicker. It was work. It was like carrying a second box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:55:She had never once looked up. He went back over the morning, and he was sure of it. On the rise she had looked at three walls and called them a ladder. After that she had come down two miles of open road with the whole bluff in front of her, and that quick chin had stayed level the whole way, on Brom's heels, and on a hedge, and on a boy's geese. Lira looked at the top of everything. She had looked at the tops of walls since a house on a bluff, to see what it would cost to stand there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:71:She walked with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had since a landing, and her eyes had not left the bluff since it came up over the hedges. She was not looking at its walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:73:"It is bedded," she said, before he could speak. "The rock. Do you see the lines in the face of it, under the lowest wall, where the river has cut? Each of those is a floor that was laid down and then covered. I have been trying to count them for a mile, and I lose my place at the seventh."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:"At the spring you said what he was. The man under it." He shifted the box. "You said a historical-Path practitioner, and I let it go by, since I'd a mule to fetch. What does one do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:He turned the phrase over for a furlong, with the box riding on his shoulders. *How a thing is built.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:99:He had done both. It was a hundred and sixty-one days since he had laid two fingers on his own wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:129:"So you've settled what she'd say, and she's not here to say different." There was no unkindness in it. She hitched the beckets higher on her shoulders. "Write down that I asked. When you write it. I'd like it in there that somebody did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:"And I'll sit on the bank. If Brom comes round that hedge and finds you flat on your back with a hand on your chest, he'll have you over his shoulder before you can open your eyes, box and all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:137:The second post stood beside a brook, where a plank bridge had been thrown over for people on foot and the carts went through the water. It said six. They had done three miles in an hour and a half, and it was a stick that had set the pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:Vastin did not sit. He put his back against the post and both hands on the head of his stick, and stood with the bound leg thrust out before him, as he had stood at every halt for two days. A man in that binding could get himself down to the ground. It was the getting up again that could not be done without another person's arm; and Cael had watched him measure that at a fold, and at a fork, and at the top of a rise, and choose his feet every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:There was a gate into the field behind the hedge, hung on a post of the same dressed stone as the mile-mark. Cael lifted its latch. He set his box down inside, on a headland where the grass had not been ploughed, and lay flat in the lee of the bank with his boots toward the brook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:Wind was first, since it was the oldest, and it had always leaned toward a girl. It was a dry hiss in his feet, like pale grass going over in a breath of air. Two days ago, in a cut, it had carried him ten strides to a bank, away from the people whom he was guarding; and he had paid for those at the end of them, as he paid for every burst, with a locked beat in which he could not have lifted a heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:167:Shadow he knew by what was missing. By day Shadow lay shut under its seal, and it made no sound whatever; he could tell where it was only as a person tells where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there. It would lift at dusk, as it lifted every dusk. He would tell it no.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:183:It was farther out than he had ever marked, since on every other night he had been looking ahead, at the place itself. There was no check in the lean, and nothing piled. The Wind bore off as a road bears off round the shoulder of a hill, which has gone that way for as long as there has been a road. He leaned with it. He went by on the one hand, and did not look in; the lean eased, a long way beyond, and the hiss ran on toward his feet as though nothing had ever lain in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:The going-along felt current, and it had never done anything else. It had been a healer's means of telling how the rivers ran in a body, quick or slow or choked, and it knew a river only by going along with it. Set it where nothing ran, and it did not come back and say *empty*. It did not come back and say *full*, either. It had nothing to go along with, and so it said nothing at all; and for the better part of a year he had been writing that nothing down in pencil, with the day at its head, as though it had been an answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:He had been putting a cord on a stone and writing down *no length*. A cord is made to be run out along a road, and it tells a person how far he may go. Laid on a boulder in a field it has nowhere to run. A patient man may walk it round the outside, and knot it, and learn how far the thing is about; but of what lies in the stone the cord gives no figure whatever, and it was that blank which he had been entering, with the day at its head, as though the stone had told him so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:The road ran straighter from the brook, between hedges that were older and thicker, and it began to be a town's road. There were carts in both directions. At five miles there was a farm that stood close on the verge, with a woman beating a rug in its yard; at four there was a lane with a signboard at its mouth, and a painted hand on the board, and Seln read whatever stood under the hand without breaking his stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:237:The town came up with every furlong. He kept his eyes on its foot, and he learned the foot of it very well. The lowest wall ran clear round the bluff, as far as he could follow it, with square towers at even distances, and he counted eleven of those before the river hid the rest. There was a gate in it with a tower on either hand. A bridge went up to the gate, long and low, and there were things crawling on the bridge that he took for wagons.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:The stick had taken its place again on Cael's left, on the ditch side. It had not lost a stride on him in six miles. He could hear what that was costing, since the breath beside him came short at each planting of the bound leg, and the tarred case had been shifted twice to the other shoulder and twice back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:255:He said it to the road in front of him, at about the fourth mile, in a voice pitched for one hearer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:"I shall want a quarter of an hour of you before that town," said Vastin. "Not on the road. Somewhere that a man can stop. I have carried a thing three hundred miles that is not in any flat, and I find that I should like to set it down before I go through a gate behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:271:Cael turned that over. He had kept a question for that man since a board at Lowmarch, and had thought himself the only one of the two who was carrying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:3:They came to the third post with the sun a hand above the town, and they were not going to do the last three miles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:Seln had known it for some time. He had read two boards at two lane-ends, and both had said the same thing in the same paint: the gate was shut at sunset and opened at first light, and whoever was outside it slept outside it. A stick could not have done three miles in what was left of the day. Nobody said so, since nobody needed to; and it was Vastin himself who looked at the sun, and at the figure on the stone, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:23:"It is one point, on a town's ground, three miles from a gate. Seln?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:25:"She is right," said Seln. He had not sat; he stood at the hedge's end, where he could see the road in both directions, with his hands in his sleeves. "Those two will be at that gate before us in the morning, and a carter talks while he waits. *There was a woman at the three-mile who lit a fire by looking at it.* It is not a crime; it is a line for somebody to write."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:31:Vastin had his bowl standing, in the gap at the hedge's end, across from Seln. He ate as he did everything, with attention, and he thanked Brom for it, once. His face was the grey of the stone that he leaned beside. He had come nine miles since noon on one leg and a stick; and when the bowl was empty he held it for a while, as though the setting of it down were a thing that would have to be planned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:47:It stood at the pull-in's western end, where the hedge stopped and the ditch ran on beside the road. He sat down on the bank with his back against the stone and his face to the east. The moor lay along the sky there, a long way off and very dark; they had been on the top of it at noon. The town was behind his shoulder, with whatever lights it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:He had found a thing at a brook, and it had seemed to him a large one. But he had found it cold and empty, after a quarter of an hour on his back in a field, and he had learned from Karis what a first count was worth. She never trusted a figure until it had been made a second time, by a person in a different temper. He was in a different temper now. He had a bowl in him, and nine miles, and he was too tired to want anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:85:It was the first time that Cael had seen him lower than his own two feet since a cut on a moor, where he had gone down because nothing was left in him to stand with. This was not that. He had chosen it. A man who had stood for forty years at the upper end of every room had put himself where he could not rise without the arm of a boy whom the registry had no column for; and he had done it before he said a word, so that it could not afterward be taken back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:101:"It began with a day, and a word in square brackets, and after those two lines it was instruments: a registrar at a market town, with a counter and a pen; wardens, who came and stood in a yard and wrote; a panel; the frames of a house on a hill, at the end of every term, and the officers who watched the frames. Each of them laid upon you whatever he had been issued, and wrote down what it showed him, and what it showed him went under the last sheet and was sent up." The left hand moved on the stick. "There was not a paper in that folder which was not somebody's reading of you. I have handled a great many files, and I had not before met one in which the subject had been asked nothing and measured so often."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:109:"When the folder had grown as thick as my hand," said Vastin, "the seat did what it does when its instruments have not served. It sent the one that it keeps for such cases. It sent me. I came up that road with a plate, and a set of questions, and three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:113:"Four feet. I measured it, as I measure everything." His voice had not altered at all, and it was that which made it hard to listen to. "I brought to those three days whatever forty years had given me: I have stood on eleven fields, and made something over nine thousand evaluations, and I can tell a man's rank from the way that he takes a stair, before he has reached the head of it. At the end of the third day I went down your hill with nothing whatever. The plate had lain as flat as a dish; every question had been answered, civilly, and I believe that every answer was true; and I could not have built one finding upon the whole of them. I had never in my life come away from a person without a figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:141:"I did not hope for it. If that plate lit, then I had sat four feet from you for three days, with everything that I have, and had seen nothing of a thing that a sheet of brass could find in eleven seconds. I should have had to ask what my eyes were worth, and to ask it of nine thousand readings, each of which I had sworn to." His breath went out. "So I could afford neither answer: the one would have been turned upon you, and the other upon me. I weighed the pair of them for the better part of a night; and I found that it was the second which kept me from sleeping, and that a better man would have been kept awake by the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:151:He had wanted that answer for four days, since a board at Lowmarch, where he had kept the question behind his teeth and let a small pale woman ask hers. He saw now that he had wanted it for a great deal longer. He had turned it over at an academy, and on a bluff, and across two hundred and eighty days of country; and he had tried mercy in the place where the reason should go, and then policy, and then some long game of the seat's, and every one of them had been a thing done to him or for him. He had been given the true one in a borrowed room, and had never quite believed that it was all. It had not once occurred to him that what lay folded inside it might be a man's relief on his own account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:155:It did not. There had been people in his life who had filed him because they were afraid, and each of them had called it procedure to the end. This one had done a right thing, for the reason that he gave. He had then walked three hundred miles on a bad knee and got down on a bank, where he could not rise, to say that he had also been glad of it for a wrong one. Nobody had asked him. Nobody living could have found it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:167:"You heard her at the fork," he said. "The healer. She told the fire that she'd had her hands on me a long while back, and found a thing, and made me write it down and leave it alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:173:The old head came round a little, and stopped. Cael watched the question arrive. He had seen a great many arrive in that face across a table, each in its order, and he knew the look of this one: it was the obvious next thing, and any officer living would have put it, and put it at once. *By whom?* The healer was two days north, with a mule. There was a boy on a bank who had lately had a hand on his own chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:223:He wrote it by the fire, with the book on his knee and Lira's shoulder a foot from his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:225:He set the day at the head, since he could not write anything without it, and the place: the three-mile post. Then he put down what he owed, before what he had found, as an old man in Denvash had taught him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:233:*Ran the reading on myself again. Same nothing at the center. I think the reading can't do it — you can't measure a stillness with a thing that only feels movement. I need an instrument for what doesn't move. Karis says there's a man in Treswick who reads how things are built. Maybe that's the instrument. Maybe I'm about to hand a stranger the one question I've never let anyone hold. Tomorrow. The walls are close enough now to have a color.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:237:He had not known that he was afraid of it until the pencil said so. He had kept that question since he was fourteen, on a great many desks, the wrong way up. Registrars had reached for it, and wardens, and an Archmarshal with a plate; and a healer had found the edge of it with her two hands, on a mountain, and had been made to wait a long while for the rest. Every one of them had come to him, and he had never in his life carried it to anybody. And he was walking to a town, three miles off, to lay it down in front of a man whom he had not seen, on the word of a catalogue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:277:He did not turn round. Neither did she. They sat with their backs to it, and their faces to a moor that the six of them had spent the whole day coming down, until the thorn had burned to a red heap and Brom said that somebody ought to sleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The carters were gone before the two hundred and eighty-second day had any light in it, and they had left their fire raked out and a stack of thorn beside the ring for whoever came next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:5:The six did the last three miles through river meadows, in a white mist that lay as high as Brom's belt. The road ran on a causeway there, with a ditch on either hand, and the town stood up out of the mist ahead of them like a thing set on a table. Cael kept his eyes on the foot of it. He had done so for a day now, and it had begun to be a habit of his neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:9:It was wider than he had judged from the rise, and it ran brown and quick with the thaw of a whole moor in it. A bridge went over, long and low, with five piers standing in the water. He counted them twice, since Karis would ask. The roadway was only wide enough for one cart, with a bay built out over each pier where a person on foot could stand aside; and beyond the last pier there were two square towers with an arch between them, and the grey wall going away on both hands along the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She had stopped so short that he all but walked the box into her. She stood on the bank at the bridge's end, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, and she was not looking at the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:23:"There's none," he said. "It's cut to fit. You'd not get a knife in. Whoever laid that never meant to come back and point it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:25:"And the tower. Look where the tower's foot comes down on the bridge's end." Her voice had gone up, and thin. "The courses do not meet. The mason has cut each of his pale stones to lie against the dark, one at a time, as a joiner cuts a shelf to fit a crooked wall. Nobody does that with a thing that he is building at the same time. He does it when the thing was standing there before he came, and he has been told to build up against it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:27:"So the bridge was first," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:"The bridge was first. The bridge was here when there was no wall for it to lead to." She straightened, and she had gone a little pink, which he had seen perhaps three times. "I can say that. I can say it on the evidence of my own eyes, on a river bank, without a catalogue. It is the first thing that I have been able to say about that town which I did not read on the spine of somebody else's book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:43:"I have ridden over this bridge on four occasions," said Vastin. "I could not have told you that it had piers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:47:The gate was open, and it was nothing like what he had been afraid of since a board at Lowmarch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:49:He had made a picture of it in a back room at Lowmarch, from two sentences of Seln's: a gate, with a book in it. In his picture there had been a counter across the arch, and a man behind the counter with a pen, and a line of people giving their names one after another. He had carried that pen with him up the whole of a border road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:53:It lay across the roadway under the arch, a single long stone, worn hollow in the middle, with a socket cut in each end where a bar had once dropped. He knew what it was before Seln's hand moved. He had stepped over its fellow going the other way, on another road, two hundred and eighty-two days ago, with four others; and he had begun a count that morning which he had kept ever since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:59:The keeper sat on a high stool just within, in the shadow of the arch, where he could see everything that came off the bridge. He wore a long coat of grey wool that had never been dyed, and he had a frame on his knee with three wires across it and wooden beads on the wires. He did not look at faces. He looked at feet, and wheels, and hooves; and for each thing that came over the sill his thumb moved a bead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:He had come back inside a Compact wall for the first time since he walked down a bluff with a house's certificate in his coat; and he had come as the fifth part of a movement of a stranger's thumb. Nobody had asked what he was. No pen had been lifted. He went on up the paved slope beyond the arch with his box on his shoulders, and his heart going as it had gone on the lip of a cut, and found that he could not have said whether he was relieved or insulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:The old man came over the sill on his stick, with the tarred case on his shoulder. The keeper's eyes were on the stick's foot. The thumb moved one bead, and stayed there, and the grey head did not lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:81:Vastin walked through the gate of Treswick as the sixth bead on a wire, and nobody stood up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:It ran straight from the gate toward the bluff's foot, between tall houses of the pale stone, and it was full from wall to wall of things being moved. There were carts, and handcarts, and people bent under frames, and barrels rolled along by boys. There was a great noise of wheels on cobbles. After two hundred and eighty days of roads on which nobody spoke, it came against Cael's ears like a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:93:Cael saw them when he had been shown. Each was a slab of black slate as tall as a child, hung beside the step, and each was ruled with chalk into the same columns. He read the nearest upside down by old habit, and then turned his head and read it the right way. *Wanted*, said its head. Under that, a load, or a number of hands. Then a place: *the landing*, or *the second wall*, or once, *the third*. Then a rate. And at the row's end there was a daub of colour, which was blue against the second wall and a pale buff against the third, and against the landing nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Is the pass that goes with the work. A person without the tier may not live above a wall. But the wall cannot do without him by day. So it lets him in by the piece. He has a slip, with a coloured edge, that says which door he is going to and how many he is; he is counted in at the gate above, with a pen, and he is counted out again before dusk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:103:"It's day-labour," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:121:Brom went along the pegs. He passed three-strand without a glance. He stopped at a coil as thick as his thumb, and took a fathom of it off the peg, and bent it double, and looked at the cut end as a person looks into a flower.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:143:He stood in the middle of that open place with a box on his shoulder. He looked at her feet, the near fore and the off, and then at the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:"He's counted bowls for a day and a night," said Lira, to nobody. "Now he's counting mules by the leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:151:"She's a day and a half north, with oats," said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:Lira was looking at them too. Her right hand hung at her side, and her left was on the becket at her shoulder, and neither of them went near the breast of her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:She looked at him as a person looks at a horse that may be going to kick, and then she shrugged with one shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:215:The first of the five gates lay a day and a half north of the fork, under the hill-foot, and Oryn came to it at dusk on the second day with the mule's shadow going long in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:229:"No. I was seventeen days late to your gate before the snow, and I'd a day there and no more; and then the cold shut the top road, and it's only now let me up it from this end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:251:"I can't mend a thing that's whole. It'd have to be parted again, where it's knit, and laid straight; and then I can knit it, and it'll be a wrist." She let him have that. She had never found a way of saying it that was easier to hear. "That's a hurt, and I'd be the one doing it, on purpose. I'll not do it tonight. I've walked a day and a half, and I don't put my hands on that sort of work tired. And I'll not do it at all unless you tell me to in the morning, when you've slept on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:267:And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:275:It took an hour. She spent as she had not spent since a cut on a moor, laying the two ends true and holding them so while the body was coaxed into beginning again; and when she sat back, the yard had gone grey at its edges and somebody was holding a cup against her teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:283:She had meant to be on the road by sunrise. It was past noon before her legs would have it. Three weeks had been five gates and a ford, with one day in hand; and she had spent the day at the first gate, on a carter. She untied the mule, and turned its head along the hill-foot, and began to walk it off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:The day, when it came, was the two hundred and eighty-third. She had the second box unroped before there was any sun, and its lid off, and she knelt on the boards of the loft with fifty-two sheets in three heaps before her: eleven, and twenty-nine, and twelve. They were her own first copies, the ones that she had drawn on her knees under a ring of stones, and on a stair, and in a drowned hall, with a lantern held low. Cael had carried them a long way. He had not seen them out of their wrapper since a cold term.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:7:"I had meant to take him the best," she said. "I lay awake over which they were: there are three from the Stair that came out clean, where the cutting was deep, and one from the Hall that I am proud of." She squared the third heap with the side of her hand. "And then I saw what I was doing, which was choosing what a man was to think before he had looked. If I show him my four best, I have shown him four things that a careful person can draw; so I shall take him all of it, with the bad sheets in their places."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:13:She had a square of oilcloth for them, and a tape; and Seln watched her tie it, from the ladder's head, with a carrying-frame on his back that he had hired for the day and a keg lashed to the frame. He had been down to the boards before the lamp was out and had come back with a card in his cap, and the card's edge was buff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:He stood at the very end of it, by the yard door, where the draught was, with his back to the room and his face to the stone. He was not tall. He had grey hair, cut short and badly, as a person cuts his own; and a coat that had been brown and was now the colour of dust, with dust worked into every seam of it, paler than any that the street could have put there. His shoulders were round; and there was a bowl in front of him, from which he was eating steadily, with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:59:So this was Cleon. The name had been said at a spring, by a small pale woman with one glove off, and asked after once; nobody had said it since. It sat on a dusty coat and a pair of shut eyes no better and no worse than a name ever sits on a stranger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:89:"Drawn: by eye, with a rule, squared to a hand's breadth. I had no paper wide enough to rub, and in two of the three places the cutting was too shallow to take wax."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:He grunted. It was a short sound, down in the chest; and Cael, who had spent three days across a table from an Archmarshal, knew approval when it was trying not to be heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:107:"Many days. The same forty-one kinds of sign are on all three, and they stand in one order." Her voice had gone thin, and she steadied it; he heard her do it. "I cannot date stone, but I can date what lies upon it. One of those floors is beneath a ring that nobody remembers raising, and one under a stair, and one under a hall that has a marsh grown over its roof; and I have laid them against every stratum of paper that I know, and they are underneath all of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:149:"That's tomorrow's. It's got the day on it. It's no good the day after, and no good this afternoon. Second bell. You go where loads go, and you give that to the man at the yard door, and you say nothing to him that he doesn't ask for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:191:He did not turn; his shoulders came up a little, and went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:195:Cael knew what he had seen. He had watched Karis do the same thing at a bridge the morning before, so short that he had nearly walked a box into her. It was what a body did when its legs had gone on with the day, and the rest of it had only then taken in what had been set before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:He had been across the yard already. He had a bowl in his hand, from the hatch, with a spoon standing in it, and he set it on the boards between her boots. Then he stood there, as he had told a healer at a fork that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:223:"*Matter*," she read, with her lips. "*Its bearer, one. Hands—*" She stopped. "There's a figure. And his name, at the top, and a rank, and a number as long as my arm. And a day." She turned it over. The back was blank. "That's all that there is. That'd take five people through the buff gate and up to the top of everything. And it's not got one of them on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:235:He did not push. He had seen her look at a blue coat from a smith's yard, the day before, with both her hands held still; and he thought that whatever she was working at had not finished with her yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:245:"There are two. Carts go to the left, by a street that runs the whole way about the crown, half a mile, to the yard's lower gate; and half way along it there is a bar, and a beam, where every load is weighed, and the weight is written against an owner." His eyes came up. "It is a book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:249:"The lane runs under the wall for two hundred paces, and there is no bar on it, since no cart could use what is at its end." He drew five short strokes, one below another, like the rungs of a ladder. "It is a cut, between two buildings, that goes down the back of the bluff to the same yard: a stair of five flights, which I made twenty-two steps. It is a stride and a half wide, and the walls on either hand are three men high and have no door in them, nor any window. Nobody stands in it. I went down it with a keg, and up again empty, and I did not meet a soul."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:263:He wrote the day by the lantern, when the pot had gone round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:273:He read the entry over, and it was thinner than what he had seen. Every reading that had ever been made of him had begun at the top, with a name, and a day, and a word, and had gone on down the sheet looking for whatever would bear the word out. At a cook-house counter he had watched a pair of sore eyes begin at the foot of a drawing, where the weight came down, and climb, and ask the paper for nothing that it had not offered. He did not know whether a person could be looked at so. He did not know whether he wished to be.

