editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:65:It was registry iron, a foot and a half long. It had a number struck into its head, thirty-two, and beneath the number a short stroke and a bar. Its point had been turned on a wheel, and it was bright.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:And under the number, where there was no need for anything at all, he had put the short stroke and the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:It was a down-stroke, from his right shoulder at hers, such as he would have given Teague's bow or the boy. She did not slip it. She brought the stave up level in both hands and took him square, on the bare wood between her two fists, and the shock of it ran up his own arm to the teeth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:183:It was a short stroke, and beneath the stroke a bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:191:"No. And it is nothing to do with size." She laid her finger on the sheet whose measure was double. "This place is twice the others, and it has the same stroke and the same bar as they have, drawn no larger and no differently. Whatever that square is for, it is not for how large a thing is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:201:She took the dead man's ledger again, and opened it at its first page, and set it down above the four sheets, so that a small pressed stroke and bar in pencil lay over four in a clerk's ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:119:Karis turned the sheet a little, and the lamp showed the stripe to the whole table. Somebody had drawn a brush across everything that followed that last stroke of the pen. The bar was as broad as a thumb and as black as a hearth-back, and it had been laid on so thickly that the ink had cracked in drying, like mud in a rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:73:He had made them a sign, since five names would not go on a scrap of bark: five short strokes side by side, like a tally with its bar left off. He drew it under the carter's own mark and read the whole aloud to an empty meadow, and then he handed it across the plank and looked at each of them twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:"I am touching it. That is a different thing, and I had better be honest about which." Her voice was small and exact in the dark. "It is cut as the bands on those stones were cut. I can find a hooked stroke with two bars across its stem under my second finger, and I cannot tell by touch whether the bars stand close or wide; so I shall not say that it is the same hand." She let her hand come down. "I have a wall here that I would give a year of my life to read. It is against my spine. My paper is in a box at a stone, and there is a finger of oil in that lamp, and the oil is for his shoulder if it turns. I have gone over that three times, to see whether I could make it come out otherwise. It will not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:He had known that there would not be. He could see the iron itself, a dark stroke above the frosted turf, and the grey stone beyond it; and the air between that bar and its fellow was as empty as any air. He took a stride, and another, and let Anchor look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:17:Brom set the kit on the table. Its case lay there in tarred canvas, with the strap coiled on the lid; inside were a cord knotted at the fives, stake thirty-two with the stroke and the bar and a bright turned point, and a mallet. The stake had never been driven. Beside it Karis put a dead man's ledger, shut. The three boxes stood against the warm wall, and each of them had one leaf in it that was the same as its fellows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:103:Pike looked at five hands, and looked again. He drew the paper back under his stone and wrote beneath the struck name: five short strokes, side by side, like a tally with its bar left off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:"That is the only place where one of his roads walks round a blank. I said so on the fifty-eighth day, and it is still so. But he draws a hill with short hatching, and water with three wavy strokes. Look here, south of the ring with the bar, a day down and a little aside. He has hatched hills all across this corner, close as thatch, and in the middle of the hatching there is a round place the size of my thumbnail where he lifted the stick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:It was the third time he had been asked, in three different ways, and the first time the answer seemed to be put away somewhere for later. She took the slip from the carter's hat, where he had left it on the shaft. She read Pike's hand, slowly, and the five strokes under it, and then she fished a stub of charred stick out of her apron and made her mark on the bark, pressing hard. It was a thorn twig, three spines to a side, with a wavy line under it for water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:203:It had been drawn in oak-gall ink and gone over, here and there, with a burnt stick. There were roads on it, each a double line, as a wheel sees a road. Three wavy strokes stood for a ford and a square with a dot in it for a hold. A ring with a bar through it lay on a river in the west, and Cael knew the ferry. Between one square and the next ran rows of small ticks, and he had counted the ticks from the ring to a square at the head of a valley before he understood that they were days. Roads went off the hide's edge to the north, and the south, and the east. Wherever a wheel had no business, he had put down nothing at all, and the skin was bare.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"That's been on my oak since the thaw," he said. "It's the carters' well, at the fork, where the south road splits. It went sour. A lad was sent down after a bucket, and they wound him up asleep; he woke, and he's not been right about a ladder since." He drew five strokes on the bark. "Nobody's been down it; they water at a stream a mile on, and curse me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:He came back with four coins in a sock and a boy behind him carrying a sack on his shoulder. The coins he counted into Brom's hand. The sack, which was flour, and a good twenty pounds of it, the boy let down at Brom's feet as though that were the natural place for anything heavy. The old man scratched his mark on the slip with a nail, three upright strokes and a bar across them, and gave it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:37:"I do not know that these are letters. I said so on the floor, and nothing has altered. But the strokes stand one after another, with gaps between, and a thing set in groups can be counted in groups." She laid her finger under a hooked stroke with two short bars across its stem. "That. I want to know whether that is on any other strip. If it is on four, it is a kind. If it is on this one alone, it is a scratch, and I shall say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:71:He had brought fleece down from the high fold. Cael remembered the slip, for it had hung on the plank the day they came: eleven bales, at a mark the bale, delivered dry. The big man laid it down with a slap. Somebody at the landing had put a row of strokes on it for a witness, eleven of them, and through the last a short bar had been drawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:141:It was the same pattern as the two at the stone, and it was clean, since no rust had come to it in that dry cold. Under the beaten head there was the registry's stamp; and under the stamp, punched deep, were two figures, and beneath the figures a short upright stroke with a bar across its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:207:He had not raised his voice, but Cael came and knelt beside him. Under the two punched figures, on the newer iron only, there was a mark that the old bar did not have. It was small and deep, and made with two blows of a different punch: a short upright stroke, and under it a bar laid across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:275:*Hundred and thirteenth day, night. At the edge of the second one. A stone, and two bars, both numbered thirty-two; one five cold terms old, one a cold term or two, with a mark under its number that Seln won't read. A floor with no stones on it, and a hole, and a stair. Nobody on the ruts.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:"There. Don't touch it. It goes out to the left in a curve, and comes back to its own line a little farther on, as a path goes round a pond; but he's drawn no pond. He draws water as three wavy strokes, and a hill with short hatching, and he's put neither inside the bend. There's nothing in it at all but bare skin."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"Thirty-four," he said. "And beneath the number, the stroke and the bar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-53.md:113:Karis laid the brass along the hide once more, with all of them looking, and it ran in among the water-strokes as it had run on the lid of a box. The bare round that she had ringed lay a finger to one side of it.

