editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:125:"The first of it's sound. It's the word you had on the face, for the mark." He did not say it as she would have said it; he said it as a man names a beam. "That's the bearer, and there's only the one. Two runs hang from it." The reed moved. "One there, of five. One there, of six, and that's the one in the thin. And after the pair of them, his number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:221:He wrote slowly. *Withdrawn by its bearer*, said the long column; and the narrow one said *four*. He blotted both. Then, with the pen still lifted, he ruled a line beneath the whole of it, across both columns, as a man rules off an account that has come out even.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:"It stands four times on that floor, always behind a bearer. I uncovered it on my ninth day under the town, and it was not the word that I had wished for. It was only *set*, as a stone is set, or a stake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:7:They carried them poorly: the big one had his on the right shoulder, which was the bound one, and neither of his arms came up above the elbow when he shifted it. The small pale one walked with her left hand laid flat against her coat. The girl had both her arms, which was new since the thaw; she used the left as a person uses a tool that has just come back from the smith. The boy with the book was sound, and came last but one, as he always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:There was no frost to speak of; the thaw was three weeks old, and the joint had not talked since the board came off. He brought it all the same, wrung out of the kettle, on his two palms, as he had on every night since he joined. Teague held out the arm; he had tried to tell him once that it was not wanted, and had seen the boy's face, and had let it be wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:151:It was the season's line, with its ruled space at the end. Cael had seen that space left blank on purpose at first snow, and carried blank over a thaw onto a new cloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:165:"I said after the thaw. It's three weeks gone. I said whatever's on that bearing, and I've just heard him write the word." His eyes went along them, and they did not stop at five; they went on to the door-post, where a stick was grounded. "I'll say it here, where it's heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:Cael saw it from the bench under the great ash as soon as there was light to see by, on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, when the meadow was grey and forty fires were being blown up out of their ashes. It was a salt cart, with a pair of mules, and it had been drawn onto the grass at dusk by a carter who did not know how soft three weeks of thaw had left that corner. It stood now with its near wheels sunk to the axle. Half its sacks lay on the turf beside it. The carter had the mules out and was standing with his cap in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:55:There was no glass. A fortnight and more of thaw had come up that hill since a stick cracked it, and the seep ran down the middle of the road now as a seep ought, a stride wide and a finger deep, talking over the stones. The lean man had his near cob by the cheek-strap. He walked it down on the wet with its head held, and the other followed, as beasts had been walked down that place all through a cold term by people who had the nerve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:235:"I know the walk. She lays tight." The ferryman got up. He looked along his own hauling rope, where it came in over the sheave from the oak post, grey and fretted; and Cael remembered that two yarns had been gone from it since a flood. "I've crossed on that since the thaw, forty times, with my heart in my mouth and a turn of old line for a stern strap. I lent you a rope before the snow. I said what I wanted back, and I never looked to have it."

