editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:81:"I don't know. I've never gone so far into anybody who wasn't dying." She looked down at him. "May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:181:She came out of him at the four hundred and ninetieth breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:197:"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:211:It was not the first thing that he had kept back. He had kept a date from everybody, at a spring, and a warning. But he had never before kept a thing from the person whose hands had drawn it out, with those hands hardly cold upon him; and he found that it had a weight which the others had not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:[[TIER-B: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf …" / "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." — elided in three places; the plan says only that Lira offers the leaf and that Oryn does not hear it. Staged in the lean-to after the finding: Lira reminds Oryn that she asked at the fork for whatever walked out of the archive, all of it, and says that there is a paper in Cael's coat which did; Oryn asks whether it is a finding; Lira answers; Oryn declines to have it read at her table, and restates her term. The fair copy stays in Cael's coat throughout and is not unfolded. Oryn's last line may belong here or to the first scene of this chapter, where her term is given in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:89:"You drafted an order at a spring," she had said. "Each row to be taken up singly; a schedule to be made of every sheet as it is turned; and the schedule to be signed by two hands. I told you that it was quite a good order, and that I had nobody to sign the second half." She had held out the leaf. "I have somebody now. I should like them counted into their boxes by a person who has done it before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:"Eleven," said Vastin, and made his stroke. "I have eleven. The box may be shut." He turned his head a little, toward the big man with the lid. "I should be obliged if it were said back to me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:99:Cael heard the pencil go down again, and he did not turn round. He had seen that man stopped at a spring in the middle of an order, by nothing more than a small grey person's eyes. It seemed to him a large thing, and one that nobody in the gallery was going to remark upon, that the same order had now been asked for; and it was being carried out to the letter, with a stub of pencil on a kit's case, by the man who had not been let to give it.
