editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:"He may be either. A hold's man does not ride a mile behind a dray for nine miles when he could be past it in a quarter of an hour. A lad sent for a boat does not stop when a file stops." Seln looked down the fold. "I do not say that it is the same man. I say that I told you a road would be watched, and that somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher, and I take no comfort from how low I must put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:235:"I know the walk. She lays tight." The ferryman got up. He looked along his own hauling rope, where it came in over the sheave from the oak post, grey and fretted; and Cael remembered that two yarns had been gone from it since a flood. "I've crossed on that since the thaw, forty times, with my heart in my mouth and a turn of old line for a stern strap. I lent you a rope before the snow. I said what I wanted back, and I never looked to have it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:241:She had not meant to, and Cael saw her check it. There had been a slab at that spring, twelve feet long and waist-high, lying level on its own rubble in the lee of the rock. She had called it a table. She had laid six years of an office's paper out upon it under river pebbles, and nothing had stirred but a corner now and then by the width of a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:Cael knelt at the basin's lip, upwind, and dipped the pot. The water was as cold as a knife. Neither of them said one word. He did not know what that man had made of a date, and he had never asked. It came to him, on his knees in the bents, that he was not the only person at this spring who kept a thing out of his book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:25:"It stands four times on that floor, always behind a bearer. I uncovered it on my ninth day under the town, and it was not the word that I had wished for. It was only *set*, as a stone is set, or a stake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:He had been stood on a plate once; and something had been written about him at the head of a sheet, in a place that a printer had ruled for it, and he had carried it ever since. He had known at a spring that a hole in the ground could be filed in the same fashion. He had not known until this morning that the two filings were one word, in one line, on the back of a skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:57:"There is a stroke with a bar. That is on the stake, and in the book, and on every sheet of the four places that I matched to the hide." She drew it in the middle box, small. "I told you at the spring that there were other sheets, under other signs, and that I would not call them places. I turned their corners down, and I did not tell you everything that I had seen on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"It is three marks. Call them what you like; I must call them something, and I shall call them so." Karis took the pencil up. "But a grade grades something. The one that is given at a kindling grades a person, and there is a man in this room who signed for a good many." "This one is not for size, as I showed you at the spring; a place of twice the measure has the same bar. It is not for which is fenced. So I have gone through everything else in which one of these sheets differs from another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:There was a number there, small, after a clerk's flourish; Cael had seen it at the spring, on every sheet, and had taken no more notice of it than of the printer's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:119:"I was coming to that," said Karis. She did not seem put out. "The sheets with two bars have one line that the others lack, above their eight stations. It gives the four signs of the place behind the iron, and then a point of the compass, and then a measure in lengths. I saw that at the spring as well, and took it for a fence's survey tied to its own floor." Her pencil tapped the line. "It is a run, from the fourth place to this one. An office has written down how far it is from the one to the other, and which way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:[[TIER-B: Teague: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins Teague. Staged in the doorway of the back room, with his eyes on the hide and the divider's point, to nobody in particular and then to Cael. Whatever stands between the two parts may be his or another's (a question from Cael, or Karis's assent); the scene ends on the second part — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:237:Three of the stakes were gone from the weir. Cael saw the gap as they came round the shoulder, and saw a captain see it; that had been a slip on Pike's plank once, at four marks, and it had been forfeited for a walk along a ridge. The tall grey building stood beyond, with its wheel going round in a white fall. There was the fence of split posts, and the dozen roofs, and the ford below with a flat stone on either bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:295:"Shoulder," said Oryn. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:323:"Arm," she said next. "The left. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:337:"Hand. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:367:"Sit on the plank. Leg out. I want my hands on that knee, and on your wrist. May I?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:[[TIER-B: "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*." … "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is." — elided between its halves, and the plan pins no speaker (the packet says only that Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line). Staged as Karis's, at the table, with stake thirty-two, the dead surveyor's first page and the Stair's abstract laid together under the window, to the six and Vastin. The first half can stand here. The second half ("how old") is earned on the page only in the next scene, by the running numbers; if the unit must stand whole in one place, it can be moved down to follow "Two hundred and six." without touching either scene — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:"I thought that you had; I saw the square, and I shut my mouth on it. A second mark, on sheets that I would not so much as call a place, was the first step of a theory; and I had six people round a rock who would have taken the second step for me." She had drawn the sheets out while she spoke, from under six turned corners, one from each row. "They are one group: the same four signs on all six, one sheet to a year. And this is what their square holds."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:75:"It is three marks. Call them what you like; I must call them something, and I shall call them so." Karis took the pencil up. "But a grade grades something. The one that is given at a kindling grades a person, and there is a man in this room who signed for a good many." "This one is not for size, as I showed you at the spring; a place of twice the measure has the same bar. It is not for which is fenced. So I have gone through everything else in which one of these sheets differs from another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:"It is a series number," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:107:"A series that was opened before the present manner of numbering is carried under its old style," said Vastin. "I should expect it, and I do not know when the manner changed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:141:"A surveyor's length I know to the stride. I walked behind that cord for a whole cold term, and I know what a day of ours comes to in it." Her lips moved over the office's figure. "This run is three such days, less a little." She closed the legs by the width of a hair; then she lifted one point, and swung it round, along the brass, as a person walks a pair of compasses across a chart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:[[TIER-B: "Somebody laid them down in order" — a fragment (no stop given), and the plan pins no speaker. Staged at the table, with the four running numbers read along the hide from west to east and the divider's point standing past the fourth ring: the first thing said aloud once all five are in view. Brom could own it, pricing what he sees; so could Lira, or Cael; Karis could own it only as a thing that the paper shows. The next lines follow from any of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:161:[[TIER-B: "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker. Staged as Karis's, at the table, to Lira's "Who?" and to Vastin's drawn breath, with her bound hand flat on the sheet of three boxes. If it is another's (Cael's, said for her), the lines on either side still stand — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:[[TIER-B: Teague: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins Teague. Staged in the doorway of the back room, with his eyes on the hide and the divider's point, to nobody in particular and then to Cael. Whatever stands between the two parts may be his or another's (a question from Cael, or Karis's assent); the scene ends on the second part — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:183:It held. Cael had not been sure that it would, outside a meadow. A quiet man went ahead, small against the pale grass, and after him seven feet of ash with a leaf of steel on it. Then came a pair of shoulders that did not look round; and Cael walked a stride behind the left of those, where he had been put. He had never in his life walked at anybody's shoulder to be asked what he saw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:189:That had been settled in the yard, and it was the first thing that the field had cost anybody. The salt cart that had been put out of the mud was going down to the mill for meal, light, behind its two mules; and its carter had said to Teague, cap in hand, that a man's arm was owed something. Teague had looked at an old man with a slat on his leg, and at the length of a ridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:193:The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have had to wait for him, and had shut it, and gone to the tail-board. He had offered his copper, and the carter would not have it; he said that it was the captain's fare, and paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:205:There was a flat rock there. Teague lifted his chin at it, and the file broke, and sat, and the old woman undid a strap of leather and looked at a wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:211:*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:243:"Eleven off the ridge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:255:She was thinner. That was the first thing, and Cael had not expected it; three weeks of five gates had come off her as a cold term comes off a horse. She stopped at her stride and a half and looked the eleven of them over, and it took her no longer than it had in a cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:265:It was a stride to his left, by the lean-to's post, where the light was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:271:[[TIER-B: "Nineteen days." / "Then it's eleven more." — the two lines are whole, but the plan pins neither speaker. Staged at the lean-to's post, with Vastin standing where he has just been ordered to stand and Oryn looking at the slat from her stride and a half; one of the two gives the count and the other the remainder. Vastin could own the first (he has called his own day every morning since the cut) and Oryn the second; or the other way about. Nothing after this depends on which — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:281:[[TIER-B: Oryn's terms restated — the packet gives only two fragments, "three holds owed" and "a stop on my own route"; its sense is given too (she comes east as a healer on her own round, and is on nobody's count). Staged as Oryn's, at the fence, in answer to Teague, with the six and Teague's four in hearing. Her own sentences are not written here — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:287:[[TIER-B: "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." — the second line is Oryn's (Tier A 25 lists it with her Millrace lines); the plan does not pin the speaker of the first. Staged at the mill's bench at dusk, with Brom's bowls laid along it and Oryn's own bowl set at its end before an upturned keg. Lira could own "Sixth chair." (she told Oryn at this mill that there was a bowl); so could Brom, or Cael. Oryn answers, and sits — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:291:It was a keg, and not a chair. She lowered herself onto it as she did everything, squarely, and took up the bowl; and Cael, who had watched that woman eat standing for as long as he had known her, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, found that he had to look at the weir for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:329:[[TIER-B: "You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced" — the third line is given without its stop, and no speaker is pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the forearm: Oryn reading what the arm has done since Thornwater, Lira answering in one word, Oryn's reply. That order of speakers is the natural one and is not the plan's — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:375:[[TIER-B: "It held in a cut" — a fragment, with no speaker pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the knee and her eyes shut, after Vastin has told her of the wall on the eleventh day. Oryn could own it (reading the joint: what it bore on the road and what it was then asked to bear in a stair); Vastin could own it, of the one time he stood on it with the stick up, two days ago; Cael could, speaking for him. Her verdict follows in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:379:[[TIER-B: "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his." — four lines, none pinned to a speaker. Staged on the plank as the close of the knee's reading: one of them answers her verdict with the word; she remarks on it; it is defended; and its owner is named. Vastin could own the first and third, Oryn the second, and Vastin or Lira the fourth (with a look toward Cael, who stands at the lean-to's post); the scene ends on it — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:It was the factor. There was the leather apron, and the slate on its cord, and the pair of pale eyes that had gone along six people and a healer at a gate and counted them. They counted now. They went from a quiet man, who had come up level with the cobs, to four with loads; and they rested last upon the tail-board, where a pair of raw hands lay on an ash stick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot." The crook's foot came round, without haste, and pointed at the tail-board. "And for him, a seat at that fire for the whole of a cold term, every year that he cares to come, with nothing asked of him but to be within a shout of my road. I'll not say what for. I was on the lip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:"How long the thing stood, that the eight ran into. And whether the old man lifted a foot while it did." The pale eyes went to the tail-board, and stayed. "I told him what I saw. It stood for as long as he never moved; and he never moved. I said that the man who made it called himself a fool for the place he'd made it in. He didn't seem to take the fool's word for that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:55:There was no glass. A fortnight and more of thaw had come up that hill since a stick cracked it, and the seep ran down the middle of the road now as a seep ought, a stride wide and a finger deep, talking over the stones. The lean man had his near cob by the cheek-strap. He walked it down on the wet with its head held, and the other followed, as beasts had been walked down that place all through a cold term by people who had the nerve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:67:Vastin had got down at the head, as he had undertaken to do for every slope. He was coming down the dry edge last of all, a tread at a time, with the bound leg swung out stiff; and forty strides from the top he was standing still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:71:[[TIER-B: "Here." — the packet gives the one word and pins no speaker. Staged at the place where the line was struck on the 279th, forty strides below the cut's head and three above Brom's flat stone, with the file halted on the dry edge and nothing on the ground to mark it. Vastin could own it (he has stopped there of himself and is looking at the road); Cael could own it (he is the nearest to him and is the only other who stood at that line). Nobody else is within four strides — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:73:It was the place. Cael knew it by Brom's stone, three strides below, and by the lie of the two banks; there was no other sign of it in the world. A plane had stood on that spot from one bank to the other, as high as the scree, and a man had been the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:[[TIER-B: "Two words," … "Both of them ours." — elided, and the plan pins no speaker. Staged as the next thing said on the dry edge after the stick comes down, low, as the road allows at a halt; Cael's "Noted." follows it and answers it. Vastin could own it, speaking of what this road has taught him to say; so could Brom, who has turned on his stone three strides below and is looking up at the two of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:101:It was a stride farther down, level with the middle of the road, where the water ran now. Brom had gone by his own flat stone without a look, since he was not a person who visited places. But Cael had stood in that road on the two hundred and seventy-ninth day, behind the middle of an invisible thing, with his face two feet from it. He could not have lifted a hand there, while eight broke on what was in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:125:The fist opened, and lay flat. They stood. A carter stood at his cob's head as a carter does, and an old man on a tail-board looked at his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:127:Nothing came of it. It was a rider on a road, on a morning when carts were using that road again, behind six people who had no quarrel with any horse. After the space of forty breaths Seln lifted two fingers, and the file went on round the foot of the spur. But the small grey man had let it come up to him, which he never did; and as Cael went by he saw the right hand go out at the hip and turn over, palm upward, not empty but asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:"I am aware of it. I have been got up by one or another of you a good many times since a stair, and I have not yet been charged for it." He laid the stick across his thighs. "I am told that a road has no keeper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:"He may be either. A hold's man does not ride a mile behind a dray for nine miles when he could be past it in a quarter of an hour. A lad sent for a boat does not stop when a file stops." Seln looked down the fold. "I do not say that it is the same man. I say that I told you a road would be watched, and that somebody is on it who behaves as a watcher would. I can put it no higher, and I take no comfort from how low I must put it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"How many. Which way. That one of you rides, and five carry, and that there is rope on the cart. That you stop at a cairn and do not go up to a gate." He was silent a moment. "It is very little, and it is exact. I have sold less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:219:Vastin got down off the tail-board for the last time, and paid his third copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:245:"He has not come down to the landing," he said. "There is a horse tied in the alders, above the last bend. It has no rider on it. I do not know where the rider is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:39:"He's taken it hard, whatever you said. That's forty coils that I'd not have laid." She jerked her chin at the lean man. "It's his road, and you'll ask him. But I'd think that a coil could ride with its own kind, to the place where it's wanted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"There's a tail-board. It's a copper a day to sit on, and you'll get down for every hill; I'll not have my cobs pull what can walk. And you'll hold your tongue on the moor. I'll not need to tell you why."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The keeper in undyed grey sat on his high stool with the frame on his knee. He looked at feet, and wheels, and hooves, as he had looked at them thirteen days before, and his thumb moved the beads: eight hooves, and four wheels. He came to the boots, and Cael counted five pairs with him. The thumb went on to the tail-board, where a pair of boots hung that did not touch the ground, and it stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:109:It came to him quite plainly, between one stride and the next. A healer had made him write a thing in a book at a fork-stone: that inside Treswick he did not go near the station. He had kept it for thirteen days, to the letter, down a lane with four white alleys in it, and through a night when a thread of light on a loft's boards had not been steady. And the gate was behind him. The river was the Line, and he was over the river. The promise had been for inside, and he was out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:127:It was a town's ground. A person might speak on it, as far as the first hedge, and for a long while nobody did. The dray set the pace, which was a stick's pace or a little better. The lean man walked at his near cob's head, and the six came after his wheels in the order that they had learned, with a quiet man gone on ahead into the white. Cael walked last, behind the tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:155:"You have very little. I sat opposite it for three days once, with a plate, and I came away with nothing. But I have had a fortnight of it now from the side, which is another matter." The grey eyes had come down from the hill. "I am not asking. I said in a loft that I know no more of what you are than the plate did, and that I do not ask; and I have not found it harder to keep than a good many orders that I was given. I say only that I have noticed that I am not told. I enter it without complaint. I kept six people's worth of such things for a living."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:207:He turned his back on the plain, and held up the hand for the tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:221:He kept the file, behind two brown boots that hung from a tail-board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:257:He had turned his cobs out on the green by the overflow. He took the seventh bowl from Brom as a thing that the road owed him, and he had it empty before the old man had begun; and then he lay down under his own tail-board with his cap over his eyes. Nobody lit anything. It was a furlong off the road, and a person might have spoken there. Nobody did that either, beyond what the bowls wanted. They had six miles of wind in their ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:He had written none of it. He had said none of it, to a healer or a girl or a man with a lamp. In the fifteen days since, a leaf had been read aloud in a gallery which made a good many things look different, and he had not once let himself carry that sheet across to stand beside it. That was for the clean page, if it was for any; and a woman had laid that page down bare, with a bound hand, and had said that nobody was to rule a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:*Two hundred and ninety-fifth day. The spring, a furlong south of the border road; nineteen miles and some from the gate. Out at first light by the outer arch: six out, as six in, and none of us written. Sixty yards of four-strand, two marks. A hundred and twenty-eight. The coil rides on the rope-walk's dray, with forty of its kind for the landing. So does Vastin, for a copper a day of his own; he walks the hills. Sixteen of thirty. Lira: thirty. She says that it is an arm.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:171:"Something turned over," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:[[TIER-B: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf …" / "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." — elided in three places; the plan says only that Lira offers the leaf and that Oryn does not hear it. Staged in the lean-to after the finding: Lira reminds Oryn that she asked at the fork for whatever walked out of the archive, all of it, and says that there is a paper in Cael's coat which did; Oryn asks whether it is a finding; Lira answers; Oryn declines to have it read at her table, and restates her term. The fair copy stays in Cael's coat throughout and is not unfolded. Oryn's last line may belong here or to the first scene of this chapter, where her term is given in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:247:"You went stiff, on the plank, somewhere past the middle of it. She didn't see; she'd her eyes shut. I hadn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:317:"I am obliged." Vastin considered the far bank, and the track that climbed away from it toward a notch. "I am informed that I am to walk a stride behind a man who is slow to answer. I find it the first posting in some years which I understand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:339:Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:37:The scow's bow touched. He watched six people go up the bank toward the lower inn, with a stick behind them, and saw that the stick was no longer a dozen strides back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:"So it's pride."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:There was no frost to speak of; the thaw was three weeks old, and the joint had not talked since the board came off. He brought it all the same, wrung out of the kettle, on his two palms, as he had on every night since he joined. Teague held out the arm; he had tried to tell him once that it was not wanted, and had seen the boy's face, and had let it be wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:Every crew that had lain up for the cold was there, and a good many carters. The hide-woman had a stool by the hearth, and the three grey men had their board on a keg and were not throwing. It was the hour at which a one-armed man read out what the day had brought to his plank; and the room had gone quiet for it, as it had on a night of first snow, when a crew came in with white on its shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:"*Clearance; the cut below the shoulder hold. Eight. Twenty; paid.* That's twelve lines." The stone went back on the leaf. "And then you never came home. You left word with me: the cut, and whatever lay beyond. A crew's been gone eighteen days past its slip, and I've a line to rule for it, and I don't know what to put. Where's beyond?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:153:"I said that I'd leave that until I'd a column for it," said Pike. "I've not found the column. I've had a hundred and sixty-eight days to look." He dipped. "A blank's a kind of entry, so long as a man knows why it's there. But a blank with nothing against it can't be told from one that was forgotten. So I'll put a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:157:"That's when I left off looking," he said. "It's not a number. It says that on this day the keeper of this book knew what he didn't know, and signed for it." He blotted the place with the heel of his hand. "Eleventh. And dated."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:163:He did not hurry; he stopped a stride from the bench's end, with his thumbs in his belt over the hand-axe, as he had stopped at that hearth on a night of snow; and the room, which had begun to talk, left off again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:165:"I said after the thaw. It's three weeks gone. I said whatever's on that bearing, and I've just heard him write the word." His eyes went along them, and they did not stop at five; they went on to the door-post, where a stick was grounded. "I'll say it here, where it's heard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:167:[[TIER-B: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins the term on Teague and gives the heads (ten on a wall's count; shares by count; the field Teague's; the reading Karis's) without their wording. Staged as Teague's, said to Cael at the bench's end before the whole room, with his eyes having just gone to Vastin at the door-post. The four heads follow below in my own words, as his, so that the restored unit may stand before them or round them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:[[TIER-B: "The wall's still the wall." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker. Staged as the answer to Teague's term, at the bench's end, with the talk having just come round to Vastin's wall. It could be Cael's (the contract does not make the wall the field's to place); it could be Teague's own (whatever the thing is called, a wall is what the count is built on); it could be Vastin's, from the door-post. The lines after it are written to follow any of the three — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:Cael saw it from the bench under the great ash as soon as there was light to see by, on the two hundred and ninety-eighth day, when the meadow was grey and forty fires were being blown up out of their ashes. It was a salt cart, with a pair of mules, and it had been drawn onto the grass at dusk by a carter who did not know how soft three weeks of thaw had left that corner. It stood now with its near wheels sunk to the axle. Half its sacks lay on the turf beside it. The carter had the mules out and was standing with his cap in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:They were too far off to be heard: twenty strides, across the square of the old silent yard, where the pegs still stood. Brom sat at Cael's side with his arms hanging. They watched a captain look at a cart for some time, as he looked at any load, and then at a lean old woman who had come down the bank with her board under her arm. She said one thing to him, and he answered it; and she shut her mouth, and stood back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:237:Something came back. Cael saw it arrive: the straight arm jarred, from the palm to the shoulder, like a pole that has been struck on its end. And he heard the wrist, at twenty strides, across a meadow: one small dry click, such as a latch makes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:[[TIER-B: "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker; it is listed under the contract with the word "Force". Staged in the meadow a breath after the one use, with the arm already in the young one's cloth, when the carter points to a second cart sunk twenty yards off: as Teague's answer, and loud enough to carry to the bench. Teague could own it (to the carter, or across the yard to Cael, who has watched); the lines that follow do not depend on which — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:251:Nothing had come to him, and he had not looked for it. He was twenty strides off, on a bench, and nobody in that meadow had meant anything at anybody; a man had put his hand to a cart. But he had seen a line drawn through a body, plainer than he had ever seen one drawn on a hide, and he knew better than to trust it to his memory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:253:*Seen, at first light, the meadow, from twenty strides. Teague's is Force. He set a heel against a root and a palm against a cart's tail, and stood until every joint between the two lay straight. Then it went: one push, the length of a body, and no more of it after. It wants firm ground behind and a thing in front. What comes back comes down the same road and is paid where it left him. The wrist. I heard it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:283:"The quiet one's out front, where he's always been. I'll not move him. I couldn't find him to move." It was said without a smile, and Seln, at the north-west peg, inclined his head by a hair. "Then my spear. Then me; and the one with the book at my left hand, a stride back, since he sees a thing before he knows that he's seen it, and I want to be told. Then the girl, and the boy behind her. He'll watch her feet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:287:"Then she'll be told. In the middle, with whoever's healing, and a beast when there's a beast. Then the big one, since he's slow to answer and hard to hurt." His eyes came to the end of the row, where an old man stood on a stick. "Then you. A stride behind him. I'm told that's where you've stood."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:301:It was a small thing. Cael, at a captain's left shoulder, a stride back, felt it happen behind him, as a person feels a door shut somewhere in his own house: nine people stopped on one flat hand, and there was not a sound in that corner of the meadow but the fires. He glanced once to his right, and Teague had not turned his head. But the chin had come up, by the width of a finger, at nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:85:The fifty-two sheets had lain there for eleven days in three heaps, as they had lain upon three floors. They were to go up at the bell, and a clerk was to write that they were gone. Lira lifted them one at a time, flat on her two forearms, the slow arm and the quick, while Brom held each box's lid. On the third box, six strides from the desk, sat the old man, with his bound leg along a plank. He had the tarred case for a desk, and a leaf of Karis's notebook upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:131:On a box, six strides off, a pencil had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"There was nobody on the kerb, and nobody in the smith's door. I have been down to the quay and looked at the bridge from below. It is one cart wide." He walked a few strides. "A person who wished to see us leave this town need not follow us about it. He need only stand at one end of that."

