   307	Brom lifted the boxes himself. He did it one at a time, slowly, with both hands, to the height of that brown back, and he looked at his own two arms after each as a man looks at a pair of tools that he had given up for lost. The mule stood for it with her ears back. When the third was on, with the flat strapped over all and a sack of the mill's meal for a counterweight, he laid his palm on her neck for the space of a breath; and it was the first time in twelve days that he had been able to reach it.
   308	
   309	"That's a healer's road and a researcher's whole archive on one beast," he said. "I'd not care to be the mule."
   310	
   311	"She's had worse company," said Oryn.
   312	
   313	"Twenty," said the old man, to nobody.
   314	
   315	"Of thirty," said Cael.
   316	
   317	"I am obliged." Vastin considered the far bank, and the track that climbed away from it toward a notch. "I am informed that I am to walk a stride behind a man who is slow to answer. I find it the first posting in some years which I understand."
   318	
   319	Karis had not crossed.
   320	
   321	She was on the near bank, kneeling at the flat stone that the wheels aimed for, with the calfskin spread on it under four pebbles; and she had pinned a clean sheet to the skin's eastern edge with two thorns out of the hedge. The Ternhall brass lay along the pencil line, through four rings, and ran on over the join.
   322	
   323	"I have made the office's figure again," she said, when he came back through the water to her. "By daylight, with nobody talking, and it is what it was." She held out her right hand. "I shall want them once more."
   324	
   325	He gave her the brass legs, shut, with the cork on. She opened them upon the hall's ring and the fourth, as she had done at a table; she closed them by a hair, and swung one point round along the strip, and it came down on the clean paper. She pressed, and it left a prick in the sheet no bigger than a grain of sand.
   326	
   327	Then she took the pencil in her left hand, the bound one, in its new linen, and drew.
   328	
   329	She began in the middle of the fourth ring, where the carter's skin ended, and the line went over the join of hide and paper without a tremor. It ran along the brass's edge to the prick, and it did not stop there. She carried it on, a hand's breadth beyond, to where the sheet gave out, and lifted the pencil only at the paper's edge.
   330	
   331	"You've gone past it," said Cael.
   332	
   333	"I do not know that it ends at the point. I know that an office measured as far as that, and wrote a figure. I am not going to draw a stop that the paper does not give me." She blew the lead off the sheet. "It is a line. It goes where it goes."
   334	
   335	She gave him back the dividers, and he put them into the book.
   336	
   337	They went up out of the green valley in the file that had been set under an ash, with a mule and two healers in the middle of it. At the notch's head the ruts divided. Teague did not look along the pair that went back to a plank and a cloth. He lifted two fingers, and the tall man turned his spear the other way.
   338	
   339	Cael walked at a captain's shoulder, a stride back.
   340	
   341	He had a healer's finding in him, word for word, and it was whole; he had two words of his own behind his eyes, which did not agree with it, and which he had told to nobody. He had a sentence from under a town that had begun to lean. Ahead of him a quiet man went small into the pale grass, along a line that a woman had ruled past the place where the figures stopped.
   342	
   343	It ran east, off the edge of everything that she had. He went where it went.

