    57	Brom's shoulder. She put the flat hand on it, as she had wanted to at a marsh and had been told *binding, not hands* by a woman with a cloth bag, and she held it there, and came out of it, and said, "She was right. It wanted leaving alone and it was left alone and it's a shoulder. Carry with the other one another month. You will anyway." Brom said, "Aye," which was his whole speech on the subject of his shoulder for the year.
    58	
    59	Lira's arm. Two fingers on the wrist, the flat hand on the forearm, the count: "Fifteen days at the fork. It's had thirty-three. It's an arm." She looked at Lira's two hands, both at her sides and neither in her coat. "You listened when it told you the second time."
    60	
    61	"Once," Lira said.
    62	
    63	"Once is once more than I priced." That was all of it, and Lira took it as praise, because from Oryn it was.
    64	
    65	Karis's hand. The bound one, the cut across the back of the left hand from a step's edge in a cut, eight days old, bound by Brom with thread and a strip of shirt and his teeth, and Oryn unbound it and looked at it a long time in the going light and said, "Who sewed this."
    66	
    67	"Brom."
    68	
    69	"With what."
    70	
    71	"Thread. His teeth for the knot."
    72	
    73	Oryn looked at Brom, once, and said nothing, and rebound the hand with her own linen, tighter and flatter, and said to Karis, "It'll close. It'll leave a line. Don't write with it for a week — the other hand. You've got one." And Karis, who had been writing with it for eight days and would not stop for a week, said "Noted," which from Karis was not agreement, and Oryn heard that it was not and let it go, because a healer picks the orders she enforces.
    74	
    75	Vastin's knee. He came to the table with the stick and she said, "Sit," and he sat, and she put both hands on the knee through the trouser and then said, "Trouser," and he rolled it, and the knee was a knee with a binding on it that had been put on at a cut nineteen days ago by the hands that were on it now, and she read it. It took longer than the others. He watched her face, because her face did the reading's work in the register her voice did not, and it did not change, and then it did, a little, at the corner, and she took her hands off.
    76	
    77	"It held in a cut," she said.
    78	
    79	Vastin did not say anything.
    80	
    81	"You stood on it. With the stick off the ground. You've done it once, and it was in a cut, and you did it because a cut was where it went and you wanted to stand on the place." She did not ask him. She had read it. "It held. That's not the same as the month being up. The month's the month. Eleven more days on the stick and then I'll say, and if you put it down before I say I'll know, because I'll read it, and it'll tell me."
    82	
    83	"Noted," Vastin said, and she looked at him. "That's new."
    84	
    85	"It's a useful word."
    86	
    87	"It's his." She tilted her head at the bank, at Cael, and rebound the knee, and it was done, and the man with the stick went back to standing where he had been told to stand, and stood.
    88	
    89	Then she wiped her hands, and looked at the pan, and did not drink from it, and said, to the ford, "Cael. Bench. Lie still when I say."
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	He lay still.
    94	
    95	He had lain still for nobody, in five years, except her, and for her three times, on a sack and on a table and at a marsh's edge, and each of those had been a minute at her count, and this was not going to be a minute, and he knew it before she put her hands on him, because she had said *the long one* at a fork and had meant it, and because she had carried this reading on her route since the autumn and was going to have it whole.
    96	
    97	The bench was the lean-to's, against the back wall, under the turf, out of the ford's noise. He lay on it on his back with the log on the ground beside his hand where he could reach it and would not, and his coat open, and the straightedge and the dividers taken out of it and put on the plank table in her order, because she had said *nothing in the coat* and had meant the brass. The light was nearly gone. She did not light the lantern. She sat on the stool beside the bench and said, "May I?" because she said it every time, and he said, "Yes," and she put two fingers on his wrist, as she began every reading, and waited for the count to settle, and it settled, and then she put the flat hand on his ribs, on the left, low, where the front hand had been on a table in the autumn and the ribs had been quiet since a marsh, and went in.
    98	
    99	He felt her go. He always had; he had told her so, at a fork, and she had said *I know*. Her current came in through the flat hand as it came in through everything, steadily, without a check in it, and went down through the ten — the twelve — as water goes down through a bed of stones, finding the ways between, and he lay still and let it, and did not go with it, because that was the term and he did not shave terms.
   100	
   101	It took a long time.
   102	
   103	He did not count it. He had decided not to before she put the hand down, because a count was a thing done, and lying still was the absence of a thing done, and he could not do both. So he lay, and the ford ran outside, and the wheel turned, and the light went, and somewhere on the bank Brom said a word to Lira and Lira did not answer it, and the flat hand on his ribs did not move, and Oryn's breathing beside him went slow, and slower, and stopped being a thing he could hear, and he understood that she was as far in as she had ever been in anyone, and that she was going round.
   104	
   105	Going round. He felt her do it. Her current, which ran to things — that was Tide; a current that ran *to* whatever was hurt, and arrived, and did the work — ran down through the twelve to the middle, to the place, and did not arrive. It went round. As the twelve went round. As everything went round, and had, as far back as he could feel, and he felt her feel it, from his own side: the current bending where the bed bent, and the bed bending because of a stone that the river had gone round for so long that the going-round was the river's shape, and the stone at the middle not doing anything, because doing was not what it was.
   106	
   107	She stayed there. A long time. He felt her try — not push; she did not push; a healer who had spent her life reading current did not push a current at a thing that was not one — but *stay*, at the edge of it, with all that she was held still at the bank of a thing that had no bank, and read it as nothing, and read the everything around it, and read the nothing again, and it was the same.
   108	
   109	And while she stayed there, because he was lying still and had nothing to do with his hands or his count or his eyes, and because a man who owns twelve things and has been told to lie still with them does not stop owning them, he turned the twelfth.
   110	
   111	He did not decide to. He entered that afterward, honestly: he had not decided. It had turned as a floor leans that you have stood on for an hour; the eye going along a row whether he told it to or not. Cleon's inside. The lean. He had had it four days and had turned it on nothing since a board, because there had been nothing made to turn it on, on a road; and here, lying still under a turf roof with a healer's current going round a place in him, the twelfth turned, by itself, inward, toward the place, because the place was where everything in him was looking, and the twelfth looked where he looked.
   112	
   113	It should have found nothing. That was the card: *made things only; not a river; not a man.* He was a man. The still place was in a man. A record of a thing that was not made would read as what a heap read as, which was nothing with a shape, and he lay and waited for the nothing, and it was the only time in the reading that he was not lying still, and he knew it, and could not stop it any more than he could stop his eye going along six marks in a row.
   114	
   115	It did not read as nothing.
   116	
   117	It read as *still*. That first, and the same as Oryn's finding, and he held there, because it agreed with a healer and a thing that agrees with a healer is a thing you can enter. Still. Not flowing. Not empty. And then, under the still, as six marks on a board had been six marks and then were six marks with a direction — not a movement; a *lean* — the still place leaned.
   118	
   119	Not toward anything. He could not have said toward. It was not a row; it did not go left to right; there was no bearer and no borne. But it was not a heap. A heap sat, and this did not sit; it was *set*, as a thing is set that somebody has put where it is so that a next thing can sit on it, and the twelfth, which could tell him a thing was an instruction and could not tell him what it instructed, told him that the still place at the middle of him had the inside of a made thing, and would not tell him what it was made for, and he lay under the turf roof with a healer's hand on his ribs and did not move, and did not breathe differently, and did not let it into his face, because her fingers were on his wrist and she would feel a check in him as she felt everything, and there was no check, because he had lain still for her before with a thing found in him and this was only another one.
   120	
   121	He did not name it. He had no name that was not a guess. He had said so to her in a yard and it was on a page.
   122	
   123	But he heard Cleon, at a table with a cup: *If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be made and reads it anyway — Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it.* He had entered it at the time under the observation he could not file. He filed it now. It had been an instruction, and it had leaned, and he had not seen the lean until the thing it pointed at was under his own ribs.
   124	
   125	The eyes were paying already, in the dark, which was the one place they could pay unseen: the lean-to's open side gone to a smear, the lantern that was not lit no darker for it, and he let them pay and did not blink against it, because a blink is a thing a healer's fingers feel.
   126	
   127	Her hand came off.
   128	
   129	He felt her come up out of it — not fast; a healer does not come up out of a long reading fast — and the fingers left his wrist last, as they always did, and he heard her breathe again, and the stool creaked, and she sat a while in the near dark with her hands in her lap before she said anything, and he lay still, because she had not yet said he could stop, and did not.
   130	
   131	"Up," she said.
   132	
   133	He sat up. He picked up the log from the ground and did not open it.
   134	
   135	She said it plainly, in the register, as she said every finding, and she said it looking at him and not at the ford, which was not the register and was the other thing.
   136	
   137	"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
   138	
   139	He said, "Noted," because it was the word, and because it was true, and because it was the same finding she had given him at a marsh with less certainty and had now given him with all of it, and a finding that has been made certain is a different entry from one that has been made.
   140	
   141	"It hasn't changed." She looked at her hands. "Not since the autumn. Not a hair. I went as far in as I've ever gone in anyone and I stayed as long as I've ever stayed, and it's a still place that everything goes around, and I can tell you every current in you to the bank, now, all twelve — twelve; you've two I didn't have in the autumn, and one of them's not for fighting and I don't know what it's for and I'll not ask — and I can't tell you one thing about the place they go around, because there's nothing there for a healer to read. Nothing. And everything, round it." She stood up. "That's my finding. It's the same finding. It's not going to be a different one, because I've read it as deep as I can read, and that's the bottom of what I am."
   142	
   143	He did not tell her.
   144	
   145	He entered that too, that night, honestly, and the reasons in their order, because a thing withheld from a healer who has just gone as deep as she can go on your behalf is a thing that wants its reasons written down. Cleon had said not to, and Cleon had been right about the stair. He had no words that were not a guess, and he had told her once that he would not guess. And she had gone to the bottom of what she was, and had come up with her own finding, whole, and it was hers, and a man does not lay a rival instrument's reading over a healer's the minute she has finished, on her own bench, in the dark, as if her finding were the lesser one because it had found nothing. It had found nothing. That was a finding. The other had found a lean. That was a finding too. Two instruments, one place, two findings that did not agree, and he did not know which was the true one or whether both were, and he was not going to decide it on a bench, and he was not going to make her carry a thing she had not found and could not read.
   146	
   147	So he said "Noted," again, and she said "Good," and put the roll on her back by its strap, and that was the reading.
   148	
   149	Lira came in from the bank, then, with the light gone, and stood in the lean-to's open side, and said to Oryn, not to him, "There's a thing we read in Treswick. About the marks. About him. You'd want it, I think. Or not."
   150	
   151	Oryn looked at her. Then at Cael, on the bench, with the log shut on his knee.
   152	
   153	"Is it a finding?"
   154	
   155	"It's a leaf," Lira said. "Older than the board. It says what the thing was built for."
   156	
   157	"Then it's not mine." Oryn took the pan and poured it out on the ground, and set it on its rim to dry. "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table. My table's for things I can put my hands on." She looked at Cael once more, and the look was the look she had given a knee: a thing read and priced and not finished. "Lie still when I say. That's still the term."
   158	
   159	"Noted," he said.
   160	
   161	"It's the word," she said, and went out to the bank to see about the mule.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	They went at dawn on the two hundred and ninety-ninth, and he catalogued them from the ford, because it was the first time the count had been what it was and he wanted it.
   166	
   167	Twelve, and he took them in the order the road took them, because the road was the thing they were going to be in.
   168	
   169	Teague first, on the road above the ford, with the wrist wrapped and the hand that was on the end of it hanging as it hung, and the four of his behind him — the tall woman with the spear grounded and lifted, the broad man with the crossbow across his back and the limp set for the day, the young one with a pack that was too big for him and was going to get lighter, and the older woman with the cloth bag, who did not carry a weapon and never had. Then the crew's: Karis with the case and the map, and the map with the straightedge's line on it — he had laid the brass from Denvash along the bearing the night before by the lantern, and she had drawn along it, once, with the pencil, from the fourth circle out onto the blank sheet to the divider's point and past it, a line true to a hair on paper across a country that had none — and the bound hand holding the notebook with the other one writing, because a week was a week and she had not agreed to it. Brom with the kit and the first box. Lira with the third box and two arms. Seln at the back, where the count was kept. Vastin with the stick, day twenty, on the road, where a stick could be put down and would not be. And Oryn, with the roll on her back by its strap, and her hands out of her coat, walking as she walked to everything, square to it, on a road that ran east for two days with her holds and then did not, and she had said so, and was walking it anyway, on her terms, which were the only kind.
   170	
   171	And himself, with the middle box, and the brass in his coat, and twelve things held to nothing, and a place at the middle of them that one instrument said was nothing and one said was made, and neither of them his to decide.
   172	
   173	Twelve on the road, two of whom were on no wall's count — a healer of Tide and a healer of no Path, on either end of the ten that were, walking the same bearing for reasons that were their own — and a mule — hers since a fork, and back on the bearing because she was — which had Karis's boxes on it now beside her roll, because Oryn had put them there herself at the ford and had said nothing about it and the mule had not minded.
   174	
   175	The bearing ran east from the ford, past the Stair's bowl and the Hall's marsh, five days to the fourth site with the Compact's cordon round it, which none of them had crossed, and past that, on the line, to a point in a straightedge's shadow that had been quiet since there was paper to say so. The sun came up along it. He had not planned that; a bearing that ran east had the sun come up along it every morning, and he entered it anyway, because it was true this morning and he was the one who wrote down what was true.
   176	
   177	A healer's route and a researcher's archive, on one mule, on one road, walking the same way for as long as the road ran the same way; and the twelve of them behind it, in the order the road took them, going east into the light with the wheel turning behind them in its race, slow, and the ford-keeper at the mill's door with flour to the elbow, counting them out, and arriving at the number, and saying it to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:10:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 1 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Teague's spear is a tall MAN** (he / him): the locked Book 7 wrote him so (ch25, ch51, ch52), and Book 8 follows its own edition (OWNER-pending #59). Where the plan says "the tall woman", read "the tall man"; his silence toward the six until his question at the pass stands. (2) **Teague can read** (locked Book 7 ch25: he read a slip twice). Never write that he cannot; if he does not read a sheet it is because it is not in front of him or he has no need. (3) **Lira's asking:** she asked Oryn once about the ankle on the mountain in Book 7 and asked nobody for anything from then until Thornwater; do not call Thornwater her first ask since Norhold. (4) **The stroke and bar** is on stake thirty-two (the Stair's newer stake and the kit's unused one) and on the fourth site's thirty-four ONLY; stakes thirty-one and thirty-three do not carry it. Later records may show the grade on survey SHEETS; the field stakes do not all carry it. (5) **The cord:** the kit's cord is twenty strides, knotted at the fives (four knots to a length); Book 7's Ring was sixty-two cords and twenty strides round. The court is about 1,107 yards across, which is about FIFTY cords, not 159. Give no yardage for a cord. (6) **"West"** was first said aloud at the six-person table on the 268th (Karis's protected "It's west… Treswick…" speech, printed once in ch6). (7) **Deployment since the Line is twenty-nine** at the end of Movement 1 (two uses of the Iron read in earnest); trials are not counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:14:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 3 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Deployment since the Line is thirty-two** at the end of Movement 3 (one Ember grain and one Wind burst away, both at the cut on the 279th). Ten fragments; the eleventh has not come; nothing came of standing two feet from Vastin's wall. Purse 138 marks. (2) **Iron Wall has been shown once** (the cut): a plane from a line struck with the stick, wall to wall, rooted — he may not lift a foot — loud to anything that hunts a Path, and it spent him to the floor. **Vastin's RIGHT knee** was bitten there; Oryn shut the holes and bound the joint with laths for a month from the 279th (stick; no kneeling, no running, no wall); he said "I cannot undertake" and has kept it to the letter. Brom's right foot, two toes, ten days from the 279th. (3) **The date:** Cael and Seln each know that the technician's sheet carries the day of Cael's Kindling. Neither has said or written it; neither knows what the other made of it; nobody has interpreted the sequence. No figures are ever printed, no span of years is computed, and there is **no second calendar or offset** (one registry reckoning only). Vastin does no date arithmetic and knows nothing of it. (4) **The records at the spring:** Karis has matched FOUR sign-groups to the hide and has given NO number of places; no fifth place has been described. The same stroke and bar stands in a ruled square on all four matched places' sheets (in the field: stake thirty-two and the fourth site's thirty-fours only). The remark "Behaves as device. Cannot be." is on the Stair's sheet in the fourth row; its hand is NOT identified. The instruments column reads true on every line. (5) **Seln has taught one thing, to Vastin** ("Draft nothing. Just look."). Cleon has been named (by Karis) and nothing more is known of him than her protected lines give. (6) **Oryn** went north from the fork on the 281st with the brown mule, her linen and her crossbow with one bolt; Millrace in three weeks; she owes Cael the reading ("the one thing I'm walking up that road owing"); the station term is written in the log. Her past as told at the fork: a house for the sick in a city inside the Line, the column for the patient's tier, a woman from the river sheds mended off the sheet; she has not been through a gate since. (7) **Loads:** boxes on Brom (good shoulder), Lira (both arms) and Cael; the tarred kit case on Vastin, taken up unasked at the fork-stone; Karis the flat and the hide, with the copied page in oilcloth in her coat. **Cael laid his hand on that page once, on the rise.** Vastin has walked beside Cael since the rise. (8) **Stillhounds:** a den that has learned a road runs to where a declaration ends (Karis's sixth line). The sixty yards of four-strand rope are still owed to the Lowmarch ferryman.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:18:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 5 audit, 2026-10-11 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Eleven fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two; purse 134 marks** at the end of Movement 5 (the night of the 290th). The eleventh, Iron-Wall-adjacent, came from Vastin in the alley's quiet minute; it did not fill, it *set*; its prompt is *This far?*; it has not been deployed and **Vastin has not been told** (Cael holds that as a debt he cannot yet pay; Lira has read the log). (2) **How acquisition lines are printed in this book:** the protected "Fragment eleven" and "Fragment twelve" units are CAEL'S OWN cataloguing of what he reads — set once, in italics, framed as his plain words; they are NOT printed as a system block and take no FRAGMENT ACQUIRED heading or code fence. (3) **The Deepstacks:** an iron door with a sliding bar inside; twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine; three galleries. Middle gallery about 110 strides, Cleon's sloped desk and fixed lamp at its end, a shelf above it with a jar, a tied bundle ("Paper. Not stone.") and the black oak plank's sacking; the fifty-two sheets on three tables, 11/29/12; the plank stands against the third table. Eastern gallery: the old town's tall tally-books. One CARRIED lamp, Cleon's, held "a hand to the left" (Cael holds it and has begun to put it where the eyes are about to go). The boxes go up at the clerk's bell and come down each morning on a fresh card; the clerk writes *The same* and *four*. (4) **Cleon** knows Karis's name (he heard Brom say it and has used it once); his own is on the card and has never been said aloud. He has shown his Path from OUTSIDE only (bear / carried / nothing; "They're orders."; "Same make."; the limit: it does not work on a river, a man or a tree, and costs his eyes for an hour). **His week runs from the 287th to the 293rd.** He has not noticed Cael and knows nothing of the alley or the mechanism. (5) **Karis** has fourteen of twenty-three letters and reads numbers and number-words only; seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing; the bundle is tied and unread; Vastin's copied page is in her coat, unshown to Cleon. Her LEFT palm has seven stitches of Brom's under a strip of his shirt; she writes with the right. (6) **Vastin:** his right knee went again in the alley on the eleventh day of Oryn's thirty (a wall on it against her order, which he heard and asks that she be told); re-splinted with a slat from Brom's box, twice its size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. He gave his name, rank and office aloud at the yard hatch on the 285th; nothing of it was written. He still knows nothing of what Cael carries. (7) **Brom:** the left (good) shoulder wrenched under a dray on the 286th and worse after the alley; he carries his box on the bound right shoulder, a debt entered for the mill's healers; the broker's offer (a guard's post inside the second wall) was answered "I'll think on it." and declined in fact. Lira: day twenty-five out of wool on the 290th; a bruised right shoulder; she drops from heights without the arm. (8) **Seln:** a registry clerk knows his face and voice (the price of a truthful answer at the hatch); he watches from the roof above the cut. His case is **SHUT** — never "unopened" (this edition's Book 6 opened it for the five) — and its contents are named nowhere in this book. He has seen one watcher three times (horseman's boots with the spur-rests cut away). (9) **The faction** (six, hooded, no faces, no Path opened) knows the loft and has shown that it knows; it saw Vastin's wall, a rock, a knife and a boy who stood still, and nothing of quiet ground or the fragments. Cael has ruled that the cut is not climbed at dusk again. The loft is paid to the 295th.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:20:> **[Coordinator rulings after the Movement 6 audit, 2026-10-12 — binding; they override any plan text below that differs.]** (1) **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line thirty-two.** The twelfth is **Rune-adjacent, from Cleon** ("given, not taken"), acquired at his "There," on the 291st; it gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*), reads made things only, and costs the eyes afterward. Its lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics in Movement 6 and never reprinted. (2) **How it behaves:** it leans at every made thing, unbidden and OUTWARD, and Cael is learning not to look; it has never been used on a person, the crown or the leaf, and **never turned inward**. He refused the inward turn once, deliberately, until Oryn has been asked. **The first inward turn is unbidden, at Millrace, under Oryn's deep reading** (*still*, then *set*); the deliberate inward turn belongs to the plain's edge in Movement 9. (3) **Who knows the twelfth:** Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and Cleon. **Not Oryn (owed at the mill, with the readings round the place); not Vastin** (who also still does not know the eleventh or the mechanism). **Cleon's warning** ("If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — …") is known to Cael and Cleon only and is in no book; Cael tells nobody. (4) **The leaf** was read aloud once by Karis and heard by Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin; Lira, Brom and Seln read her copy; Oryn has not heard it. **Nobody quotes the leaf's wording again.** The reductions that may be said: *made*; *for a kind*; "the Architect" as a **title** (historical comparison only — "as old as the Architect" — never a present agent); "four hundred years" only where this packet's plan fixes it (here, the 298th log alone). (5) **The chronicle's line is marked and nobody speaks of it again**; the hole is never glossed. **Nobody joins the leaf to the lamps, sigils, plates, Arbiters or stations**; no *terminal*, *circuit*, *infrastructure*; no light, plate or instrument is said to attend, notice or want. (6) **Pole two was said aloud at the yard fire and is not in the log.** The line of march from it: "a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine" — "I want to stand in the middle of it." (7) **Seln's case is on his chest, shut, contents unnamed; he keeps it "a while longer".** Closed chapter 18 says he has carried it twelve years; print no other count. He will not follow the cut boots again and expects them on the road east. (8) **State at the night of the 293rd:** purse **130**; the reserve sealed in the third box; loft and handcart to the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed to the Lowmarch ferryman; Hesk's dividers and note shut in Hesk's book (never opened on a distance); Karis's fair copy in Cael's coat, the other in her notebook; the skin leaf under Cleon's jar and going to no counter; its back (a list of parts) unread — Cleon's work for the 294th. **Vastin: right knee day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**, slat-bound, stick, no kneeling, running or Wall; he sleeps in the stable's end stall and rides the handcart. Karis's left palm stitched (Brom's seven); Brom neither arm above the elbow; Lira day twenty-eight out of wool on the 293rd. (9) **Oryn** watched a Kindling at her fourth gate, is two days behind, will not come late to the mill, and means to read Cael "as deep as she went", asked first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:24:> Movement 7 already carries the 294th, all-day list, twelve fragments, Karis's bound hand, Vastin day fifteen and no mule (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:28–30`). Its author must additionally inherit:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:28:> - **Cleon.** He has shown the Path's inside, been told the twelfth's mechanism, declined a dangerous sentence, given the warning, deliberately looked away for two days, promised the leaf stays under his hand and above no counter, and said “Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts.” (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:15–239`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:115–125,227–239`). He let the fifty-two sheets lie one extra night and has written the 294th card (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–125`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:31:> - **Money, lodging and tools.** Purse 130; reserve sealed in the third box; loft/handcart through the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed. Hesk's dividers and note are shut in Hesk's book with Cael (`manuscript/chapter-37.md:95–99,141–143,267–273`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:37:> **[Coordinator note 2026-10-11 — NO-SOURCE drafting, binding.]** Draft WITHOUT opening any source chapter under `books/`. The source-chapter references in the line below are retained for the coordinator's and reviewers' tools only; the author does not read them. Events come from this packet, `BOOK_MAP.md` (the plan sections, the protected-text section and the reserved-truths section) and `STATE_LEDGER.md`. Write `EVENT-LIST.md` first. Where a protected unit is quoted in the plan only in part, leave `[[TIER-B: <unit> — coordinator to supply protected text]]` and write around it. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 1]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:42:  - From `BOOK_MAP.md`: §2 items 27–31; §3 A, C (294th; road east); §5 (the Teague cutaway); §7.1 (Force; Rune; the still place); §10 Tier A 11, 16, 23, 25, 27 and the S17–S18 Tier C lines; §12 C2, C4; §13 (the "Noted" order; Vastin's order).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:45:## Where we enter
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:47:It is the 294th day. The list of parts takes all day, and Cleon's finger is on the last line at the bell. Twelve fragments. Karis's hand is bound and she writes with it anyway. Vastin is on day 15 of a month. There is still no mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:49:## What this movement is for
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:54:- **The list of parts and the goodbye (294th).** "Your hand's better than mine on the fourth line." At the iron door: **"Work. Not a finding."** / **"Mind the stair. It leans."**
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:55:- **The road east (295th–297th; ~5,000).** On the bridge he counts five piers, and does not look at the crown. The spring, with the wind in their faces this time. The fork passed. The shoulder hold's factor: "He said you'd say that." **The cut revisited:** "Here." Vastin puts his weight on the knee with the stick off the ground, once. "Two words. Both of them ours." / "Noted" (Cael's). The feeling of being *known* is entered under each date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:56:- **Lowmarch (297th).** Pike: *Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.* The price and outcome columns are left blank, and the season line is dated. Cael reads the line the right way up. **Teague's term and contract, verbatim:** ten on a wall's count; shares by count; the field is Teague's; the reading is Karis's. "Contract. Not more than that." / "The wall's still the wall." Cael prices it honestly: Teague is the better field captain, and the rival clock still runs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:57:- **Force seen (298th morning).** The meadow cart. The frame lined up heel to palm, a body's length. The wrist clicks, and the young one's cloth is ready. "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." Cael enters the line and its cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:58:- **The grade cracked (298th morning).** Teague is at the door for the dividers, not the marks. There are three marks; the box for the first tier is ruled and never used. Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line: "A mark is a grade." Ages by first sheet: 61, 94, 138, 206. The third-tier point falls past the fourth, in the straightedge's shadow. **Oldest-last.** "It's observation … Not what it means." **Vastin's first "Noted."** Teague: "Oldest last." / "Then that's where we're going." **The log, verbatim** (the grandmother is *Cael's*: §12 C4).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:59:- **Millrace (298th evening).** "Eleven off the ridge." / "I know." The healers exchange one look, then both look at Brom's shoulder. Vastin is ordered to stand ("Nineteen days." / "Then it's eleven more."); it is his first order taken without walking it back. Oryn restates her terms: three holds owed, and "a stop on my own route". "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." **She asks each patient, "May I?"** The readings: Brom's shoulder; Lira's arm ("You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced"); Karis's hand ("Who sewed this."); the knee ("It held in a cut"; "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his.").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:61:- **Dawn (299th).** Twelve and a mule. The pencil line runs from the fourth circle onto the blank sheet and past the divider's point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:67:## What must remain true / withheld
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:72:- The grade is observation only, with no who and no why. No first-tier site appears. "Four hundred years" occurs only in the fixed 298th log.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:76:## What the author owns
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:80:## Where we leave pressure
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:17:## 1. Entry state and ending state (binding)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:19:### Entry (Book 7 close; source Ch1 opens on the hundred and thirty-first day past the Line)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:32:### Ending (source Ch24, dawn of the 308th; must hand off to Book 9)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:35:- **The reveal is delivered once, in the record's voice:** the leaf in the Deepstacks. "The Architect" is the record's title; the name is scraped away; it was about four hundred years ago; the purpose was "that the un-bound should not come again". The chronicle's "went into the ordering he had made, and is in it —" is **marked, not read**.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:49:## 2. The spine — every major source event, in order (all KEEP)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:58:6. **KEEP — Thornwater (265th–266th).** The ford in thaw. Oryn's yard. Lira's arm is released ("Five months and twenty days." [OWNER-pending #56] / "That's a season. Take it off."). Lira asks, for the first time since Norhold: "Will you mend it." / "Lie still". The ankle is mended. "Thank you." / "Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks." The map on Thornwater's table: "That's a line." Cael tells her about the cord: "I don't know what that is. Neither do you. Write that down too." Oryn comes down because Lowmarch and Millrace are her first stops after the thaw [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6]. Nobody asked her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:79:27. **KEEP — The list of parts (294th) and Cleon's goodbye at the bell:** "Work. Not a finding." / "Mind the stair. It leans."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:80:28. **KEEP — The road east (295th–297th).** The bridge (five piers counted; the crown not looked at). The spring. The fork passed. The shoulder hold's factor: "He said you'd say that." **The cut revisited** ("Here"): the stick briefly off the ground; "Two words. Both of them ours." / "Noted." The feeling of being *known* is entered. Lowmarch at dusk on the 297th. Pike: "Treswick. Survey read. Bearing." The season line is dated. Cael no longer reads pages the other way round. **Teague's term and contract:** ten on a wall's count; shares by count; the field is Teague's; the reading is Karis's; "Contract. Not more than that."; "The wall's still the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:81:29. **KEEP — Force seen (298th morning).** The meadow cart: the frame lined up heel to palm, a body's length; the wrist clicks; the young one's cloth. "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:82:30. **KEEP — The grade cracked (298th morning).** Three marks. Cleon's list puts the mark at the kindling and the mark on a sheet in one line: "A mark is a grade." Ages by first sheet: 61, 94, 138, 206. The third-tier point lies past the fourth, on the line. **Oldest-last.** "It's observation." **Vastin's first "Noted."** Teague: "Oldest last." / "Then that's where we're going." The log (§10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:83:31. **KEEP — Millrace (298th evening–299th dawn).** "Eleven off the ridge." / "I know." The two healers look at each other and then at Brom's shoulder. Vastin is ordered to stand ("Nineteen days." / "Then it's eleven more.") and takes the order without walking it back. Oryn's terms restated. "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." **She asks each patient first ("May I?").** The readings: shoulder, arm ("Once is once more than I priced"), Karis's hand ("Who sewed this."), the knee ("It held in a cut"; "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his."). **The deep reading**, with consent. **Rune turns inward unbidden: *still*, then *set*.** Withheld. Oryn's finding (§10). The leaf is offered: "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." Twelve and a mule at dawn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:84:32. **KEEP — The leg (299th–303rd)**, five dated entries: the Stair's bowl (299th); Oryn's road turning north at a cairn at noon on the 300th, where she walks on east; the Hall's marsh (300th); two camps with no water (301st–302nd); the crest (303rd). "Known" is entered under each.
