   150	
   151	Oryn looked at her. Then at Cael, on the bench, with the log shut on his knee.
   152	
   153	"Is it a finding?"
   154	
   155	"It's a leaf," Lira said. "Older than the board. It says what the thing was built for."
   156	
   157	"Then it's not mine." Oryn took the pan and poured it out on the ground, and set it on its rim to dry. "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table. My table's for things I can put my hands on." She looked at Cael once more, and the look was the look she had given a knee: a thing read and priced and not finished. "Lie still when I say. That's still the term."
   158	
   159	"Noted," he said.
   160	
   161	"It's the word," she said, and went out to the bank to see about the mule.
