    89	It came as it had come once before, on a scrubbed table. It did not go down into him, as his own read went down into the ground; it went along. He felt it leave her right hand somewhere about the thirtieth breath, and set out toward his feet without haste, as a joiner runs a thumb down the edge of a plank to learn whether it is true. It turned where there was no more of him, and came up the other side, and arrived under his neck, and set out again. There was the old hitch in it, a shade late upon his right side; he knew that now for a taped heel, favoured so long that it had got into everything she did.
    90	
    91	"That's skin," she said, from a long way off. "That's breath. Those are the three that I taped; they've been leaned on since, and they've forgotten it. That's a heart that's had a hard week."
    92	
    93	He let them go by, and did what she had taught him on that table, which was nothing at all: every door in the house left standing as it happened to stand. He had thought it then the hardest thing that he had ever been set; and he had done it since on a road, on every day, with ten of them and then with twelve.
    94	
    95	"Now yours."
    96	
    97	She went among them slowly, and named each as she came to it by what her hands met; and he lay in the dark behind his eyelids and heard himself told off like a load on a landing.
    98	
    99	"That one's quick, and it's hardly got a bank; I know whose it was. That's a deep narrow one. That's the heavy one, the workhorse, down in the soles, and here's something that waits, right through the frame. A hot point, by the left hand. A thing with a lid on it, which I'll not lean on; it's lifted since dusk, and you're keeping it down, and I'll let you, since it's that or have it up." She breathed. "Three lanes, like a ploughed field. A fine thin one that wants a corner to sit in. A short sharp one. And mine."
   100	
   101	He was at the hundred and fortieth breath, and Tide lay in him as still as she stood, since it knew its owner.
   102	
   103	"That's ten, and I had ten. Now where's the old man's?"
   104	
   105	He did not answer, since he was not to.
   106	
   107	"There. I'd have walked past it. It doesn't run at all; it stands in the stream like a post, and everything parts for it. That's eleven." Her hand had not shifted on his chest. "And there's a little flat thing behind your eyes, laid by, like a rule folded on a shelf. It's doing nothing. That'll be the reader's."
   108	
   109	She was silent for six breaths, and he felt the slow current pass down him once more and return.
   110	
   110	
   111	"Twelve," she said. "I'll not ask how many more you mean to bring me."
   112	
   113	---
   114	
   115	"Now the middle," said Oryn.
   116	
   117	It was the two hundred and sixth breath. He had been on that plank for seventeen minutes, and she had only just finished with what he carried.
   118	
   119	He felt her go to it, as she had gone once before. Her current came up his left side toward the hollow under the breastbone; and where it should have passed through, it parted.
   120	
   121	He had not had a picture for that until three days ago, and he had one now. He had stood on a bridge and counted piers so that he need not look at them, and he had seen what a brown river does at a cutwater. It does not stop there, and it does not go in. It divides upon the stone, and runs down either side, and joins again below; and a person on the bank can tell the whole shape of the pier by nothing but the water.
   122	
   123	"There," she said, very low. "It's as it was. Everything goes about it."
   124	
   125	She did not leave it, this time. She had come as far as this before, and had said what she met, and gone out again; tonight she stayed. He felt her send the slow limping current round it on the left hand, and then on the right, and then, which she had never done, round it from above and from beneath. She went about that still place as a person goes about a house which has every shutter closed, trying each wall in turn with the flat of a hand.
   126	
   127	"There's no bank," she said. "It's not a bank that stops me. There's nothing there to push on. I come up to it, and then I'm past."
   128	
   129	He drew the two hundred and fortieth breath. That was twenty minutes, and she had said that she would not stop at twenty.
   130	
   131	"It's not small. I'd thought that it was small."
   132	
   133	She went round, and round again. Her voice had sunk to almost nothing. The heel of her hand on his breastbone eased and settled with each passing, and he lay beneath it and kept every door as it stood.
   134	
   135	It was at about the three hundredth breath that the twelfth turned.
   136	
   137	He had not bidden it, or so much as thought of it, since he had been counting, and the count was all of him that was awake. It had lain where she said, a little flat thing behind his eyes, folded; for seven days it had leaned at whatever a hand had made, at pegs and rivets and the piers of a bridge, and each time he had given his eyes some other work, and it had come up short.
   138	
   139	His eyes were shut now, and there was nothing that he could give them to do.
   140	
   141	And something was being drawn in him, under her hands, which had never been drawn before. It was an outline. Her current went round the place on every side, and round again; and the thing behind his eyes came about to that, mildly, as a head comes round in a dark room toward the only sound in it.
   142	
   143	It did not look outward. It leaned in.
   144	
   145	*No*, he began to say; and he found that he could not say it without saying it with the whole of himself. A refusal was a holding, and she would feel a jaw go hard. She had felt it once, at the hundred and eighth breath, on another table, and had told him that he was helping.
   146	
   147	He could not shut that door, and he was not to open it, and so he left it as it stood.
   148	
   149	It leaned, for the space of three breaths, at the middle of him.
   150	
   151	*Still.*
   152	
   153	There was no voice in it; it was only there, as the weight of a stone is there in the hand that lifts it. It was not *nothing*. He knew how the thing said that: flatly, of a red square of tile, or of a table's idle leg. It was not *sits*, which it said of a page. It was a word that he had never had of it before; and he had used that very word himself, in a log, for longer than he had owned the thing which now said it.
   154	
   155	Her current made one more round, coming up from beneath, and parted, and joined.
   156	
   157	And the lean came down flat, as a foot arrives upon a floor.
   158	
   159	*Set.*
   160	
   161	His heart struck once, hard, against the heel of a healer's hand.
   162	
   163	He let the breath out slowly, like a man asleep, to the count, and he drew the next. He had stood on a river bar with a hound's teeth in the sack on his arm, and had not answered it, and that had been easier than this. He kept his jaw loose, and he kept his two hands lying open on the plank, with the palms upward, and did not let one finger of them close.
   164	
   165	*Set*: as a stone is set, or a stake. He knew three places where he had met that word. A thing had come into him in a stair, not as a filling, but as a post is stood in its hole; a small pale woman had found it under her own glove, when she had wished for a name; and it stood in the fourth line of a list on the back of a skin, hung level with the people at a kindling.
   166	
   167	There was a next word. He could feel the shape of it, as a tongue feels the gap where a tooth has been.
   168	
   169	He did not let it come. He put it down, as he had seen an old man put down a sentence in a gallery, with one hand off the stick; and he lay under a healer's palm and went on with his count.
   170	
   171	"Something turned over," said Oryn.
   172	
   173	It was a murmur, from very far away.
   174	
   175	"Not in the middle. Out at the edge of you. One of yours, in its sleep." Her hand had not moved. "Leave it. It's nothing to me."
   176	
   177	He left it. He was at three hundred and eleven, and he had never in his life lain so still.
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	She came out of him at the four hundred and ninetieth breath.
   182	
   183	He knew it by her hands: the one on his breastbone lifted first, and then she drew the other from under his neck, a little at a time, and let his head down upon the wood. She stood for a moment with both of them hanging; then she felt behind her for the keg, and sat on it heavily, and put her elbows on her knees.
   184	
   185	He had seen her grey, and he had not seen her so. Her face in the lantern was the colour of the ash in a dead ring, and her cropped head hung, and her lips went to fifty, and began again at one.
   186	
   187	Lira had come off the bench. She stood at the lean-to's post with a cup of water from the race, and she did not offer it until the second count was done.
   188	
   189	"That's forty minutes," said Oryn at last, and drank. "I said that I'd go as deep as I went, and that's it. There's a floor to me, and I've had both feet on it this quarter-hour."
   190	
   191	Cael had sat up. The lantern had begun to hurt him a little at the edges, already, and he looked at the turf of the roof instead.
   192	
   193	"You'll want it plain," she said. "I've only the one way of giving it."
   194	
   195	She set the cup down upon the shelf, among her things.
   196	
   197	"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
   198	
   199	The race ran by in the dark, and nobody said anything.
   200	
   201	He had heard her say before that a thing was past her hands. She had said it on a hillside, of a girl's arm; and she had told him afterward that when she said so he was to believe her, and not stand looking at her as though she had kept something back. He believed her. She had gone round that place for the better part of half an hour, at every height, with all that she had, and she had not come out and said that it was nothing. She had said what she was, and where she ended.
   202	
   203	It cost her to say it. He saw that, and he thought that it had cost more than the forty minutes. A person who has read every body that was ever laid before her, for thirty rounds of a country, had sat down on a keg in front of a boy and a girl and told them the size of her own tool.
   204	
   205	It was a whole finding, and it was hers.
   206	
   207	He had another. It lay behind his eyes in two words, and the two findings did not say the same thing. Hers said that there was no flow in that place for a Tide to feel. His said that something stood there which had been put.
   208	
   209	He looked at the roof, and kept his own.
   210	
   211	It was not the first thing that he had kept back. He had kept a date from everybody, at a spring, and a warning. But he had never before kept a thing from the person whose hands had drawn it out, with those hands hardly cold upon him; and he found that it had a weight which the others had not.
   212	
   213	"That's all?" said Lira. She was not asking him.
   214	
   215	"It's all that I've a right to. I could dress it for you. I could say that it's a stone, or a knot, or a door; I thought all three on a road north, between one gate and the next, and they're none of them true." Oryn rubbed her eyes with the heel of her hand. "I know what I read, and I know when I'm not reading. I'll not make a guess sound like a finding because a boy's come a long road for one."
   216	
   217	"I didn't come for one," said Cael. "I came to hear yours."
   218	
   219	"Then you've heard it."
   220	
   221	Lira had not moved from the post. She had her chin up, and Cael knew what was coming, since he knew that girl. A thing had been asked for at a fork-stone, in so many words, and she had carried the asking for eighteen days as she would have carried a count.
   222	
   223	[[TIER-B: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf …" / "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." — elided in three places; the plan says only that Lira offers the leaf and that Oryn does not hear it. Staged in the lean-to after the finding: Lira reminds Oryn that she asked at the fork for whatever walked out of the archive, all of it, and says that there is a paper in Cael's coat which did; Oryn asks whether it is a finding; Lira answers; Oryn declines to have it read at her table, and restates her term. The fair copy stays in Cael's coat throughout and is not unfolded. Oryn's last line may belong here or to the first scene of this chapter, where her term is given in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
   224	
   225	The paper stayed where it was. Cael had put his hand to his coat when the girl began, and he took it away again.
   226	
   227	He was not sorry. A leaf had been read once, in a gallery, by the one person who could read it; and the woman on the keg had spent herself to the floor that night upon the one thing that she had come down a river for. She had drawn a line round her own table. It was the same line that a small pale woman had drawn on a desk with a bound hand: what a person had found went on one page, and what a person had been told went on another.
   228	
   229	"Go to bed," said Oryn. "The pair of you. I'm going to sit here until I can stand."
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	His eyes began to pay before he was across the yard.
   234	
   235	It came as it had come on a stair under a registry: first a dryness, and then sand, as though somebody had thrown a handful of the road into his face. By the door of the cart-shed the mill's one lantern had a ring round it, like a moon in mist, and he did not look at it twice.
   236	
   237	They had been given the cart-shed, with last year's hay thrown down between the wheels. Ten people lay in it, and a big man's breathing went up and down at the far end like a slow saw. He found his place by his hands, and lay down on his back with his arm across his face, and let the water run.
   238	
   239	It ran for a long time. Somewhere beyond the hay a broad man turned over on his bad hip, and grunted, and a boy said three words in his sleep which might have been a count. He had not turned the thing; it had turned of itself, and he had not looked at anything, with his eyes or without them. It billed him all the same, to the grain, as a toll-keeper charges a cart that was pushed through his gate by somebody else.
   240	
   241	"You're awake," said Lira.
   242	
   243	She was a yard from him in the dark, and by her voice she had not lain down.
   244	
   245	"Yes."
   246	
   247	"You went stiff, on the plank, somewhere past the middle of it. She didn't see; she'd her eyes shut. I hadn't."
   248	
   249	He did not answer at once. He could not have told that girl a false thing, and there was no need to; he had only to find the true one that was his to give.
   250	
   251	"Something came," he said. "I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to."
   252	
   253	"Is it bad?"
   254	
   255	"I don't know what it is."
   256	
   257	"That's twice now that you've given me a clerk's answer and looked sorry about it." The hay rustled; she had put her back against a wheel. "I'll not dig. I dug at you all the time that we were on a bluff, and all I ever turned up was what you'd have told me in a week if I'd let you alone. You'll tell me when it's a thing."
   258	
   259	"On the road."
   260	
   261	"If there's road," said Lira, and he heard her lie down.
   262	
   263	He listened until her breathing had gone long. She had asked him a thing, and had been given less than the whole of it, and had said that she would wait; and he did not know many people who could do the third.
   264	
   265	He could not see to write. He took the book out and wrote by feel, as he had on a moor, with the pencil's butt against his thumb to keep one line from running into the next.
   266	
   267	*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day, night. Millrace; the cart-shed. A hundred and twenty-seven. Told Oryn, before she read me: the two readings at the brook and the post, and why; the eleventh; the twelfth. Then she read me, asked first, for forty minutes, as deep as she goes. Her finding is hers. I have it word for word, and I am not copying it here, since she can say it.*
   268	
   269	*One thing more came, under her hands, that nobody asked for. I am not writing what. Reasons, in order.*
   270	
   271	*One. It was not bidden. She said that nothing was to be turned while she was in. I turned nothing, and it turned. I'll not hand a healer the thing that broke her term and call it a finding.*
   272	
   273	*Two. It is one look, by a tool that was never meant to look at a man. A count that one hand has made is a hope, and I have nothing to lay it against.*
   274	
   275	*Three. What she found is whole. If I set a second word beside it tonight, it stops being what she found, and becomes half of what two people think.*
   276	
   277	*Four is not mine to write.*
   278	
   279	*Ruling: hold. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   280	
   281	He shut the book on the pencil.
   282	
   283	The fourth lay in him where it had lain for seven days. A man with red eyes had poured a cup of water, under a town, and had said what he was to do if the thing ever read something that ought not to be made, and read it all the same. He was not to tell that man, and he was not to tell anybody. He was only to remember that it had been said.
   284	
   285	He had remembered, and it had been easy, for a week, since it had been a sentence about a day that might never come.

