   195	So a cart went on a furlong ahead, with a brown coat on its tail, and for the fourth day together Cael walked toward a pair of boots that hung.
   196	
   197	They passed the well at the fork, which had a house over it now, with a door hung and a new rope on the windlass; a crew had taken that from the plank in the cold. He put his hand on the curb as he went by, since he had hauled foul air out of that shaft for three days on a bitten leg, a long while ago. Then the road climbed, and they were on the back of the ridge.
   198	
   199	It was the same pale grass, combed all one way, and the same wind. The country fell off on both hands. He had come along it once in the dark before a frost, behind a mule, to ask a healer whether she would come; and he had gone back along it with the answer. He had not known then that the left-hand ruts ran to anything but three holes in the ground.
   200	
   201	Nobody spoke. Twice a flat hand went up, far ahead, for nothing that Cael ever saw, and the file stood, and went on at two fingers. The second time the boy stood exactly when Lira did.
   202	
   203	They came to the fork a little after noon, where one pair of ruts kept the ridge. The other went down the western face through a notch that wheels had worn, and the cart had stopped at its head, where the carter was locking a wheel. The old man had got down there, as he got down for every hill.
   204	
   205	There was a flat rock there. Teague lifted his chin at it, and the file broke, and sat, and the old woman undid a strap of leather and looked at a wrist.
   206	
   207	Cael took out the book.
   208	
   209	He had sat on this rock with it once, and written two lines about a healer. The mill's wheel was to be heard below, very small, like a tired man splitting wood. He set the day at the head, and the count.
