     6	
     7	"You've a thing to say," said Oryn, without turning. "You've had it in your boots since you came down that notch, and you walked as if one of them had a stone in it."
     8	
     9	"Two things. I wrote that you'd have them from me at this mill, before anything else was said."
    10	
    11	"Then say them. I've a loaf to eat before I'm fit for you, and I'll hear it while I eat."
    12	
    13	He had the book out, and he did not open it; he had found on a ridge that he did not need to.
    14	
    15	"You told me to write it down and stop touching it. On the two hundred and eighty-first day I read myself twice, with yours: once in a field by a brook, and once at a milestone, in the dusk." He kept it level. "I went round the place, and not into it, and I told myself that round wasn't touching. I knew what you'd meant when you said it. I read your words for the leave that I wanted. I'd do it again, and I'm not asking you to say that it was right."
    16	
    17	She had taken her hands out of the water, and she dried them, a finger at a time, on the skirt of a dead man's coat.
    18	
    19	"What did you get?"
    20	
    21	"The same nothing. But I found out what the nothing was. Yours feels what moves; it was all that it ever felt, in you or in me. Where nothing moves it comes back empty, and it would come back empty from a gatepost. I'd been calling that a reading of the place, and it was only the end of the tool."
    22	
    23	"I could have told you that."
    24	
    25	"You did tell me. You said that you'd never felt such a thing in anybody, and I heard a finding. It was a limit."
    26	
    27	She was silent for a little while, with the loaf in her lap, and then she broke it.
    28	
    29	"I set a rule on you for a healer's reason," she said. "A person who's found a sore place goes back to it every night with his fingers. You kept my words, and you went round what they were for, as a carter goes round a toll by way of the field; and that's worse than breaking it outright, since it's what clever people do." She chewed. "And then you carried it seventeen days, and came down a ridge to tell me so before I'd laid a hand on you. That's the other half of it, and I'll take the two together. It's entered. What's the second?"
    30	
    31	"There are twelve. You had ten at the fork."
    32	
    33	Her jaw stopped.
    34	
    35	"The eleventh's the old man's. It came in a stair, under the town, while he held a wall for us on that knee, and he doesn't know. I can't tell him how it came without telling him how all of them came, and that's six people's to give."
    36	
    37	"That's a debt."
    38	
    39	"It's written as one."
    40	
    41	"And the twelfth?"
    42	
    43	"There's a man under the registry who reads how a made thing stands: which stone bears, and which is carried. He was showing Karis the inside of it, and I was holding his lamp." He drew breath. "It wasn't taken. It turned round in me, as a sheet is turned on a desk. He's been told, since it was his; he said that it wasn't supposed to be possible, and then he poured me a cup of water."
    44	
    45	"What does it do?"
    46	
    47	"It leans. It'll lean at a table, or a bridge, or a hinge, and I've to stop it looking. It gives one word, and it bills the eyes for an hour afterward. It reads only what somebody made, and he was plain about that. Not a river, and not a tree." He made himself finish. "Not a man."
    48	
    49	Oryn had turned on her stone, and she looked at him as she had not yet looked at him that day, the whole of him, from the boots.
    50	
    51	"You've a place in you that doesn't move," she said slowly. "And you've been handed a thing that reads what doesn't move."
    52	
    53	"Yes."
    54	
    55	"Have you turned it there?"
    56	
    57	"No. It offered, on the first afternoon, and I said not until you'd heard."
    58	
    59	"I've heard." She ate the last of the crust. "I'll tell you what I think, and it's a healer's, as it was before. The man who owns that tool told you what it's not for, and a man generally knows his own tool. That's his word. Here's mine. Tonight I'm going into you as deep as I go, and I can't read a thing that's being stirred while I'm reading it. So for as long as my hands are in you, nothing's turned: not that, and not any of the others."
    60	
    61	"And after?"
    62	
    63	"After, you're on a road, with your own eyes to spend, and I mend what's brought to me. I don't keep people." She got up, stiffly, with a hand on the stone. "But not on my plank."
