editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:13:It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:107:He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:221:"Nothing is mine that I have not counted." She drew the gloves on, a finger at a time, and slipped the tape of the uppermost row, and turned back its first sheet by the edges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:237:There was one more column, at the right of every sheet, narrow, and ruled off from the rest. Its printed head was in plain letters, and it said what the office kept that column for: the anomaly. Karis went down it on the first sheet with one gloved finger. She turned to the second sheet, and the third; and then she lifted a corner in the lowest row of the six, which was the oldest, and looked there. It was the same on every sheet. In every clerk's hand, in every one of six years, that column held a single word, and nobody had ever written a second word beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:247:Then she did a thing that was very like her. She went back through the whole row from its first sheet, quickly, looking at nothing but the heads; and each time that she met a group of four signs which she had not met before, she laid one finger of her left glove down upon the deal. She could not read a group. She could tell one from another, as a person with no letters can still tell his own mark from his neighbour's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:275:The fourth row was the shortest, of seven signs, and it stood near the foot of the Stair's twenty-first sheet. When he had found it he straightened, and put both hands in the small of his back. Cleon looked from the plank to the three tables, and at the plank again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:58:- **The grade cracked (298th morning).** Teague is at the door for the dividers, not the marks. There are three marks; the box for the first tier is ruled and never used. Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line: "A mark is a grade." Ages by first sheet: 61, 94, 138, 206. The third-tier point falls past the fourth, in the straightedge's shadow. **Oldest-last.** "It's observation … Not what it means." **Vastin's first "Noted."** Teague: "Oldest last." / "Then that's where we're going." **The log, verbatim** (the grandmother is *Cael's*: §12 C4).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:29:There was tar on the lamp's ring before the first sheet had been looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:82:30. **KEEP — The grade cracked (298th morning).** Three marks. Cleon's list puts the mark at the kindling and the mark on a sheet in one line: "A mark is a grade." Ages by first sheet: 61, 94, 138, 206. The third-tier point lies past the fourth, on the line. **Oldest-last.** "It's observation." **Vastin's first "Noted."** Teague: "Oldest last." / "Then that's where we're going." The log (§10).

