   173	Twelve on the road, two of whom were on no wall's count — a healer of Tide and a healer of no Path, on either end of the ten that were, walking the same bearing for reasons that were their own — and a mule — hers since a fork, and back on the bearing because she was — which had Karis's boxes on it now beside her roll, because Oryn had put them there herself at the ford and had said nothing about it and the mule had not minded.
   174	
   175	The bearing ran east from the ford, past the Stair's bowl and the Hall's marsh, five days to the fourth site with the Compact's cordon round it, which none of them had crossed, and past that, on the line, to a point in a straightedge's shadow that had been quiet since there was paper to say so. The sun came up along it. He had not planned that; a bearing that ran east had the sun come up along it every morning, and he entered it anyway, because it was true this morning and he was the one who wrote down what was true.
   176	
   177	A healer's route and a researcher's archive, on one mule, on one road, walking the same way for as long as the road ran the same way; and the twelve of them behind it, in the order the road took them, going east into the light with the wheel turning behind them in its race, slow, and the ford-keeper at the mill's door with flour to the elbow, counting them out, and arriving at the number, and saying it to nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:71:[[TIER-B: "Here." — the packet gives the one word and pins no speaker. Staged at the place where the line was struck on the 279th, forty strides below the cut's head and three above Brom's flat stone, with the file halted on the dry edge and nothing on the ground to mark it. Vastin could own it (he has stopped there of himself and is looking at the road); Cael could own it (he is the nearest to him and is the only other who stood at that line). Nobody else is within four strides — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:[[TIER-B: "Two words," … "Both of them ours." — elided, and the plan pins no speaker. Staged as the next thing said on the dry edge after the stick comes down, low, as the road allows at a halt; Cael's "Noted." follows it and answers it. Vastin could own it, speaking of what this road has taught him to say; so could Brom, who has turned on his stone three strides below and is looking up at the two of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:167:[[TIER-B: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins the term on Teague and gives the heads (ten on a wall's count; shares by count; the field Teague's; the reading Karis's) without their wording. Staged as Teague's, said to Cael at the bench's end before the whole room, with his eyes having just gone to Vastin at the door-post. The four heads follow below in my own words, as his, so that the restored unit may stand before them or round them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:[[TIER-B: "The wall's still the wall." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker. Staged as the answer to Teague's term, at the bench's end, with the talk having just come round to Vastin's wall. It could be Cael's (the contract does not make the wall the field's to place); it could be Teague's own (whatever the thing is called, a wall is what the count is built on); it could be Vastin's, from the door-post. The lines after it are written to follow any of the three — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:[[TIER-B: "Don't put it in front of anything that needs doing twice." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker; it is listed under the contract with the word "Force". Staged in the meadow a breath after the one use, with the arm already in the young one's cloth, when the carter points to a second cart sunk twenty yards off: as Teague's answer, and loud enough to carry to the bench. Teague could own it (to the carter, or across the yard to Cael, who has watched); the lines that follow do not depend on which — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:43:[[TIER-B: "A mark is a grade. The one they give a boy at fourteen says what he's *for*." … "The one on a sheet says how *old* the thing is." — elided between its halves, and the plan pins no speaker (the packet says only that Cleon's list puts the kindling mark and the sheet mark in one line). Staged as Karis's, at the table, with stake thirty-two, the dead surveyor's first page and the Stair's abstract laid together under the window, to the six and Vastin. The first half can stand here. The second half ("how old") is earned on the page only in the next scene, by the running numbers; if the unit must stand whole in one place, it can be moved down to follow "Two hundred and six." without touching either scene — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:155:[[TIER-B: "Somebody laid them down in order" — a fragment (no stop given), and the plan pins no speaker. Staged at the table, with the four running numbers read along the hide from west to east and the divider's point standing past the fourth ring: the first thing said aloud once all five are in view. Brom could own it, pricing what he sees; so could Lira, or Cael; Karis could own it only as a thing that the paper shows. The next lines follow from any of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:161:[[TIER-B: "It's observation. I'm telling you what the paper says. Not what it means." — whole, but the plan pins no speaker. Staged as Karis's, at the table, to Lira's "Who?" and to Vastin's drawn breath, with her bound hand flat on the sheet of three boxes. If it is another's (Cael's, said for her), the lines on either side still stand — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:[[TIER-B: Teague: "Oldest last." … "Then that's where we're going." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins Teague. Staged in the doorway of the back room, with his eyes on the hide and the divider's point, to nobody in particular and then to Cael. Whatever stands between the two parts may be his or another's (a question from Cael, or Karis's assent); the scene ends on the second part — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:271:[[TIER-B: "Nineteen days." / "Then it's eleven more." — the two lines are whole, but the plan pins neither speaker. Staged at the lean-to's post, with Vastin standing where he has just been ordered to stand and Oryn looking at the slat from her stride and a half; one of the two gives the count and the other the remainder. Vastin could own the first (he has called his own day every morning since the cut) and Oryn the second; or the other way about. Nothing after this depends on which — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:281:[[TIER-B: Oryn's terms restated — the packet gives only two fragments, "three holds owed" and "a stop on my own route"; its sense is given too (she comes east as a healer on her own round, and is on nobody's count). Staged as Oryn's, at the fence, in answer to Teague, with the six and Teague's four in hearing. Her own sentences are not written here — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:287:[[TIER-B: "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." — the second line is Oryn's (Tier A 25 lists it with her Millrace lines); the plan does not pin the speaker of the first. Staged at the mill's bench at dusk, with Brom's bowls laid along it and Oryn's own bowl set at its end before an upturned keg. Lira could own "Sixth chair." (she told Oryn at this mill that there was a bowl); so could Brom, or Cael. Oryn answers, and sits — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:329:[[TIER-B: "You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced" — the third line is given without its stop, and no speaker is pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the forearm: Oryn reading what the arm has done since Thornwater, Lira answering in one word, Oryn's reply. That order of speakers is the natural one and is not the plan's — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:375:[[TIER-B: "It held in a cut" — a fragment, with no speaker pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the knee and her eyes shut, after Vastin has told her of the wall on the eleventh day. Oryn could own it (reading the joint: what it bore on the road and what it was then asked to bear in a stair); Vastin could own it, of the one time he stood on it with the stick up, two days ago; Cael could, speaking for him. Her verdict follows in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:379:[[TIER-B: "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his." — four lines, none pinned to a speaker. Staged on the plank as the close of the knee's reading: one of them answers her verdict with the word; she remarks on it; it is defended; and its owner is named. Vastin could own the first and third, Oryn the second, and Vastin or Lira the fourth (with a look toward Cael, who stands at the lean-to's post); the scene ends on it — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:223:[[TIER-B: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf …" / "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." — elided in three places; the plan says only that Lira offers the leaf and that Oryn does not hear it. Staged in the lean-to after the finding: Lira reminds Oryn that she asked at the fork for whatever walked out of the archive, all of it, and says that there is a paper in Cael's coat which did; Oryn asks whether it is a finding; Lira answers; Oryn declines to have it read at her table, and restates her term. The fair copy stays in Cael's coat throughout and is not unfolded. Oryn's last line may belong here or to the first scene of this chapter, where her term is given in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:"Work. Not a finding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-41.md:205:"Mind the stair. It leans."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-46.md:197:"There's a place in you I can't read because it isn't *flow*. I've spent my life reading flow. Whatever that is, it's not the kind of thing my Path was built for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:137:*Treswick. Survey read. Bearing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:*Karis cracked the grade. The sites are ranked by age and the bearing runs oldest-last — somebody laid them out in order, and the order runs into the deep edge, toward the first one they ever made. Second-tier's the fourth site, the one the Compact fenced. Third-tier's out past it, on the line, quiet for centuries. Teague's crew came after the thaw like he said — ten of us now, and he's still better at this than I am, and the ledger still says so. Two crews, one bearing, and a straightedge my guardian sent me because my grandmother said a man who catalogues everything eventually needs to draw a line through it. Turns out she was right about the line. Turns out the line was already drawn four hundred years ago. We're just the first to follow it to the end.*

