485:- **The Deepstacks.** Inside the iron door: a sliding bar; **twenty-seven steps of the dark stone in three flights of nine**; a cross-passage; three galleries side by side, cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock (the rock was cut first; the dark stone laid against it second; the town's mortared work third — Karis's first finding, undated). **The middle gallery** is about a hundred and ten strides, shelved to the roof with nothing ticketed; sorting tables from ninety strides; Cleon's sloped desk at the end under a fixed lamp on an iron arm, with a shelf above it (a cup, a jar, a thick bundle tied both ways in brown tape, and a black oak plank in sacking). The eastern gallery holds the old town's tall tally-books; the western, quarrymen's gear. **The fifty-two sheets lie on three cleared tables, 11 / 29 / 12 (the Ring, the Stair, the Hall), as they lay on the floors, and stay there; the boxes go up at the clerk's bell each evening and come down each morning on a fresh card.**
486:- **Cleon.** Protected, all his: "I know that hand." (of the Hall's fourth sheet); "Being believed — … It's the end of being able to be wrong."; "Give me a week." (the 284th); "They're orders."; the two-part limit ("I can tell you these are orders. I can't tell you what they order…"; "It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree… It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else."); "Same make."; and the 287th close ("documentation… built the same way twice… never once slept well since."), in which he uses Karis's name (he heard Brom say it). Karis: "Older than [UNBOUND]." **His Path has been shown from OUTSIDE only**: he looks at a made thing from its foot upward, without blinking, and says which signs BEAR (white), which are CARRIED (black) and which are NOTHING (red, packing); his plank's four rows are the same lines as rows on the Hall's fourth, the Stair's ninth and twenty-first, and the Ring's sixth sheets. **On the 287th he set his own week to run from that day to the 293rd, with everything else put aside**; at its end every sheet goes up. The bundle on his shelf ("Paper. Not stone.", from the same shelf as the plank) is tied and unread. He has not noticed Cael, who holds his lamp ("a hand to the left"); he knows nothing of the alley, the fragments or the mechanism.
487:- **The decipherment (Karis alone).** Of the forty-one kinds of sign: **seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing**; the carried are what a word is spelled in. The plank's cutter counted each row in figures (a ring is five, a peck one) and then wrote the number out in words, in the old town's tongue or its near kin; she proved it against the old tally-books by pattern alone before looking. **Fourteen of twenty-three letters by the 290th; she reads numbers and number-words only**, has refused to guess one tempting six-sign word, has not untied the bundle, and has not shown Cleon Vastin's copied page (it is in her coat). **The leaf has not appeared.**
488:- **The yard door, the 285th.** The clerk's form had no line for matter that had gone up and come back; he asked one question ("The same?") and was about to send up for the under-registrar. **Vastin gave his old name, rank and office aloud** (protected: "Vastin. Archmarshal, Compact enforcement. You'll find the name is —"); the clerk's pen came up and **wrote nothing of it**. **Seln, posing as a porter on the hatch's bench, answered truthfully** (protected, whole: "He's on the count. Four hands, same as yesterday, entered by the practitioner and answered for by her, and the form asks what a hand's for, not what he was. The researcher's at your counter and she'll want to be told we're through,"). The clerk wrote *The same* and *four*, and has done so each day since. **The cost: a Treswick registry clerk now knows Seln's face and voice.** "I'm sorry." (Vastin) / "Don't be sorry. Be nobody." (Seln). Seln watches from the roof above the cut's head from then on. No restricted name has been written.
489:- **Brom (the 286th)** righted a broker's dray off a lad's foot in the registry's yard with his back and **no Path opened**, wrenching the LEFT (good) shoulder; a broker's man offered a guard's post inside the second wall (a sealed monthly pass, a bed, the house's coat, four marks and found; "Ask for the factor."); "I'll think on it."; he took up his box on the bound RIGHT shoulder ("Cart was heavier"), and carries it there — **a debt Cael has entered, owed at the mill to two healers**. "You think Karis is right?…" / "Good. I'd hate to have turned down a real job for a wrong hunch." His two toes were done on the 289th.
490:- **Seln's night walk (the 286th night):** from a warehouse roof below the blue gate he looked at the registry, its three doors (the front for names; the yard for loads; a returns hatch at the side where a courier may lay a sealed thing unasked) and the station's light, and at the case on his chest; **he decided nothing**. "I looked at it." The case is **shut** (never write "unopened": this edition's Book 6 opened it for the five); its contents are named nowhere in this book. He has seen the same watcher three times (horseman's boots with the spur-rests cut away).
491:- **Vastin** went toward the lamp four times on the 287th, twice on the 288th, not at all on the 290th. "How long did it take you." / "I was fourteen. I'm nineteen. I'm not there yet either." (protected).
492:- **The alley (the 290th dusk)** — the cut of twenty-two steps (an iron rail at its mouth). Six, hooded, faces tied: two came in by the yard gate with shod staves and barred it; four held the head (a crossbow, a stave, one who came down to the plane, and a signaller with a shuttered lantern). **Vastin turned uphill and struck Iron Wall on the ninth step, wall to wall** — "In that place there is no round." Two bolts broke on it. Nobody else declared: Brom tipped the loose third tread and let it go, and sat on his box; Lira dropped from the landing and put her knife into a stave's shaft (her arm touched nothing; the right shoulder took the wall); **Cael went one step up and stood square at the plane's foot, the reason, for a minute and some (eighty-one counts)**; Seln's weighted cord from the roof bound the signaller's arm to the rail and the lantern was never shown. They left at a walk: none taken, no faces, nobody dead; a shortened watchman's stave and two plain bolts left behind.
493:- **THE ELEVENTH.** In that quiet minute **Cael acquired Iron-Wall-adjacent, from Vastin** — it did not fill, it *set*, "as a post is stood in its hole"; its prompt is *This far?*; he has told it no each time. **Not deployed. Not told to Vastin.** The protected lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics (not a system block): "Iron-Wall-adjacent. Vastin. Partial. Iron family. Held. … Tier equivalent unknown. Deployment: none. Eleventh." **Eleven fragments; deployment since the Line: thirty-two.** Vastin: "I put the wall in front of it anyway. I'd like that entered…" ("it" is what Cael is, which he could not read) / "It's entered," — and Cael has entered more than the man knows: **a debt he cannot yet pay** ("Not told. I can't tell the taking without telling the rest, and the rest is six people's"). Lira has read the log.
494:- **Costs.** **Karis's LEFT palm** cut on a step's edge: seven stitches, Brom's, bound in a strip of his shirt (no healer inside the gate will touch a hand without writing a name; she writes with the right). **Vastin's right knee has gone again on the eleventh day of Oryn's thirty** (a wall on it, against her order, which he heard word for word and asks that she be told): a lath cracked, re-splinted with a slat from Brom's box, twice its size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. Brom: both shoulders. Lira: the right shoulder bruised; day twenty-five out of wool.
499:All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial; cold read; source-distance (1 tracked passage, ch27 scene 2; probe 2% / 14%); plan / canon / protected-text audit (RATIFY WITH LINE FIXES; ten exact fixes; all nineteen author flags and every minted item KEPT). Eight applied by the coordinator (five markers restored whole with speakers — two of Cleon's had been staged as Karis's — and three joins). Two DECLINED: the audit wanted the eleventh's lines printed as system text under a FRAGMENT ACQUIRED heading; the protected unit is Cael's own cataloguing (it names Vastin and says "Eleventh"; the twelfth says "me"), so the author's frame stands and the coordinator set the unit in italics instead of a code fence. **Repair r1 in progress with the same author.**
504:- **The Deepstacks' ground.** Inside the iron door a bar of black iron as long as a man's arm, shot from within. A stair of the dark dry-laid stone, **twenty-seven steps in three flights of nine**, with two landings, low-roofed, unworn. At its foot a cross-way a dozen strides long, lined with the dark stone; off it **three galleries side by side**, each as broad as a cart, cut in the bluff's pale bedded rock with a pick. **The middle gallery** is shelved to the roof in bays divided by uprights of black oak, about a hundred and ten strides long; nothing on its shelves has a ticket (some bundles have tags on their under sides). From ninety strides the old town's sorting tables stand end to end down the middle; at the end, against the cut rock, a high sloped desk of black oak with a lamp in a glass chimney on an iron arm, and a shelf above it (a cup, a jar of water, a thick bundle tied both ways in brown tape, and the board in sacking). **The eastern gallery** holds the old town's ledgers; its tall books stand as high as the roof and are reached by ladders. **The western gallery** is the quarry's own: the board lay on its bottom shelf under quarrymen's wedges. The cold does not move; a flame stands straight; no sound comes down from the registry. **On the 284th** he gave his orders for the three tables over his shoulder, with his finger still on its line, and told them to get their loads off; when Cael next looked he was standing at the first bare table and the leaf lay face upward on the desk with nothing across it to keep the place. The Hall's table is not six strides from the desk, and nothing said there is too low to be heard at it.
507:- **Karis on the stone (her stated conclusion):** the rock was cut first; the dark stone was laid against the cut; the town's mortared work is third. She puts no year to any.
508:- **The clerk at the hatch (285th).** His voice: thin, dry, exact. His difficulty was his own form: matter entered on the 284th had gone up at his bell and come back, and his book would have said six boxes and two cases. He said that he must send up to **the under-registrar, who keeps the schedules**. After Seln spoke he wrote *The same*, the day, his mark and *four*, and has written *the same* each day since. He asked one thing ("The same?"). He looked at Seln "properly, from the cap to the boots".
510:- **The lamp.** Low; a hand to the left of what is looked at; held on one knee. Lira on the stair; Brom ("You're a wall. I asked for a lamp."); from the third hour of the 285th, Cael. Cleon says *lamp*, *left*, *up a finger*; he has carried it himself for thirty years, "one hand for the light, and half an eye for where I'd put it". By the 289th Cael moves it before the word: all the looking runs from the low left corner of a thing upward to the right.
511:- **The chairs.** One before the desk's slope, with a cushion in the shape of one man; one against the wall, heaped with bundles on the 284th, cleared and wiped on the 285th, and carrying his cushion on the 287th (his own chair has none). Karis is found sitting on it on the 288th.
512:- **Cleon.** He missed his noon bowl on the 284th and has gone up for it on the other days. He does not touch paper with his skin: he points with a reed as long as a forearm. Three bowls of counters (white pebbles, black pebbles, red squares of broken tile). He does not blink while he looks; his eyes run; he rests them shut for an hour. To Cael at two feet the Path "made no sound at all". Brom said Karis's name in his hearing on the 286th ("I said no names." / "I never gave it to you. I gave it to her."). "It was the first thing I did when I came here": he has looked at the grey bridge twice. He made a sound "that was nearly a laugh" on the 290th.
514:- **Bearer, carried, nothing.** "White" bears (take it out and the row comes down); "black" is carried (it hangs from the white); "red" is nothing (packing, so that a row fills its stone). **Of the forty-one kinds: seven bear, twenty-three are carried, eleven are nothing** (Karis's count on the 288th, when all fifty-two sheets had been laid). A bearer stands at the head of every run. Vastin: "To do a thing; to what; and how many times."
515:- **Cleon's week (as repaired).** On the 284th, from his chair, with his eyes shut, Cleon said what being believed was to him (protected) and then asked for a week (protected: "Give me a week."); Karis asked the five for the same week in her own words ("I should like his week"), and Cael said that it was hers. On the 287th he re-set it: he had spent the half of the first on his own page, which he read every morning before he would look at hers; that is put by, and the week is his, from that day, with nothing else on the desk. At its end every sheet goes up and the clerk writes that it is gone (he will not have matter lying a month under *the same*). Its seventh day is the 293rd; three days are left after the 290th.
516:- **The bundle.** On the shelf beside the jar, tied across both ways in tape gone brown; it came off the same shelf as the board, behind it. "Paper. Not stone." He put two fingers on it and did not take it down. Nobody has untied it. Karis has not asked for it.
517:- **The decipherment.** (1) The pecked figures equal the number of signs in each row: the cutter counted his own work. (2) The sign-group after each figure is the count in words. Karis tested it without looking at the board: from the oldest of the old town's tally-books (the eastern gallery, third bay on the left, the eleventh shelf) she wrote only the pattern of like and unlike letters in that hand's spelling of twelve, nine and seven, and then laid the pattern against the cutting. It held in every place. The tongue is the old town's "or that tongue's grandmother". **Eight letters** from the board. (3) The carried signs on the floors spell further numbers: *ten* on the Ring's fifth sheet and the number after ten on the Stair's fourteenth confirm the eight; two more letters from the Ring's ninth, confirmed on the Hall's eighth (**ten**, the 288th); three more from the Stair, confirmed on the Hall (**thirteen**, the 289th); one more from the Hall (**fourteen**, the 290th). Three signs are described: a hooked upright, a thing like a gate, a thing like a fork. **She has read no word that is not a number.** One group of six signs stands four times in the Stair's twenty-nine, always after a bearer; she has four of its letters and a word that she wishes to find there, and she has covered it and not written it.
518:- **Brom's yard.** A broker's dray (two wheels as tall as Lira, one mule, a square of slate painted on its side) loaded with roofing slate shed its near wheel on the yard's slope; the slates slid and pinned the carter's lad by the boot against the dock's kerb. Brom stopped the yard-men's lever at the high side, took the low corner on his left shoulder with no grey, held while Lira drew the lad out by his belt, and was let down onto two split logs driven under the axle by Cael and a yard-man. The lad's five toes bend. **The broker's factor** (a good grey cloak, a broad hat, a shaved chin, clean hands, a tally; no name, no face): the house is the third on the brokers' street with the slate above its door; a guard's post at its store inside the second wall; a standing pass, sealed, by the month; a bed in the house above the wall; the house's coat; **four marks, and found**. His master "keeps eleven on his doors" who will not shift a keg until they have lit up, "and then it's a line in somebody's book". Brom: "I'd have taken it at sixteen and written home the same night."
519:- **Seln's night (the 286th).** The district is empty at night; a watch's handcart goes its round with a lantern; the blue gate is shut, with a brazier in the arch. He sat on the leads of **the chandler's warehouse on the ring road, the tallest roof below the blue gate** (four floors, a hoist-beam, an outside stair with no door). The case weighs "something under three pounds"; its key is in his waistcoat. **Every registry on the Line has three doors**: the front for names; the yard for loads; and a returns hatch at the side, with a lamp and a night clerk, where a courier lays a sealed thing at any hour and neither may ask the other a question: "A registry enters what is laid on its ledge." Treswick's is down a passage off the square's eastern corner. He considered the hatch and put it by; he considered leaving the case on the Deepstacks' unticketed shelves and saw that for filing; he ruled nothing. He marked that a clerk had seen his face and that nothing had fallen.
521:- **Vastin.** At the yard on the 286th he held the dray's mule by the cheek-strap with the left hand and gave no order. On the 287th he moved toward the lamp four times (Lira counted); on the 288th twice; on the 290th not at all. On the 288th he began to post a watch and stopped ("That's one"). On the 289th he held three tall books at Lira's word, qualifying it ("I cannot undertake to carry them to a table"). His three heads on the night of the 290th: the knee (he had Oryn's words in his mind as the stick came down, and asks that she be told that he heard her); what the six came for (they looked past him at Cael); and that he knows no more of what Cael is than the plate did.
522:- **The alley.** The gate at the cut's foot can be barred from inside the cut. **An iron bar is let into the wall at the cut's mouth, beside the parapet, for people coming up under loads.** Six, hooded, each with a cloth over the lower face: two with shod staves from the yard; above, one kneeling with a crossbow on the highest landing, one with a stave beside him, one who came down to the plane, and one at the rail with a shuttered lantern. The plane stood from the lip of the ninth step, wall to wall and to the sky, seen for a breath as a bloom on the dusk. Two bolts broke on it (the second a hand from Cael's face); a palm and one shoulder were laid on it. Brom dropped his box across the fourth step, tipped up the loose third tread (a slab of dark stone a forearm long and a hand thick), lifted it to his thighs and let it go down the steps at the first stave. Lira came off the landing over the box and drove her knife a third of its length into the second stave's shaft; she took the wall on her right shoulder. Brom's boot put the second man down the steps; one of them then hauled on the box's becket, and Brom sat on the box. Karis's boot went from under her on the dished sixth step; her bare left hand opened on the lip of the step above. The hood on the landing lifted two fingers; the lantern was raised to the parapet; **Seln's cord (a clerk's page-weight: a pear of lead on a thin cord) took the arm to the rail** before the shutter opened. Two low notes whistled. They left at a walk: the bow unstrung, the cord cut in two places, the dead lantern taken up, the gate unbarred. Cael's count was eighty-one. **A stave was left** (a watchman's, shortened, its ferrule filed) and **two bolts** (plain, no fletcher's thread).
523:- **Seln's finding.** They opened no Path, though the one on the landing stood like a man who has one: a Path opened under that wall is entered by whoever sees it, and they took the same care as the six to have nothing of theirs entered. The bow was to make somebody open; the light was to tell somebody below. They went for a box and not for Brom: they were told to bring away what was carried. "What they would have been glad to find was on a roof above their heads." The four went two east and two west along the lane, hoods back before the first corner; the two below left by the carts' gate, which had been left on the latch for them.
524:- **Karis's hand.** A straight cut across the heel of the left palm, from the root of the thumb to the wrist's other side; "not deep… only long". Bound in the cut with Brom's neckcloth; sewn in the loft with **seven stitches** of waxed thread on a curved roping needle held in the lantern's flame; the thread bitten; bound with a strip of his shirt. She said her fourteen letters over, one to a stitch. She writes with the right. Why no healer: in the district's house the entry on the sheet at a bed's foot (name, registry, tier) precedes the treatment, and after the fourth bell a card waits until those with names have been seen.
526:- **Small things.** Brom keeps a twist of cloth under his box's lashing with a roping needle and waxed thread. A pot of horse liniment was begged from the stable for his left shoulder. Vastin mended the becket of Cael's box with the stable's awl. The old keeper at the third gate did not write the lateness against the card on the 290th. The third tread of the cut's bottom flight is now out of its bed.
532:**Where everyone is.** Six in the loft: Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Vastin (on a pallet under the blanketed gable window, unable to stand), and Seln on the ladder's head. Cleon is under the registry with fifty-two sheets on three tables, the board against the third, and tomorrow's card written and given. The clerk has written *the same* on six days. Oryn and the brown mule are north on her round (not on the page in this movement). Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
534:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Eleven fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He has stood unfiled in the middle gallery. He has been the lamp since the 285th and has knelt two feet from Rune in use for five days; nothing has come of that. He has begun to put the light where the looking will go. He wanted the Path tried on himself, wrote that he had not said so, and has not said so. He has the eleventh (Iron-Wall-adjacent), has read its lines, has told it no three times, and has not used it. **He has not told Vastin**, and holds that he cannot without telling the mechanism, which is the six's. He has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; he has walked the lane with its four alleys fourteen times. He has ruled that the cut is not climbed at dusk again and has ruled nothing else for the morning. Body: sound; a tarred case struck his ear.
536:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Arm: day twenty-five out of the wool on the 290th.** It held a lamp-ring, a ladder's rail and a belt; she has practised dropping off a ladder with it tucked (eleven times before the 289th) and came off a landing so in the alley. **Right shoulder bruised on a wall; the arm took nothing.** Her knife is sound. Her registry paper has not been touched. She keeps the square's side of Cael in the lane. She has read the 285th's log and the 290th's.
538:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. **No grey at any point.** Toes done on the 289th. **Left shoulder wrenched under the dray on the 286th and made worse by the slab on the 290th; he cannot raise either arm above the elbow.** He has carried his box on the bound right shoulder since the 286th and took two falling people on that arm on the 290th: owed to two healers at the mill, entered by Cael as his own debt as much as Brom's. He was offered the broker's post and has, in his own mind, turned it down (the factor has been told only "I'll think on it"). **The purse: a hundred and thirty-four.** He sewed Karis's hand. He means to buy the rope going out.
540:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork. She has said "Older than [UNBOUND]" once, at Cleon's table. She has heard Cleon say, of himself, what being believed is, and has known it for her own as well; she told Brom what had believed him (her fifty-two sheets agree with the thing on his shelf). She asked the five for his week and was given it. **Fourteen letters of twenty-three; she can read the cutter's figures and number-words and nothing else, and will not guess.** She knows that seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried and eleven are nothing; that a bearer heads every run; that the board and the floors carry the same rows. She has not asked for the bundle. **Vastin's page is in her coat, unshown; the title on it has not been spoken.** She holds the flat, the hide, the ledger, her notebook and the day's card. **Left palm: seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt.** Three days of Cleon's week remain.
542:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. A clerk of the Treswick registry knows his face and has heard him speak. He watches from the roof over the cut. He has looked at the registry, its three doors and the station's light from a roof, with the case on him, and has ruled nothing. **The case is on him, shut; its contents are not named in this movement.** He has the better half of his cord and the stave. He has seen the same boots three times.
544:**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **He stood a wall on the eleventh day of thirty.** The knee let go sideways when he released it; one lath cracked; Brom has bound it again with a slat; it is twice its size; he cannot stand or hold a bowl on the night of the 290th. He knows what Oryn said it would cost. He gave his name, rank and office at the yard hatch on the 285th and was not written. He has apologised to Seln and been told to be nobody. He has asked for the wall to be entered and has been told that it is. **He does not know** the mechanism, what Cael carries, that anything was taken from him, the sum, or what is under the bar; he does not know what the six in hoods hoped to see and has said that he does not ask. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. Whether he heard Seln speak of the roof is not known.
546:**Cleon.** Rune Path, Bronze; shown from outside only. He has said that he knows the hand; has shown the board; has said what bears, what is carried and what is nothing; has given the limit in both its parts; has said what being believed is to him and asked for a week (the 284th); has re-set that week to run from the 287th with his own page put by; has spoken his close; has Karis's name and has used it once. **He has taken no notice of Cael.** He does not know that anybody was set upon in the cut.
550:**The faction.** Six, hooded, unnamed, faceless. It reached inside the third wall, opened no Path, took nobody and nothing, and saw a wall, a rock, a knife in a stave and a boy standing on a step. **It saw nothing of what Cael carries.** It saw Vastin's face uncovered and an Iron Wall. It knows the loft, and has shown that it knows.
552:**Objects and money.** **134 marks** (136 less two for the loft's second week, paid on the 289th; the loft is theirs to the 295th). The reserve sealed in the third box. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are in the loft. The fifty-two first copies are on Cleon's tables. In the loft besides: a shortened watchman's stave with a knife-cut in it; the card for the 291st. Two bolts were left lying in the cut. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
554:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; not Vastin, Cleon or the clerk. The eleventh: Cael and Lira (from the log). The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone. Cael's wish to be read: Cael and his book (Lira reads the book).
556:**Open threads.** A debt to a man who cannot be told. A knee that may have bought a stick. Two shoulders and a hand for two healers at a mill. A bundle that nobody has untied, and a page that has not been shown. Nine letters to find, and one word covered with a glove. A boy who knows which way a lamp will be wanted. A question that he has not asked, of a man who has not looked up. A case that has been looked at. A clerk who has heard a name. Boots under a lamp. A stair that will not be climbed at dusk, and no ruling yet on what is to be climbed instead.
559:Packet 006 carries the 291st, borrowed handcart, Karis's bound left hand, eleven fragments, and the fact that three clerk questions remain unasked (`MOVEMENT-006.md:26–28`). Its author must additionally inherit the following surviving particulars:
562:- **What has been deciphered and by whom.** Karis alone has fourteen of twenty-three letters; she can read figures and number-words only. She knows seven kinds bear, twenty-three are carried and eleven are ornament/nothing, that a bearer heads a run, and that board and floors repeat the same rows. She has covered one tempting six-sign group and has not guessed it. Vastin's copied page remains in her coat, unshown to Cleon (`chapter-31.md:63–189,319–339`; `chapter-32.md:3–19`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:111`).
563:- **Cleon's knowledge and statements.** Cleon knows Karis's name and has used it once; he knows the hand, has externally demonstrated bearer/carried/ornament and “orders,” has stated both limits, has matched the same make, and has delivered the documentation/built-twice close. His week runs from the 287th through the 293rd. He has not noticed Cael, learned of the alley, shown the inside/lean, or acquired knowledge of the mechanism (`chapter-27.md:187–199`; `chapter-29.md:193–289`; `chapter-30.md:253–279`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:117`).
564:- **The eleventh and its entry.** Iron-Wall-adjacent, from Vastin, partial/held/defensive/rooted, tier equivalent unknown, deployment none. Cael has read it, refused its *This far?* three times, deployed nothing, and told only the log; Lira has therefore read it. Vastin has not been told. Cael recorded both Vastin's requested wall entry and more than Vastin knows (`chapter-32.md:237–257`; `chapter-33.md:149–175,205–227,291–299`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:105,125`).
565:- **Vastin.** Right knee, original day eleven of thirty: gone sideways again, re-lathed with one slat from Brom's box, twice its former size; on the night of the 290th he cannot stand or hold a bowl. He knowingly heard and broke Oryn's no-wall order and asks that she be told he heard. He has no office on the page, no stated age, and still knows nothing of the mechanism or eleventh (`chapter-33.md:129–145,185–227,285`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:115`).
566:- **Karis.** Left palm has a long shallow cut and seven stitches, bound in a strip of Brom's shirt; she writes right-handed. No healer inside the gate has seen her. She has three days left of Cleon's week (`chapter-33.md:63–125,277,305`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:111`).
567:- **Brom.** Toes are done. The older right shoulder remains bound; the left/good shoulder was wrenched under the dray and worsened by the alley slab, leaving neither arm raisable above the elbow. He carries his box on the bound right shoulder. He has effectively rejected the broker's four-marks-and-found guard post, but the factor heard only `I'll think on it.` Cael has entered the shoulders as a debt for the mill's healers (`chapter-29.md:45–147`; `chapter-31.md:265–271`; `chapter-32.md:117–137`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:109`).
568:- **What the faction knows.** It knows the loft and has deliberately shown that knowledge. It saw Vastin's uncovered face and Iron Wall, plus a rock/knife/still boy, but no Path from another crew member, no face of its own was secured, and it saw nothing of quiet ground or Cael's fragments. Seln's finding is that it wanted a visible declaration and a carried box and avoided being entered itself (`chapter-33.md:247–289`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:121`).
569:- **Inn, purse, count and day.** Six are in the long loft over the stable; it is paid through the 295th, with pot at dusk and seven bowls. Purse 134, reserve sealed in the third box. The below-ground daily count remains Karis as bearer plus four hands; Seln is never one of them. Night of day 290 is complete; Movement 6 opens day 291. The cut will not be climbed at dusk again, but no further route ruling has been made (`chapter-31.md:265–267`; `chapter-33.md:277–305`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:101–103,123`).
570:- **Lira and remaining objects.** Lira is day twenty-five out of wool, has a bruised right shoulder, and the left arm took nothing. Three boxes, kit case, flat and hide are in the loft; the 291st card is there; the shortened watchman's stave is with Seln; two plain bolts remain in the cut; sixty yards of four-strand are still owed and Brom means to buy them going out (`chapter-33.md:285–307`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:107,123`).
571:- **Seln and the case.** Seln is at the ladder head/roof watch, knows the same altered boots through three sightings, and has the better half of his cord and the stave. A Treswick clerk knows his face and voice. His case is on him and **shut**; five people know its contents from Book 6, but this movement names none. He looked at the registry/front-yard-returns-door choice and decided nothing (`chapter-30.md:3–151`; `chapter-33.md:183,247–275`; `book-06 chapter-38.md:167`; `book-06 chapter-39.md:5,15,217`; `AUTHOR-REPORT.md:113`).
573:**Movement 5 CLOSED (2026-10-12).** Same-author repair r1 (ch27–31; ch32–33 untouched apart from the eleventh's lines set in italics): the eight coordinator-applied fixes kept and re-joined — **ch27's scene at the Hall's table now turns on Cleon** (Cael's "Then what is it?" goes to Karis, who does not answer; Cleon, eyes shut at his desk, says the "Being believed" unit to himself in the hearing of five; Karis tells Brom who has believed him; Cleon opens his eyes for "Give me a week."; **Karis then asks the five in her own words — "I should like his week" — and Cael says it is hers**); ch30, Cleon re-sets his own week from the 287th (half the first went on his own page each morning; that is put by); ch31's opening keeps only her side of being believed; Seln's line at the hatch whole, with the narration noting that he said nothing that was not in the clerk's own book or at his ledge, "and he had said *we*, which lay in no book at all"; Cleon's close whole with the author's joins. The tracked passage re-ordered (Cleon gives his table orders over his shoulder with his finger on his line; when Cael looks again the chair is empty and the leaf lies face up, unmarked); two physical anchors in the cipher proof; restated method compressed; first-use anchors added. Sol recheck: **CLOSE, no line fixes**; the tracked passage RESOLVED; every binding ruling borne out; the coordinator's ruling on the eleventh's lines (Cael's own cataloguing, in italics, not a system block) treated as settled and consistent on the page. The "New canon minted" and "State at movement end" sections above were refreshed from the post-repair author report. Final: 35,987 prose words; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (17 protected). Purse 134 marks; deployment thirty-two; **eleven fragments**; the night of day 290, in the loft at Treswick.
576:## AFTER MOVEMENT 6 — chapters 34–40, days 291–293 (drafted 2026-10-11, author claude-opus-5-5, NO-SOURCE method)
579:- **Entry seam with Movement 5 ratified** (Sol plan audit): the loft on the night of the 290th; eleven fragments; deployment thirty-two; purse 134; Vastin's knee re-splinted; Karis's left palm stitched; the fifty-two sheets on their three tables.
580:- **THE TWELFTH.** On the 291st, at Cleon's "There," (his finger at the end of the Stair's fifth row), **Cael acquired Rune-adjacent, from Cleon** — "given, not taken": it did not pour or set, it *turned*. Protected, Cleon's, in this order: "A record sits … An instruction *leans* … This is a stair."; then Karis's "The strata lean too."; then "There,". The protected lines are Cael's own cataloguing, printed once in italics (never a system block): "Rune-adjacent. Cleon. Partial. Rune family. A made thing's lean … Deployment: none. Twelfth." with *He looked away. Kept.* and *Not a man.* **Twelve fragments; deployment since the Line: thirty-two.** It gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*); it costs the eyes afterward (paid on the stair, Vastin guiding him); made things only.
581:- **How the twelfth behaves (ruled against the audit).** **It leans at every made thing, unbidden, outward** — the board, a rope-walk, a bridge, a hinge, a table — and Cael is learning not to look ("it looks before I've said that it may"). That is the plan's own wording ("the twelfth leans at every made thing … He learns not to look"; "catches himself reading made things everywhere") and stands. **It has never been turned inward, on Cael's own still place**, and he refused that turn once, deliberately, until Oryn has been asked. **The first INWARD turn is unbidden and belongs to Millrace (Movement 7).** It has never been used on a person, the crown or the leaf. A few unmeant outward glances (the rope-walk on the night of the 291st; Hesk's dividers; the inn table) are not counted as deployments.
582:- **Who knows the twelfth:** Lira first, on the stair ("Twelfth. His. Lean."); Brom, Karis and Seln; **Cleon, told by Cael at the bell, in the order he has it** ("You did what." / "That's not in the classification system. That's not *supposed* to be possible. That's — " / "No. I'll not say that one. Not down here. Not to you."). Cleon knows how this ONE fragment came and nothing of any other. **Not Oryn; not Vastin.** **Cleon's warning** (protected, whole: "I'm going to pretend you didn't tell me that … Everything I read is *made* … If that fragment ever reads something for you that shouldn't be *made* and reads it anyway — … Don't tell me. Don't tell anyone. Just remember I said it.") is known to Cael and Cleon only and **is in no book**. "Cael did not tell anyone. He remembered." Cleon looks away from the boy on purpose from then on.
583:- **The honest way up (the 291st on).** The carts' street: a paved shelf round the bluff to a banded pole and a weigh-beam; the beam-man chalks four weights against the practitioner's name on the card and will not weigh a man. The stable's handcart is theirs while the loft is (to the 295th); Brom between the shafts; **Vastin rides on the boxes, sleeps in the end stall of the stable (he does not go up the loft ladder again), and takes the inner stair on his seat and hands.** The clerk has written *The same* and *four* on ten days and has left three things unasked.
585:- **The 292nd.** Hesk's parcel (the dividers; **Hesk's note, verbatim**) came by the carrier with the cut hat; Cael has not opened them on any distance. The dinner: four marks, seven cups poured and two kept under saucers (Karis; Oryn): **purse 130**. Protected, **Cael's, said to Vastin**: "Out here you count who's owed a seat … It's a different ledger." Seln read a slip, burned it and said "Closed."; what was on it is not given. **The first inventory written inside walls.**
586:- **The light.** On the night of the 292nd the thread of the crown's light on the loft's boards was **not steady for a count of one**. Cael felt "the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him", gave himself three plain causes, did not cross and did not look again; it is the fourth entry on the page, ruling *hold*. Lira drew the blanket to the frame without looking. **Nothing in the text likens the light to a plate, a ring, an Arbiter or a thing that attends** (the draft's comparison was cut).
587:- **Oryn's fourth gate** (unnamed hold): she watched a girl's Kindling from a bench, with no office, plate or clerk, and laid hands on nobody. She is two days behind, will not come late to the mill, and will read Cael "as deep as she went", asked first. She has heard of no leaf and no twelfth.
588:- **THE LEAF (the 293rd).** A single leaf of horn-coloured skin between beech boards from the bundle on Cleon's shelf; twenty-six lines on its face in the floors' letters; a list on its back, **not gone into**; a place four or five letters wide scraped pale, with one upright stroke left. **Karis read it aloud ONCE** (protected, whole, in the record's voice: "In the years of the unmaking … that they should not come again."). Her one account: "It's a title, not a name; the name's gone."; ***The Architect*** (spoken once, by her; the word on Vastin's copied page); by the year at the leaf's head, in the old town's count, **about four hundred years ago**; the purpose is the un-bound. "Older," she said. "Made. Us." **Heard by Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin; nobody who heard it quotes it.** Karis has all twenty-three letters (her first word that was not a number was *and*; the covered six-sign word was *set*).
589:- **The chronicle.** Under the lower board, a later year-book of the town on paper. Karis read one line out (protected: "— and of him it is said that he did not die in the way of men, but went into the ordering he had made, and is in it —") and stopped at a thumb-wide hole: "I am not going to read into a hole." "Marked." (Karis) / "Marked." (Cleon). **The hole stays a hole: no gloss, no guess, no second source, and nobody speaks of the line again.**
590:- **Two pages.** Vastin reached once aloud (protected: "The apparatus … And the mark that is given at the kindling — ") and Karis stopped him: evidence on one page, inference on another, and the second page has nothing on it. **Nobody joins the leaf to the lamps, the sigils, the plates, the Arbiters or the stations**; *terminal*, *circuit*, *infrastructure* do not occur. Karis made two copies with the gap ruled as an empty box; the fair one is in Cael's coat.
591:- **The street and the fire (the 293rd evening).** At the inner stair's head Cael read Karis's page **the right way up** for the first time ("There was no desk on the other side of this."). He told Seln on the carts' street; **both lines are Seln's**: "Made." / "For a kind." At the yard fire Lira read first; the fixed order held; Cleon came above ground once, ate half a bowl and undertook that the skin stays under his hand and goes to no counter. **Seln:** the case is on his chest, **shut**, its contents unnamed; "I'll keep it a while longer." (a decision, not a disclosure; no count of years is printed here — closed chapter 18 says twelve). **Vastin** (protected): "I believed it was imperfect." / "Now you're telling me it was *aimed*." / "At a category. Which turned out to be a person. Who's sitting across the fire."; to Lira's "His?": "No. I read it, after. I did not sign it." / "I would have." Brom priced it and withheld a verdict ("I'd want to hear the other side"): **the record's fear is reported and not endorsed.**
592:- **POLE TWO.** Cael, on his feet at the fire (protected): "Since my Kindling I thought the system forgot to make room for me … It didn't forget. It was *built* so there'd be no room. Four hundred years of a whole world, so that I wouldn't happen." / "I happened anyway." Lira: "Yeah." / "Now what." Cael: "Now we find out how old the oldest one is … There's a site at the end of the line that's been quiet since they built the machine." / "I want to stand in the middle of it." **It is not in the log.** "Four hundred" stands exactly twice in the movement (Karis's account; pole two).
593:- **Still withheld:** the eleventh from Vastin (the debt entered as heavier); the twelfth from Oryn and Vastin; the deep-layer date (Cael and Seln, each alone, unsaid, unwritten); what is in Seln's case; the back of the leaf.
594:- **Bodies.** Vastin's right knee: **day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**, slat-bound; stick; no kneeling, running or Wall. Karis's left palm stitched under linen (she has begun to use it without noticing). Brom: neither arm above the elbow. Lira: day twenty-eight out of wool; right shoulder bruised. Cael sound; eyes recovered.
595:- **Money:** **purse 130 marks**; the reserve sealed in the third box; loft and handcart to the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed to the Lowmarch ferryman. **Names:** none minted. **Length:** about 36,100 against 34,000, accepted.
598:All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial; cold read; source-distance (5 tracked passages; probe 3% / 18%; the two capacity-failed lanes were relaunched and ran); plan / canon / protected-text audit (REPAIR, bounded; thirty-seven exact fixes; all thirteen author flags KEPT). **Seventeen applied by the coordinator**: the sixteen marker restorations and the light. **Four AMENDED to the plan's own pieces** where the source's fuller wording clashed with this edition (Cleon's speech uses a term the edition never has; the leaf's aside contradicts the live first reading; the title sentence would have doubled *The Architect*; Seln's speech put the case on his back for four years against closed chapter 18's chest and twelve). **Twenty DECLINED**: fix 17, and fixes 18–36 as a class (the audit read "does not turn unbidden until Millrace" as forbidding the outward lean that the same packet prescribes). The editorial read's "marked, not read" objection to the chronicle is DECLINED (the line is protected text that must stand once; the hole is what is not read). Speakers the author's staging had left open and the audit pinned: the cups line is Cael's; both street lines are Seln's; "there" is Cleon's. **Repair r1 by the same author (claude-opus-5-5): thirteen joins written, five tracked chains broken, two orientation cues; Sol recheck CLOSE with no line fixes.**
605:- **The carts' street and the beam.** (r1: in the yard Seln names the risk before the ruling — "a bar at the foot of it, and a beam, and a man who writes" — and Cael answers that he would "sooner be weighed in front of fifty carters than shut in where nobody's looking"; the card is spelled out as one bearer, Karis, and a figure of four meaning Cael, Lira, Brom and Vastin by count, with Seln on no card.) From the lane's end a broader way opens to the left between the last house and the top wall: a shelf cut into the bluff on its outer side, paved with grooved setts, wide enough for two drays; up-traffic keeps the wall, down-traffic the kerb; it curls in under an archway to **a banded pole let down between two posts**, with the weigh-beam beside it (a pan as broad as a cartwheel on three chains; a man in a leather cap with a ruled slate, eleven years at that beam). His rule: "What goes by the slot's the hatch's. What comes on wheels is mine." **The honest way:** Karis lays the card on his ledge and says that it is matter for the lower archive on its practitioner's card; he reads the card's head with his lips and chalks four weights against that name. No name is spoken; none of the four restricted names is written. He will not weigh a man ("There's no column"). Vastin had given Cael the rule beforehand in the yard ("a load that travels on a practitioner's card is entered to the head of the card… it has an article, which I do not give you") and says one sentence at the beam and no article. Karis tells Cleon that his name is on a slate ("It's been on worse").
606:- **The handcart.** The stable's own, two wheels and a pair of shafts, lent by the woman at the half-door while the loft is theirs, "if it comes back swept". Brom goes between the shafts (leaning up; walking backward as the brake down). Vastin rides on the boxes, facing backward, and is got off the wheels at the dock's corner.
607:- **Vastin from the 291st.** Let down the loft ladder on a box-rope, with a turn round the ladder-post under Brom's weight; he does not go up it again and sleeps in the end stall of the stable, on straw, at no charge. He goes below because the card written the evening before says *four*. He comes down the inner stair on his seat, Cael taking his heel at each step, and goes up it backward on his hands. On the 293rd he takes the whole stair alone, a quarter of an hour early, and is three steps from the iron at the bell. He uses the road's hand-signs (*seen*; *nothing?*) from his straw.
609:- **What Cleon learns of the 290th:** that Karis cut her hand on a stair ("Which lays?" / "The other."), and no more.
610:- **Cleon's speech at the board (r1; the three plan pieces fixed, the rest mine).** Of the tally-book: "A load came in at a gate. It was paid on, and when the day was done a clerk put it down. He might have begun at the head of his column or the foot of it; it'd be the same tally. There's not a mark on that leaf that's waiting for anything." Of the Stair's sheet: "Whoever cut this set each sign where it is for the sake of the one after it. Take them out of their order and you've a pile of chippings. Leave them in it, and you're handed on, sign to sign, whether you can read a letter of it or not. I don't read it. I'm walked down it." The word "bearer" is not used of signs. **"There" is Cleon's**, said as his finger stops above the last sign of the fifth row. **The acquisition as re-ordered:** Cael first sees the board's four rows go aslant at the tail of his eye and takes it for the lamp; checks the flame; then looks at the fifth row (*Goes*); then knows it and whose it is. The comparison with how the earlier fragments came is gone from this scene.
611:- **Rune from inside, as Cael has it.** The twelfth does not ask; it leans, and gives single words (*goes*, *sits*, *bears*, *nothing*). A made thing is looked at from where it stands on the ground toward where it is going, which is why the light belongs low and to the left. A run's lean "comes down flat" where it ends; Cleon can therefore mark where each run stops (**"red"** squares of tile between signs), which gives Karis the lengths of the words.
612:- **The decipherment, 291st–293rd.** Fifteen and sixteen on the 291st from a five-sign word and a second (the sixteenth is the old word for twenty, a score); seventeen at the bell. Eighteen to twenty-one on the 292nd. **Her first word that is not a number is *and*.** **The six-sign word that she had covered is *set*** ("as a stone is set, or a stake"); she had wished for a name. The twenty-second and twenty-third come from the leaf itself on the 293rd ("It spells them for me"). **(r1) Before she says "I can read it" she makes three unlike checks, not three passes:** one of the two new signs laid against a word in the last tall tally-book; a question to Cleon ("The fourth line… Does it sit, or does it go?" / "Sits. The lot of it sits. There's not an order on that skin from the head to the tail… Somebody's telling you what was done. He's not telling you to do it."); and a word near the leaf's foot covered with her glove, written out beforehand from the letters' use at the head, and then uncovered. She shows Vastin's copied page to Cleon only after the reading.
613:- **The refusal to turn it inward (r1).** One present choice, near the bell, after he has seen that he must tell Cleon: Oryn's order (*Write it down and stop touching it.*), which he "had got round… once already, at a waypost" and does not get round now. Cleon's limit is not recited beside it; it stands in the notice (*Not a man.*).
614:- **The telling (35.3).** Cael's own account of why Cleon may be told and Vastin still may not: the taking from Cleon would go on every hour at his elbow, and his own term was that he be told what a thing is once he has looked; the eleventh sits unused and cannot be told without the whole. Brom: "Tell him what's his. I'd not tell him ours." Seln, that night: "I should have said yes… I should have been a good deal longer about it."
618:- **The parcel.** The carrier with his hat cut away on one side lies the night of the 292nd at the inn on his way to the western landing with the bag; he knows Brom's back from that landing and breaks the bag "for a thumb that the old man knows" (Brom's thumb, in hub-grease, on his way-slip; a copper of Cael's own). The dividers: two brass legs a hand long, flat on the inner side, points of blued steel in a round of cork, a broad flat rivet, a turned stalk. Karis asks for the loan of them "when I have a hide in front of me again". Cael does not open them on any distance (his ruling of the 168th).
619:- **The meal.** Brom's ruling ("A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on"); four marks at the half-door; the lower room; a goose, a pie, a white loaf, baked apples, cider; seven cups poured, two kept under saucers (Karis, above with her letters; Oryn). **Purse 130.** **(r1) The cups line is Cael's:** Vastin says of the two absent women, "I have kept the count at a great many boards. I should have struck both"; Cael answers with the protected line, across the table; Vastin: "I see that it is… I was never shown the book." Brom fills his cup. (Vastin's remark over the dividers and Brom's "That's so" are cut.)
620:- **Seln's slip (r1: entered late).** Cael smells paper burning; a curl of black lies in the candle's dish under Seln's fingers, with one white corner and a line and a half of close writing on it before the flame has that too; "Closed." Lira: "What is?" / Seln: "It is in the dish." Cael did not see it come out of the sleeve or be read. **What was on it is not given.**
621:- **The light.** A thread of the crown's light has come in each night by a gap at the left of the blanket and lain on the loft's boards like a chalk line. On the night of the 292nd it thickens and thins for a count of one. Cael feels "the particular weight of an instrument turning toward him" (r1: the comparison with the eleven seconds in a chalk ring is cut by the coordinator's fix 37; nothing likens the light to a plate, a ring or a thing that attends) and answers himself ("A needle swings to iron. Nobody says that the needle has seen the iron"; a draught; a lamp-trimmer; his own tired eyes). He does not cross three strides of boards and does not look again. Lira crosses, without lifting the corner, and draws the blanket to the frame.
622:- **Oryn's fourth gate** (unnamed; r1: her scene now opens "On the evening of that same day, the two hundred and ninety-second, and four gates north of a fork-stone"; nine on the bench, the third of them a grandfather): a larger hold, stone to a man's head with split oak above. The hold's old woman, who keeps its days, rings the fourteen-year-olds on a threshing floor of beaten clay "at the first light after the moon's full", with a hazel stick. No office, plate or clerk. The girl (unnamed; a plait; a sheepskin too big) steps in; her eyes go to a place a hand's breadth from her cheek; she listens; her knees give a little; she tells her grandmother in her ear. Oryn sits forty yards off with her hands in her sleeves and lays a hand on nobody. "Whether it needed the ring, Oryn had never learned. Nobody at any of those gates had been willing to try it without." She has watched forty such. **Her decision:** the fifth gate has its day, the ford an hour, and she will not come late to the mill; and the reading she owes will go "as deep as she went", asked first. She is two days behind.
623:- **The leaf as a thing.** In a bundle between two thin boards of beech. A single leaf of skin the colour of old horn, stiff, curled at one corner, the lamp showing through it; written with a point and rubbed dark; twenty-six lines on its first face, in the floors' letters; a list on its back, not gone into. A little below the middle a place four or five letters wide scraped pale and thin, with one upright stroke left at its left side. Cleon: "One hand. One sitting. He ruled it first… a fair copy… scraped since it was written. I can't tell you by whom." Its head gives a year in the count that the old town's books keep. **Beneath the lower board: a dozen or more leaves of paper, a year-book of the town in its old ink hand** (floods, a murrain, who was reeve), which Karis reads at sight and puts later than the skin ("It speaks of the walls"). The chronicle's line stands on its first leaf beside a worm-hole or damp-hole a thumb wide.
624:- **The reading's joins (r1).** In the first half Karis's breath gives out "upon a comma" after "the rivers ran wrong" and she takes another without lifting her finger; nothing of the record is missing there. At the scraped place her finger stops above the one stroke and she says, in her own voice: "It is scraped. A word as wide as four of his letters, or five. I have the first stroke of it, and nothing after." After *The Architect* (said once, heard by Cael for the first time) the pencil's butt goes from the head of the skin to the pale place, and she says the title clause. **"Older. Made. Us." is Karis's, said aloud**, shutting three fingers into her palm "as a big man had counted findings at a spring". Vastin's reach is the restored line from the box.
625:- **Who heard it.** Karis, Cleon, Cael, Vastin. Lira and Brom were in the eastern gallery with the carried lamp, putting twelve tally-books back (Cleon would not have the tape touched while a book was out of its bay). Karis reads it once, asks that nobody say it back ("A thing that is said twice begins to be said differently"), and makes two copies; the fair one goes into Cael's coat.
626:- **The page the right way up (the §13 lock).** At the head of the inner stair on the evening of the 293rd, by the lamp in Lira's hand, Cael sees the first line of Karis's copy lying the right way up: "There was no desk on the other side of this."
628:- **The street (r1).** Both protected lines are Seln's. Cael, asked not to say the leaf over, gives two sentences "as a carter gives a load": "It's one man's work. All of it; the tiers, and the rest. He says so himself." / "Made," / "And he says who it was built against. It wasn't a house. It was a sort of people." / "For a kind." Seln says nothing for ten paces, with his left hand on the strap; then, "I have had two sentences, which is as much as I trust from any mouth."
629:- **The fire, as re-joined (r1).** Lira reads once; Cael turns his face away and watches eight bowls cool; she folds the page and says her line **to the fire**, before anything else; then the hole and the ruled box ("There's a stroke in the hole." / "Yes."); then she puts her thumb on the place through the paper and looks across at him. What her line does to him is told while Brom reads. **Seln (only "I'll keep it a while longer." fixed):** "There is a house on that hill which would give a great deal to have it laid upon its ledge; and I have asked myself on a good many nights what it was that I kept it out of… I had only ever seen that house from the inside of its own books. It stands upon this. I know now what the office is for, which I did not know at noon; and I had rather know it and carry the thing, than carry it blind." No count of years; the case on his chest, shut; nothing in it named. **Cael sits** on the fifth bucket when Seln hands the page back, so that Vastin's "Who's sitting across the fire" is so, and **gets up off it** for pole two. **Vastin's joins:** "I held that for the whole of my service, and was easy in it. Above Ostrand I was shown that the fault was meant. I called it corrupt; I put down a seal upon that word, and walked three hundred miles on it, and thought that I had come to the floor." He lifts his eyes from the fire only for the last piece. Before "I would have.": "He could have left it there, and it would have been true." **Pole two's joins:** "He had breath left for three words." / "It was Lira, and she had not taken her eyes off him since he got up." / "He stopped there, since the rest of it was not a plan."
630:- **The yard fire.** The stable's iron fire-basket on three legs, by the stall door; Brom's flint; upturned oak buckets; eight bowls ("I'd laid one for a mule before now"). Cleon comes in by the yard, having asked a keeper which street; he sits with his back half to the flame and eats half a bowl. Asked by Lira whether it is true: "I read how a thing's made… it's as old as it says it is… Whether he's told the truth in it, I can't say." His undertaking, to which Brom says "Good.": "That skin stays where it's lain… they'll not learn it from me." Brom's price: whatever was built was built over the whole country and down to every child; "Nobody spends so on a thing that he thinks is small… A price isn't a verdict… I'd want to hear the other side." After Lira's "You would have." she empties the old man's cold bowl into the pot and fills it hot. Cael does not write what he said.
631:- **Roles (all unnamed):** the beam-man; the two watchmen; the keepers of the blue gate by night; the carrier with the cut hat (from Book 7); the hold's old woman, the girl in her year and her small brother; a girl in a red cap and a boy who laughed (Vastin's memory of kindlings he signed).
636:**Calendar.** The night of the **two hundred and ninety-third day past the Line**, in the long loft at Treswick. Days on the page: 291 (the ladder; the carts' street; the beam; the hatch; the gallery; the inside; the twelfth; the telling; the stair), the night of 291–292 (the tail), 292 (below; the parcel; the meal; the inventory; the light), Oryn at her fourth gate from dusk on the 292nd to first light on the 293rd, 293 (the leaf; the chronicle; the copy; Vastin's afternoon and stair; the street; the yard fire). No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
638:**Where everyone is.** Five in the loft (Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln at the ladder's head) and Vastin in the end stall beneath. Cleon has gone back up through the blue gate; the leaf, the chronicle and the board are under the registry; the fifty-two sheets lie on their tables one night more. Oryn and the brown mule are between her fourth gate and her fifth. Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
640:**Cael.** Nineteen. **Twelve fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** The twelfth is acquired, read, told to Cleon, and held shut; three unmeant glances, not counted; never on a person, the crown, the leaf or himself. He keeps Cleon's warning and has told nobody. He has ruled that the twelfth is not turned inward until Oryn has been asked. He has not set foot on the square or its steps and has not looked at the crown; one unsteady moment was seen on the loft's boards and entered fourth on the page of the inventory, ruling *hold*. He has stood at the blue gate's groove by night and gone back down. He has heard the leaf, carried its copy, and said pole two aloud on his feet at the fire; it is not in the log. **Vastin is still not told of the eleventh**; the debt is entered as heavier. He owes Oryn two tellings at the mill (the readings round the place; the twelfth). Body: sound; tar on his palms; eyes recovered.
642:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. Arm: day twenty-eight out of the wool on the 293rd; it steadied a page and drew a blanket along two nails. Right shoulder still bruised. She was told first and read the page first. She has twice been refused something Cael had no leave to give (the warning; the leaf before the fire) and has said so. She closed the gap by the blanket without looking. Her registry paper is untouched.
644:**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. Neither arm above the elbow; the box on the bound right shoulder; he was the handcart's horse and brake. He put his thumb to a carrier's slip. **The purse: a hundred and thirty.** He means to buy the rope before the gate. Owed at the mill to two healers.
646:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; no point struck since the fork (she did not light the yard fire). **All twenty-three letters.** She has read the first face of the leaf aloud once, given one account of it, shown Vastin's copied page to Cleon, marked one line of the chronicle and not read on, and made two copies with the gap ruled as an empty box and the one stroke drawn. She has kept two pages in the chamber and at the fire. She has not gone into the leaf's back (a list). Left palm: stitches in, under linen; she has begun to use the hand without noticing.
648:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He taught a second person (Cael: three rules of following). He has read the page. The case is on him, **shut**; at the fire he said that a house on the hill would give a great deal to have it laid on its ledge, that he knows now what the office is for, and "I'll keep it a while longer." He was told on the street in two sentences and answered "Made." and "For a kind." He burned a slip and said "Closed." He will not follow the boots again. He expects them on the road east.
650:**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd**; slat-bound; he rides the cart, sleeps in a stall, and takes the inner stair on his seat. He kept silent at the beam after one sentence. He heard the leaf; reached once aloud and was stopped; has stopped the same sentence in his own head; has said his piece at the fire (imperfect; the fault meant, which he called corrupt; aimed) and answered Lira ("I would have."). He was told across a dinner table that out here one counts who is owed a seat. He does not know the mechanism, the eleventh, the twelfth, the sum, or what is under the bar. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it.
652:**Cleon.** Rune, Bronze. He has shown the inside of the Path to Karis; has been told by Cael how the twelfth came; refused to finish a sentence; gave a cup of water and a warning; looks away from the boy on purpose and has kept it for two days and an hour at a fire. He has heard the leaf that lay on his shelf ten years. He has been above ground with the crew once. He has said that the leaf stays under his hand and goes to no counter. Tomorrow: the list of parts.
654:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. Four gates done of five; two days behind. She watched a Kindling from a bench and laid hands on nobody. Nobody has died under her hands. She has heard of no leaf. She has decided not to come late to the mill and to read Cael as deep as she goes, asked first.
660:**Objects and money.** **130 marks** (134 less four for the dinner). The reserve sealed in the third box. Hesk's dividers are shut in Hesk's book; his note with them. The fair copy of the leaf is in Cael's hand; the other in Karis's notebook. The stable's handcart is theirs while the loft is (to the 295th). Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman.
662:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before; **Cleon knows how one fragment came**; not Vastin or the clerk. The twelfth: the six (Oryn not yet) and Cleon. The warning: Cael and Cleon. The leaf: Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin by ear; Lira, Brom and Seln from the page; not Oryn, Teague or Pike. The unsteady light: Cael and Lira (from the log). The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone, unchanged.
664:**Open threads.** The back of a leaf. A line with a hole in it, under a slip. A second page with nothing on it. A warning that is in no book. A debt to a man in a stall. Two tellings owed at a mill. A light that was not steady once. Boots that may be on the road east. A case kept a while longer. A rope to buy before the gate.
667:Movement 7 already carries the 294th, all-day list, twelve fragments, Karis's bound hand, Vastin day fifteen and no mule (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:28–30`). Its author must additionally inherit:
669:- **Leaf audience and wording discipline.** Karis, Cleon, Cael and Vastin heard the record once; Lira, Brom and Seln read Karis's page; Oryn has not heard it (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:3–29,95–105,251–269`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:3–37,53–103`). Nobody who heard it quotes it. The surviving public reductions are *made*, *for a kind*, Lira's existence conclusion, Vastin's *aimed/category/person*, and Cael's built-to-prevent conclusion (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:179–189`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:37,135,187` after fixes 10, 12 and 14).
670:- **Fragments and knowledge.** Twelve fragments; deployment thirty-two. The eleventh remains unused and known to Lira through the log but not Vastin. The twelfth is known to Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis, Seln and Cleon; Oryn and Vastin have not been told. Cleon knows how this one fragment came but no other acquisition history. The warning is known only to Cael and Cleon and is in no book (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:197–239,279–339`; `manuscript/chapter-36.md:15–63`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:219–227`). **Coordinator ruling (overrides the audit here):** the twelfth leans at made things UNBIDDEN and OUTWARD in Movement 6 (the board, the rope-walk, the bridge, the dividers, the inn table), and Cael is learning not to look; a few unmeant outward glances are on the page and are not counted as deployments. It has never been turned INWARD, and he refused that turn once, deliberately, until Oryn has been asked. Movement 7's beat is the first INWARD turn, and it comes unbidden.
671:- **Cleon.** He has shown the Path's inside, been told the twelfth's mechanism, declined a dangerous sentence, given the warning, deliberately looked away for two days, promised the leaf stays under his hand and above no counter, and said “Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts.” (`manuscript/chapter-35.md:15–239`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:115–125,227–239`). He let the fifty-two sheets lie one extra night and has written the 294th card (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–125`).
672:- **Object positions at night on the 293rd.** The original skin leaf is between its beech boards under Cleon's jar at his desk; the paper chronicle remains under the registry with its hole slipped/marked; the shelf bundle is untied and the leaf's back/list is unread (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:31–43,163–193`; `manuscript/chapter-39.md:137`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:289`). Karis's fair copy is in Cael's hand/coat after Seln returns it; Karis retains the second copy in her notebook (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:123–127`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249–251`). The fifty-two sheets lie on their three tables one night more, and the black-oak plank remains against the third table (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:117–121`; inherited location `STATE_LEDGER.md:552`). The three boxes, flat and hide are back in the loft; the bearing is on the calfskin in the second box, the reserve stays sealed in the third, and Cael sits against the third (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`). Vastin is in the end stall; the tarred kit case is last explicit in his hands on the returning cart, and the prose does not state whether it finishes in the stall or loft (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:173–205`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–265`). M7 must not silently choose a different custody if the case matters.
673:- **Bodies.** Vastin's right knee is day fourteen of thirty on the 293rd, still slat-bound, with stick/no kneeling/no running/no Wall; he sleeps in the end stall and can take only the inner stair on his seat/hands (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:53–113`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:253–263`). Karis's left palm remains stitched and bound; she writes right-handed but has begun to steady/use the left without noticing (`manuscript/chapter-38.md:227–247`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:249`). Brom still cannot raise either arm above the elbow; Lira is day twenty-eight out of wool on the 293rd (`manuscript/chapter-34.md:71–79`; `manuscript/chapter-37.md:281–285`).
674:- **Money, lodging and tools.** Purse 130; reserve sealed in the third box; loft/handcart through the 295th; sixty yards of four-strand still owed. Hesk's dividers and note are shut in Hesk's book with Cael (`manuscript/chapter-37.md:95–99,141–143,267–273`; `manuscript/chapter-40.md:267–289`).
676:- **Seln and the case.** The case remains on him and shut; its contents remain unnamed. He has read the leaf copy and decided the case is worth keeping “a while longer” (`manuscript/chapter-40.md:91–105` after fix 11). His cache decision is not a declaration or disclosure.
677:- **Light and date.** Cael alone saw the unsteady line; Lira knows only the written claim and closed the blanket gap. The ruling is *hold* (`manuscript/chapter-37.md:239–285`). The deep-layer date remains known only and separately to Cael and Seln, unsaid and unwritten (`manuscript/chapter-36.md:21–23`; `BOOK_MAP.md:339`).
678:- **Reading orientation.** At the inner stair head Cael reads Karis's page right way up for the first time; M7 must preserve that change (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:143–163`; `BOOK_MAP.md:475`).
680:**Movement 6 CLOSED (2026-10-12).** Chapters 34–40, days 291–293, 36,152 words. Same-author repair r1; Sol recheck (gpt-5.6-sol, 372k tokens): CLOSE, no line fixes — every protected unit whole, once and on the right speaker; all thirteen former joins judged the author's own and source-distant; the five tracked passages RESOLVED; overlap 0 unprotected / 27 protected; gates 0; every chapter inside the three working ranges; listening pass on all seven chapters. Recheck rulings on repair-minted items, all KEEP: Cleon's "Sits. The lot of it sits." classifies the leaf's visible FACE as a record and makes no claim about the unread back; Vastin's fire join is supported clause by clause in closed text; Cael sits on the unused bucket before Vastin speaks and rises for pole two. Text is closed for the movement, NOT locked: the book's completion pass (whole-arc read, texture lanes, listening proof) may still touch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:43:It had gone into him like a blade laid flat. He had been ready for a harder thing and a softer one, and not for a true one, said plainly, by the one person who had known him when he had nothing in him but a word in square brackets. Every clerk who had ever looked at him over a desk had looked as at a thing that might go off. He had taken it so, and had built himself, a year at a time, into somebody who would not: who stood in corners, and kept twelve doors shut, and told a Path no at a tar barrel with a man five yards away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:47:When Brom had done he did not read it again; he handed it up to Seln, across the fire, and sat for some time with his forearms on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:83:Seln had read the page standing, as a man reads who has copied other people's papers for twelve years: once, from the head to the foot, without a change in his face, and Cael did not think that he would ever need to see it a second time. He folded it in three, on its own creases, and did not give it back at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:175:He thought of Hesk's book, in his coat, against his ribs, with a pair of brass legs shut in its pages. Ever since a plate lit he had taken whatever was too large for him and put it there, a line at a time, in the order of its coming. He had done so with a word on a plate, and with twelve fragments, and with a place that did not move. He knew exactly how this would go down, if he let it: *Claim. Evidence. Ruling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:243:Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:259:They got Karis up the ladder between them, Lira above and Cael below, more asleep than awake, with her notebook held against her in both arms and the bound hand uppermost. She said one thing on the rungs, quite clearly: she said that there were twenty-three, and that she had them all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:277:*Two hundred and ninety-third day. Treswick; the yard, and then the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty. A leaf read once, below, by Karis. A copy made. Read at a fire by the three who had not heard it. The reader came up, and ate half a bowl. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:57:"If it goes, I shall sit down, which I shall be doing in any case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:It was a hundred and ten strides to the cross-way, on the stick and the uprights. He put the strap of the tarred case over his head, so that it hung at his back. Then he turned about at the foot of the first flight, and let himself down, and began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:83:He had thought, through all of that, that he was dealing with an error. A plate had failed upon one child, as a scale fails upon a weight that is out of its range. It had been his whole understanding of the case, and he had built a resignation and a road upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:At the twenty-fourth step he could go no farther without a rest, and he sat with the case against his shoulder and listened to four people climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:111:They did not hurry him. A brown light came up over the second landing, and a big man's shadow with it. Nobody said that he had done well. He was glad of that. A pair of hands that he knew took the foot of the tarred case, without a word, and bore it up for the last three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:139:He took the foot of the case for the last three steps. Nobody spoke of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:173:Cael watched him do it. It took no longer than the count of five. The mild eyes went to Brom, between the shafts, who was looking at his own boots; and to Karis, at the wheel, with her notebook held in both arms; and to an old man on the boxes, facing backward, as grey as the wall. They went to Lira, who shook her head by the width of a finger, since she did not yet know. They came last to Cael, and rested.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:199:They came down the street with the brake on, which was Brom, walking backward into the hill. The roofs of the district went by on the left hand, grey, with their smoke going up straight. Cael looked at them. He had looked at them on every journey by that road, to keep his eyes off a wall; and he found, this evening, that he had no eyes to spare for a wall in any case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-39.md:207:In the lane the four alleys had lost their white. He pushed through four bars of a paler light, with a shoulder against his own at each; and he did not look at them, and he did not think about them. He looked at a pair of raw hands on a tarred case, a yard from his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:5:It was Brom's rope, and Brom's knot, a loop under the arms that would not draw tight. He had taken a turn of the line round the post at the ladder's head and sat down against it, with his heels on the boards, since he could not lift either arm and did not need to. Lira went down first, to mind the bound leg, and Cael stood on the rungs beneath a brown coat, as he had stood beneath it on a good many stairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:75:Brom had put himself between the shafts. He could not lift, and he could not haul with his arms; but he could lean, and there was nobody in that town who leaned as he did. He took the shafts low, at his hips, with the webbing of his right shoulder showing at his collar, and walked into the weight as into a wind. Cael and Lira pushed at the tail. Karis went beside the wheel, with the flat. On the three boxes, facing backward, with the tarred case across his thighs and the stick laid along it, sat a person who had come through that gate four times on horseback, to be met.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:111:It was two hundred paces, under the wall, and he had walked it fourteen times with his eyes on Brom's heels. He had no heels to look at this morning. Brom was in front of a cart, and the cart was as high as Cael's chest with an old man on it; so he looked at the tail-board, and at a pair of raw hands laid on a tarred case, and pushed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:141:It was the question that had been asked of Cael in every building that he had entered since a plate lit; and it had never been asked of a box. He felt, at his back, on the cart's rail, a pair of raw hands shut upon a tarred case. He did not turn round. He heard the old man breathe in, as a man breathes who has the whole answer by heart, with its number; and breathe out again, slowly, through his nose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:155:They went into the pan one at a time, since Lira could lift and Cael could, and the beam's arm came down to each with a long iron groan. The man slid his weight along the bar with one finger, and chalked three figures, and a fourth for the case; and beside the four of them he wrote a name in a round slow hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:207:"Three," he said. "And the case." The red eyes had stopped at a boot that hung. They went up the slat, and no higher. "That's slower than it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-34.md:299:Karis brought it, flat on her forearms. Her face had gone very still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:The man in the dusty coat had ruled it from his chair, on the two hundred and ninety-third morning, before any of them had a load off. It was the seventh day of his week; there were twelve of the old town's tally-books lying open along a table's edge, and he would not have a tape touched while one of them was out of its bay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:155:"It gives its own year, at the head," she said. "It is in the count that the old town's books keep, and I have had twelve of those open for a week. By that count this was written about four hundred years ago."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:35:Brom had come up as far as the table's corner, and stopped there, with his arms hanging. Lira sat against a table's leg with her chin on her knees. On a box by the nearest bay, a long grey man had turned his head toward the two things on the table, and his hands had gone still on the ash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:37:Karis was not looking at the sheet any longer. She had turned, on the spot where she had been put, and her eyes were going down the gallery: over the Hall's twelve, and the long table with its three rows, and the Ring's eleven beyond. She looked at them as she had once looked at a bluff from six miles off, counting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She was at the tall book before he had done. Cael held the flame for her, a hand to the left, and watched her pencil go down the old clerk's column, hunting a word of five with those four in their places. She found it in the time that it takes to draw a long breath; and she found it again, twelve lines lower, and only then did she write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:131:He had said that he would. A person who must climb as he had to climb did well to begin early. He had gone off down the gallery on the stick, hand over hand along the black uprights, with a civil refusal for each of the three who offered an arm. He had left the tarred case leaning at the foot of the first flight. Cael heard him before he saw the place: a slow scrape, and a breath, and a scrape, somewhere up in the dark of the first flight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:The big man asked nothing else. He got up, and turned his bound shoulder to his own load, and swung it there in one heave; and Cael found the third box by its beckets and got it onto his back. The tarred case stood against the wall where it had been left, and he took it up by its strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:"I left the case. I regret it. I took it up at a fork, against a healer's word, and I have not set it down for anybody since; but I find that I cannot go up a stair on my hands and carry a thing in them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:291:Cael held the case by its foot, and the old man had the strap's end shut in his fist. At every tread the two raw hands went to the stone, and the sound leg thrust, and the whole length of him was hitched one step higher into the dark, backward, with the case drawn after him like a thing being brought up out of a well. They were face to face, and Cael could not see the face. He could hear it breathe, a yard above his own, and he bore up the weight of a cord and a mallet and one stake, so that it should not pull upon that hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:305:He put two lines of his own under the twelve, since they were owed, and since he wished to have them by heart before there was a pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:325:Cael had set his boot on the nineteenth tread, with the case, and the lamp was above him, eight steps up, in Lira's hand. It was a small brown flame in a smoked glass. He had knelt beside it for six days. It went into his eyes now like a handful of sand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:337:"I do not ask," said the old man. There was a silence, and a scrape, and the case drew upward by the height of one step. "There are eight more. Each is of the same rise; I have had occasion to learn them today. Put your right foot up. Higher. There."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-35.md:341:It was given a step at a time, in the level voice in which a clerk reads a column: the right foot, and the left, and that the tread was dry. For eight days Cael had gone two steps below that stick, so that there should be something under it if it fell. He had not thought to find the thing turned about. He bore up his end of the case, and put his boots where he was told, and was brought up out of the dark by a person who could not stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:13:He had gone through this once already, with another matter. For eleven days he had not looked at a light on a crown, and he had not done it by shutting his eyes. He had done it by having somewhere else to put them, and a reason, twice a day, until his neck no longer set at an alley. He did the same now. He looked at a raw hand on a tarred case, which nobody had made, and at the back of a girl's neck, and pushed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:113:It had come round before he could stop it. Both legs hung from that one pin; he saw the whole of the thing lean inward to it, the two brass arms and the steel at their feet, as everything on a gate leans to its hinge. It was true, and it was no more than his eyes could have told him. The sand came into them all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:143:"A hundred and thirty," he said, to the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:217:*Treswick; the loft over the stable; one in the stall. The two hundred and ninety-second day. Eight days inside. A hundred and thirty: four to a dinner, on Brom's ruling, and worth it. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:283:"Twenty-seven days out of the wool," she said, of the arm that had done it. "It's good for something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:297:"My daughter's girl is in her year. Fourteen since the hay. We ring them on the threshing floor, at the first light after the moon's full, and it was full last night." She had her arms folded in her apron. "It's never gone amiss here. But her mother's mother went down like a felled calf, at hers, and lay till noon. It's in the family to take it hard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:339:The old woman was over the line before the child's knees had begun to go. They did go, a little. She was caught, and held, with a grey head bent to hers. Oryn, on a bench forty yards off, saw the girl's mouth move against her grandmother's ear, three or four words, quick, and saw the old arms tighten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:359:The old woman had come across the clay. She stood by the bench with her arms folded, and her eyes were wet, and she was not troubling to hide it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:*Two hundred and ninety-first day. Treswick; the loft; one in the stall. A hundred and thirty-four. By the carts' street, both ways, on wheels: a weight chalked at the bottom gate to the name on the card, and to nobody else. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:179:"He chose it because of that. I should have." The grey eyes went along the roof. "I shall stay at the wheel, in case he has only gone in to come out again. You will go round by the open side, behind the drying-racks, to the barrels. Go softly, and be there before he is. And when you are there, you are a barrel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:209:The lantern burned on its post at the head of the quay steps, and under it the river went by with a noise like cloth being drawn over a table. He could see the shed's mouth, a square of blacker dark, twelve yards off; and nothing came out of it, and there was no sound of boots on boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:333:He could have crossed it; he looked at that squarely, with the fire on his face. There was a thing at his knees that would have had him through the small door on anybody's heels, and neither of those two would have known that the air had moved. There was a hiss in his feet which made nothing of walls. He had twelve things in him, and not a keeper in that arch could have named one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:379:*S.: they have time. Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-16.md:175:He said it without any sourness, as a clerk enters a balance that he does not yet know how to settle. Then he asked the hold's woman, who was passing with a pitcher, where a guest might sleep who was on nobody's sheet, and paid her a copper for the answer, and went out by himself into the yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:19:It went out to the left and stayed there. Cael looked where it went, and for the space of several strides he saw only the mist and the heather; then a piece of the mist moved against the rest, and he had the shape of it. It was low, and long in the back, and it carried its head stretched out level. It was going along the slope at a walk, forty-five yards off, at exactly the pace of a mule, and not one sprig of heather stirred round its legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:"They all know what a road is. They come up from the river quiet as a burying, two days, and never a finger lit; I've had Iron men walk through that gate who'd let a beast die in the shafts on the moor sooner than bring up the grey. They know. Then they come into my cut." A hand went out flat, and tilted. "There's a seep at the head of it. It comes down the middle of the road, a stride wide, and from the first frost till the last it's glass from top to bottom, in a place the sun never finds. A wheel goes onto that. Then the cart begins to go, backward, down a hill, with a year's goods on her and a man's own two beasts in front. What does the man do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:257:He went out. Seln got down from the sill. He had the second hazel wand in his hand, with its forty-seven notches, and he laid it on the deal, under the lamp, beside the broker's sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:"I've a bench at first light," she said, and it was plainly not what she had been going to say. "And a knee that nobody's asked me about." She went out, and did not shut the door behind her, since she never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:289:She went out to help with the boxes. He heard her say something to Brom in the next room, and Brom answer, and a rope begin to go round a corner of wood, turn upon turn, with a large man counting under his breath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day, night. The first boat brought no paper for us. It brought one man, who was the Archmarshal and is not. He held the ferry on her cable with his back. He has handed over three things: six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet. The town is the one that she named. The price is what it was. I said that I would rule when the boat was in. Tomorrow, at the plank, with hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:47:Cael had seen Paths used on floors for half his life. He had seen fire run between nine points, and a girl leave one place and arrive in another, and a Gold lay the air down in lanes; and every one of those had been a thing that went out and did something. This did nothing whatever. It was a plane, as flat as a rule and as thin as a thought. It had been drawn on the ground and stood on end. It kept the ground's own refusal to be walked through, and it would have been all one to it whether a moth struck it or a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:77:She was sitting on the scree, ten feet up, with her heels dug in. He had not seen her go there. She had a plate of the dark stone in her two hands, as broad as a trencher. She sent it down the slope flat, like a boy skimming a pond; and where it struck, the whole skin of the bank let go. A yard of scree went out from under two hounds together and carried them down to the road in a rattle of plates, on their sides, with their legs going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:165:The ice went in the same breath. The crack that the wall had made lay across the runnel like the cut of a knife. Under a heap of that weight, the glass on the uphill side of it broke up in plates of its own, and what was beneath came up black. There was water under it. The seep had been running there all the cold term, a stride wide and a hand deep, under its own roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:Pressure drew and filled under his breastbone, slowly, like a bellows under a tired arm. It went out of him narrow and deep, and it came back at him off anything curved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:141:"I did not hope for it. If that plate lit, then I had sat four feet from you for three days, with everything that I have, and had seen nothing of a thing that a sheet of brass could find in eleven seconds. I should have had to ask what my eyes were worth, and to ask it of nine thousand readings, each of which I had sworn to." His breath went out. "So I could afford neither answer: the one would have been turned upon you, and the other upon me. I weighed the pair of them for the better part of a night; and I found that it was the second which kept me from sleeping, and that a better man would have been kept awake by the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:81:Cael had told a board that he would rule when the first boat was in, since a desk might have changed the price. The boat was in. He had the price now from the office that used to certify such things, and it was last year's price, to the copper. Nothing about it had altered but this: a second person had come to the same town's name, by another road, and had not known that it was already on their table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:"I felt it, at three of those lines. So did four of you. There was no dimming. It went out at one stride, for a third rank and a first and a Copper alike, and came back at the same stride, whole. It was not as a person tires. It was as a row of lamps—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-17.md:113:"I was about to tell you what a thing is from how it went out in me. I was the ruler, at those lines; I had no other. I shall not finish that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:211:A raw hand in linen went out toward it, from the side where a stick was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:9:It was wider than he had judged from the rise, and it ran brown and quick with the thaw of a whole moor in it. A bridge went over, long and low, with five piers standing in the water. He counted them twice, since Karis would ask. The roadway was only wide enough for one cart, with a bay built out over each pier where a person on foot could stand aside; and beyond the last pier there were two square towers with an arch between them, and the grey wall going away on both hands along the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-28.md:163:"I was doing it for the second day, and I did it rather well, and I had begun to think it learned." The breath went out of him. "It appears that a thing is not learned until it has been tried by the one matter that you care for. I can keep my tongue while I am counted. I had not yet been asked to keep it while a form was filled wrongly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:139:They ran the whole length of the wall's foot, under the pale, close-jointed and without a thread of mortar; and where a pale block had been set upon a dark one, the mason had cut it to lie snug, one stone at a time. She did not say what it was. She had said it once already, on a river bank. Her gloved hand went out, and came back, and closed on the flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:271:It shut with no more noise than a book makes. The clerk's lantern went out of the world, and the feet overhead, and the last grey thread of the day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:91:It struck the edge of the ninth step, once, with the whole of a tall man's weight upon it. The sound was not the sound of ash on stone. He had heard it on a moor. A line went out from the ferrule to either wall, along the lip of the tread, as a crack runs across ice. Then the air stood up from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:165:It went from under her. She sat down hard on the stair, and the flat did not leave her arm. It was her left hand that went out, flat, to save the rest of her, and found the lip of the step above.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:273:The lantern jerked sideways. It struck the rail, and fell on the top step with its shutter still closed, and rolled, and went out inside itself with a small flat cough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:295:"I counted. Four went up, and two went out by the gate. That's all of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-32.md:299:There was no frost to fall. The place in front of him that the eye had slid off was only a stair again, with two bolts lying on it; and the roar went out of the slot as a fire goes out when the door is shut on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-33.md:37:"The four went by the lane," he said, "two east and two west, at a walk, and they had their hoods back before the first corner. I did not see a face. The two below went out by the carts' gate, which had been left upon the latch for them." He stopped a step above the old man, and looked at him for some time. "I could take one of the four, and I took the one who was there to be believed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:61:Cael went out an hour after noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:363:"She never needed me. That's the best morning I've had on this road since the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:185:The man went out by the street door. He went a full stride beyond it, onto the paving, into the noon, with one hand coming up already to shade his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:95:They went out by the door where the muck went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:257:The quay was long and bare. Six flat boats lay along it, nose to tail, with their sweeps shipped. Beyond them the great wheel of the hoist stood up against the water, and beyond that again a cable went out over the river into the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:19:When the lantern went out he lay with his hands behind his head and listened to the loft, as he had listened to every room that he had ever slept in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:The half-door was shut and barred at that hour, and the woman who leaned on it was asleep. He went out by the carriage-arch, past the painted door. Its lamp burned, and the book lay open on its stand within, with a pen on a chain, for whoever came late and had a name to give.
