editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:He was honest enough to see what he was about. He had an order from the one person whose orders he kept, and he was reading it for the leave that he wanted in it, with its author three weeks and a moor away from telling him that he had read it wrong. A registrar would have been proud of him. He would write that down as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:81:Cael had told a board that he would rule when the first boat was in, since a desk might have changed the price. The boat was in. He had the price now from the office that used to certify such things, and it was last year's price, to the copper. Nothing about it had altered but this: a second person had come to the same town's name, by another road, and had not known that it was already on their table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:165:The ice went in the same breath. The crack that the wall had made lay across the runnel like the cut of a knife. Under a heap of that weight, the glass on the uphill side of it broke up in plates of its own, and what was beneath came up black. There was water under it. The seep had been running there all the cold term, a stride wide and a hand deep, under its own roof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day, night. The first boat brought no paper for us. It brought one man, who was the Archmarshal and is not. He held the ferry on her cable with his back. He has handed over three things: six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet. The town is the one that she named. The price is what it was. I said that I would rule when the boat was in. Tomorrow, at the plank, with hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:187:"It'll keep three weeks. I'm not easy about it. I'd sooner you knew that I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-19.md:239:"It's seven," said Brom. "I'll lay hers. Somebody can carry it empty for three weeks, and I'd as soon that it were me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:267:And there was a thing that she had not got to at all. She had walked away from it at a fork two days south, with her sleeve half pushed up, and had told a boy that it would keep three weeks. She had read a hundred bodies since she first laid hands on his. She knew a wrist with a stone in it when she met one. She had met something in him a long while back that everything went round, as that carter's current went round a bad join; and she had not been able to say then whether it was a join, or a stone, or a thing that had always lain there. She had told him to leave it be. Then she had walked north, owing him the one reading that might have told her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:73:It was three weeks, less three days. She did not like to owe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:25:- **Letters/animals:** Hesk's brass and note and Oryn's bark have arrived. Letters to Hesk and Vell went west on the last ferry; Ephram's is deferred to the first boat of the thaw. The brown mule is with the five. Oryn has the grey jenny and the thirty-foot three-strand rope. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 4]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:26:- **Oryn:** last located at Thornwater, with route work north next. The old debt ran Cael→Oryn and is **PAID**: three self-readings and the full telling. Her withheld answer was never a debt. Her exact letter is: `Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.` Lira's arm waits on Oryn's in-person word. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 4]
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:58:6. **KEEP — Thornwater (265th–266th).** The ford in thaw. Oryn's yard. Lira's arm is released ("Five months and twenty days." [OWNER-pending #56] / "That's a season. Take it off."). Lira asks, for the first time since Norhold: "Will you mend it." / "Lie still". The ankle is mended. "Thank you." / "Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks." The map on Thornwater's table: "That's a line." Cael tells her about the cord: "I don't know what that is. Neither do you. Write that down too." Oryn comes down because Lowmarch and Millrace are her first stops after the thaw [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6]. Nobody asked her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:60:8. **KEEP — The ferry (277th).** Vastin arrives alone, in a brown coat, with an ash stick, a pack mended with cord and a bad knee. "Not that. Not for some months. Vastin will do; it did for my father." Pike prices him: "What'd he do?" → "Then he's nothing yet." He pays a copper and a half for bread. Teague reads his hands a step longer than a crew looks. Lira's face for men in grey doesn't fit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:61:9. **KEEP — The long table (277th midday).** The Seln–Vastin doorway exchange, their first words ever. The short thing: resigned or walked. Brom: "Three hundred miles is a demonstration." The layer: Karis supplies its existence; Vastin's new fact is "Cannot. There is no key." The name is a citation to a designer, and the trail dead-ends at Treswick. Vastin has nothing to do afterwards. "Why us." Oryn on the knee: "Then I still haven't asked." "What follows you": nothing that can cross the Line. Cael remembers the Book 6 sentence and says only: "Ask him the thing you want to ask him." Karis asks; Vastin's close (§10). The ferry goes west.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:62:10. **KEEP — The handover (277th evening)**, in Vastin's stated order. First, the records: a courier's flat of six years of survey abstracts, the anomaly column ("Total"), six rows, "More than four", and the completion report as the last sheet. Second, the page: "—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—". Third, the technician's sheet: the deep-layer line and the brush bar; Karis copies it and writes "not read". Seln goes still and does not say the sum. Vastin's vertigo; Karis owns the response. Cael's hand stays a hand's width from the page. Nobody says *west*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:64:12. **KEEP — The cut (279th).** Iron Wall is first shown: a plane across the road, rooted, and therefore loud. Brom fights silent with a rock. Lira works the scree. Cael bursts away, then goes still on the scree; the pack turns back and breaks on the wall. The heap. Seln in the heap. Five dead and three into the sink. Costs: Brom's foot; **Vastin's knee**, bound by Oryn for "a month". The lesson: "The wall held the line. And someone else had to be the reason they wanted the line." / "You do now." The log (§10).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:112:| **NEW — The ferry's first landing** (277th, high water) | quiet landing | 1,500 | Rope and flood. Vastin is seen holding a physical line, with no Path, before anyone knows it is him. Iron Wall must not show before the cut. |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:130:- **The week before the ferry (270th–276th)** (≈4,000). Tents return to the meadow. Teague's crew prepares for the road after the thaw. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 6] Oryn's first Lowmarch stop: patients at the plank, seen from the outside.
