 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:23:"I've nothing to hold."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:"Then hold nothing. Stand there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:51:It lay a little below the middle, where for the width of four or five letters the skin was paler, and thinner, and rough, as a board is rough where a plane has gone against the grain. Something had been taken out of that line with an edge. Whoever did it had been thorough; and had stopped, all the same, a hair too soon. At the left side of the pale place a single stroke was left standing. It was upright, and as long as a letter is tall, and it led to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:53:The thing behind Cael's eyes had come round to that leaf as it had come round to nothing since a plank of oak, and it leaned as a dog leans on a leash.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:79:Cael counted, since there was nothing else for him to do. He counted the lines on the skin, by the pale place, and made them twenty-six. From the eastern gallery, a long way off and round a corner of rock, there came now and then the small dry knock of a book going home upon a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:"It is scraped," she said, in her own voice. "A word as wide as four of his letters, or five. I have the first stroke of it, and nothing after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:143:"You made it a term that I should tell you nothing of what I thought until you had looked. I have kept this from you for nine days by that term, and from myself. It is a line that a man copied out of a register, a long way from here, and carried three hundred miles." She did not look toward the box. "It sends its reader to the lower archive at this town for a thing that is held here in full. And it has a word in it, which I had never met."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:He went over it as he would have gone over a cut on a moor, from the left. There was a desk of black oak, sloped, and a lamp on an iron arm, with the oil a finger down. On the shelf above stood a cup, which he had drunk from, and a jar; and a clean square in the dust. Two lengths of brown tape lay out to either side of a leaf of skin with twenty-six lines on it. There were two chairs, of which one had a cushion that had been carried from the other. A man stood with his eyes shut. A woman stood with a strip of a shirt round her left hand, and the hand was flat on the wood, and it had left off shaking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:"There is what that leaf says. It says it in its own words, of its own time, and I shall copy it on one page, and nothing else shall go upon that page." She took a clean leaf from her notebook, and laid it on the desk, bare. "And there is this. What you were about to say is not on the skin. It may be so. I do not know, and nor do you. If it is ever written, it will be written here, when somebody can show it; and until then this page has nothing on it, and nobody is to rule a line from the one to the other because a word stands in both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:239:"I was drafting," said Vastin. His voice had very little breath behind it. "I was told at that same spring, by another person, what it is when I do so. I was drawing a conclusion, in a room where I have been at some pains for nine days to draw nothing." He looked down at the ash. "I leave it where it stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-38.md:259:*and the* — there the box stood, with nothing in it — *set the apparatus over all the land, that the un-bound should not come again, who had near unmade the world before the ordering of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:It was the same wherever he turned. A river is not made, and a man is not; but a town is nothing else, from the ditch to the weathercock, and he had been given a thing that inclined toward every peg and course of it as a dog's nose goes to every post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:137:"A parcel's come the breadth of the country to a hayloft, and found it. That's eaten on. We've had the pot for ten nights and said nothing against it. Tonight we'll have what she'd give a carter with money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:153:He set a saucer over each of the two cups, to keep the dust out, and stood them at the table's end. One was a small pale woman's, who was above their heads with a lantern and twenty-one letters. The other had been carried under the lashing of his box, as an empty bowl, for eleven days; and the person whom it was kept for was a long way north along a hill-foot, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:157:He had his elbows on the board, and his eyes had gone down to the wood, idly, as a person's do who is full. It was an old table. It had been mended. *Bears*, the thing said, of the two trestles in the middle; and of the leg at the far end, by the old man's stick, it said *nothing*, as flatly as it had said so of a red square of tile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:219:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the second entry slowly, and left nothing out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:223:*Wind — Lira. Pressure — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron — Brom. Compression — Reydan. Ember — Karis. Shadow — Seln; source protected; sealed; twice in all; it asked for three hours last night and was told no. Storm — Daeva. Anchor — unnamed. Blade — Ephram. Tide — Oryn; ask first, every time; used on nobody this side of the snow, and five times on myself, the last two round the place and not in. Iron Wall — Vastin; held; entered, not told; deployment none. Rune — the reader; a made thing's lean; given; told; deployment none; it looks before I've said that it may, and I am learning to give it nothing to look at.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:257:He looked at the three strides. The old man slept in a stall now. The pallet by the gable had been rolled, and there was nothing between his knee and the blanket but boards. He could cross them. He could lift one corner of a horse-blanket, and see for himself whether the thing on the crown burned as it had always burned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:25:He had not raised his voice, and it carried all the same, as a bell carries. He was walking, not running, with his eyes on the heap of slate; and Cael, who had watched him read a wall a hundred times, saw him read this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:It was the third of the ten that he carried, a piece of Brom's own Iron come to him at second hand, and it asked under the soles of Cael's feet, low and even, as it had asked of every rut for nine days; it asked also which way the weight was going, since that was the half of its trade. It could have told him to a stone. It could have told him which slate would move next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:55:He had seen it come up a dozen times: on sprung oak, and in a hall, and on a road with hounds. It began under the collar and went over the skin like frost over a pane, and when it had gone over, a blade would not mark the man. A dray would have been nothing to him then. He would have stood up under it as under a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:Brom had a sigil in his pocket that would have made a stone of him. He had a certificate, somewhere, that said *Copper*, with a finding pinned to it that nobody had signed. He was twenty feet from a clerk who wrote down whatever was brought to his ledge; and he took a dray of slate on his bare shoulder, as a carter's son might have, with nothing to help him but his legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:Cael had his mouth open, and found that there was nothing in it that would serve. He was the one who ruled on slips. He could not rule on a shoulder; it had never been his, and the man under it was looking at him with perfect mildness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:"Put it in. Put in that it carried."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:She had said that she would, at the door, with the lamp in her hand; and she had looked at Brom's right shoulder as she said it, and then at Cael. So he came down last but one, with nothing to do but watch a box ride where a healer had said that nothing was to ride. It sat well. Brom carried it as he would have carried it on the other side, and if the knot spoke to him, he did not pass on what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:He saw it from the gallery's mouth, with the carried lamp still thirty strides ahead of him: a long dark thing stood on its end against the table's edge, beside the Hall's fourth sheet, with a fold of sacking on the floor at its foot. It was a plank. It was of oak, as black as the shelves, as long as a man's arm and two hands broad; and as the light came up he saw that one face of it had been cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:195:"It bears." The reed touched the air over a white pebble. "Take that out and the row comes down. There's nothing left for the rest to hang on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:199:"Hangs. It's carried. You could change one of those for another kind, and the row'd still stand up; it'd only be carrying something different." The reed moved. "The red's nothing. It holds nothing and nothing holds it. It's there so that the row fills its stone from edge to edge, as a mason packs a joint."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:Cael knelt two feet from that Path, and felt nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:257:"It doesn't work on a river, and it doesn't work on a man, and it doesn't work on a tree, because nobody chose where the branches went. It costs me my eyes for an hour after and nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:265:She did it without a pebble, on a leaf of her notebook, going from sheet to sheet of the Hall's twelve with the pencil held upright. She had forty-one kinds of sign, and had had them for the better part of a year; and she knew now, of the twelve that stood in one row, which kind bore and which was carried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-29.md:307:*Cleon has a board. He put it against her fourth sheet and it was the same line twice. Some signs bear, and some are carried, and some are nothing; she'll not say how many of each until all fifty-two are laid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:15:So she would say nothing next. She would read a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:That was the whole of the method, and she set it out in three lines. From the old hand's own spelling of *twelve*, and of *nine*, and of *seven*, she would write which letters in each were alike, and which letter of one word stood also in another; a pattern of same and different, and nothing more. She would fold that leaf; then she would carry it to the plank, and lay it beside the cutting, and see whether the signs repeated where the letters did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:55:So the boy carried the lamp, and held it low, and Lira went up a ladder that had not borne anybody's weight in a lifetime.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:73:On her folded leaf she wrote the three words as that clerk had written them, one under another. Then she wrote the pattern, as she had sworn to, and nothing else. In the first word, six letters; the third and the sixth alike. In the second, four; the first and the third alike, and its last the same as that pair in the word above. In the third, five; its second and fourth alike, and the same again as that letter; its third the same as the fifth of the first word; its last the same as the first of the second.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:89:She did not let herself breathe out. The seven. Five signs. The second was the hooked upright, and so was the fourth. The third was a sign like a fork. She kept her pencil on it, and said *up*; and the boy carried the light up the plank to the first row, where the fifth of the six stood out of the dark, and it was a fork. He brought the flame down again without being told. The last was a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:109:She found then that she was sitting on the cushion, and she did not remember crossing to the chair. The man had turned his head, by a very little, and seen where she sat; and he said nothing about it, and went back to his page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:121:"Twenty-three." Karis had that figure now. The last of the Ring's eleven had been laid that morning, and she had gone down all fifty-two with her pencil upright. "Seven kinds bear. Eleven are nothing at all. And there are twenty-three which are carried; and those, it appears, are what a word is spelled in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:133:She went along the carried signs only, looking for a group of the right length, in which every sign was one of her eight; and on the fifth sheet of the Ring she found one. It had three signs, and she did not say it aloud. She wrote down what the three letters were, in their order, and then she turned to the tally-book, and it was *ten*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:139:That gave her nothing new; it only made the eight more certain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:199:"Two nights ago there was a man in the smith's doorway, across from this arch, at an hour when nobody has business in a doorway. I saw him once. I did not speak of it, since one is not a finding." The mild voice did not alter. "Today there was a porter on the kerb opposite the cut's head, in the lane, with a frame at his feet. He sat there from the second bell until the third, and nothing was put on the frame. I do not know his face. I know his boots. They were a horseman's, with the spur-rests cut away, and so were the boots in the doorway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:273:Nobody sat on the kerb at the cut's head when they came down the lane. Cael looked at every doorway. Then he looked at the roof of the last building, where a parapet ran along above the slot, and saw nothing whatever, and took what comfort he could from that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:289:He stood at the foot, with the stick's head in his right hand and his left arm crooked, and she put the books into it one upon another, as wood is stacked. He held them as he would have held a seal on a cushion, with his chin lifted and his eyes on nothing; and when the third was laid on, and the dust of it went into his face, he did not sneeze. Cael thought that he had seldom seen a person so pleased to be a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:295:He had the lamp, and could do nothing but watch. She dropped the height of a man, and her knees went, and she took the whole of it on her two feet and her haunches, with the left arm folded tight against her ribs; and she came up out of the crouch without having put a finger to the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:He had nothing to set against that; he was afraid for her, and he had said so once, which was all that she had ever allowed him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-31.md:319:Karis had the tall books open along the table's edge, and the man with the reed was going back and forth between her sheets and the board, for a row that she wanted. It was dull work for a lamp. He let his breath go long, and watched the grey head and the square pale hands, as he had watched them for four days, with nothing else to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:The old man stood at the gable, under the blanket, as he stood every night until the last of them was down. He had an awl in his fingers again. There was nothing left on the tarred case that wanted stitching, and he had begun on the becket of Cael's box, without asking, where the rope had started to fray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:21:A room full of sleepers has a shape, and he had known this one's for five nights. Brom made the most of it, in the middle, breathing like something felled. Lira lay on her right side, a yard from Cael's elbow, and Karis by the boxes, small and quick, with a catch at the top of every breath that had been there since a cook-house. There was the old man's, upright, by the gable. And there was a place near the ladder's head where nothing was. He had learned to know it as a person knows where a rug lies in a dark room, because the floor does not ring there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:25:He heard nothing. The ladder did not creak; it never had, under that one. There was only a difference in the dark by the ladder's head, as when a coat that has hung on a door for a week is taken down, and the door is seen to be a door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:85:This was a night, and he had nothing in his sleeve. He looked down at the dark leather against his coat, squarely, as he would have looked at another man's file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:131:He had ruled nothing. He came down the hoist-stair as he had gone up, and the man in the doorway was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:He said nothing more. Cael waited, with his hair dripping, since it was plain that something was meant to follow; and nothing did. The man turned, and went to see whether the half-door was open, as though he had given a full report and been dismissed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:161:*Seln looked at it. Decided nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:165:They went up at the second bell. Brom carried on the right. The clerk wrote his two words and his figure before any of them had reached the bench, and did not lift his eyes, and the old man went by the hatch like somebody walking past a dog that has bitten him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:"Same tool. Same day, near enough." He did not turn round. "They're not packing. They bear nothing, and nothing's hung on them; but they're not red. They stand outside the row, like a figure at the foot of a bill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:179:"Hang from nothing I can see. They're by themselves."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-30.md:257:"I asked you for one, the first day, and I've spent the half of it on my own page; I've read that every morning before I'd so much as look at yours. That's put by. A week. Mine. From today, and nothing else on the desk. And at the end of it the whole lot goes up that stair, every sheet, and the fellow with the green book writes that it's gone." He rubbed his eyes. "He entered three boxes three days back, and he's written *the same* under them ever since. If they lie on my tables a month, he'll begin to wonder what *the same* is. I've been asked for things before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:11:"He's got straw to his chin," said Lira. She had carried it. "He said that he was obliged to the horse for the loan. I think he meant it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-36.md:31:Lira took the book across the gap, and read with her chin down; she came to the foot of the leaf, and went on looking at the place below the last line, where there was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-40.md:243:Cleon said, "Tomorrow the rest of the leaf. The list of parts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:227:*Held, with no ruling: a cord, two edges, a hair. A row that counted a sheet short. I have not laid a hand to an instrument since the hundred and sixty-eighth day.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:233:He did not open the brass upon any distance. He had a ruling against that, a hundred and twenty-four days old, and a hair in a small pale woman's notebook to remind him why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-37.md:263:He did not look again at the line, either; he turned upon the boards, so that the third box was between his shoulder and it, and drew the lantern nearer. He wrote the fourth entry on that page in the form that he had used once before, on the hundred and sixty-eighth day, for a thing that he could neither prove nor put down.

