editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:205:They made eleven miles that day, by Brom's reckoning, which was a mile more than a certain knee could do, and nobody remarked on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:The halting-ground lay in a fold of the moor beside a beck, with a dozen rings of stones on it, and black in every ring. No fire had been lit there since the first frost. The carters of that country ate cold on a road, and Cael had long since left off missing a flame at dusk. He saw Vastin look at the rings, and ask nothing, having been told why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:261:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:230:- **The first boat (flag):** first light on the 277th; eight passengers (a man with a ledger who carries the bag; two carters; a woman and a child; a drover; a lad; Vastin) and no goods; **the cordage stays on the western landing**. The bag had eleven pieces and none for the five. After noon she goes over again with twelve carters on foot, the bag and Ephram's letter; still no wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:321:- **The border road.** One of two roads out of the western landing. Alders, then moor; a halting-ground in a fold by a beck, eleven miles out, with a dozen dead rings; a long shoulder of hill; the cut; four miles of high moor to a spring; six more to the fork-stone; a long rise of a mile and more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:429:- **Vastin.** He told Cael beforehand that he would sit and could not rise without him; he sat on the bank at the three-mile post (the first time since the cut), in the inn's lower room (the second), and on the yard's bench (the third). **What he says of the file:** he read it first in the week before he came up the bluff, by the candles of four inns, and last on the morning he left a seal in a tray. **Why he declined (as repaired, in line with locked Book 6):** the reason he gave at Ostrand *was* the reason and he does not take it back: five young people had shown him what the office he served did with honest readings, and he would not lend it his name for one more; he wrote *declined* on a form that has no line for why, and would have written it had there been no night awake. **What he adds, and has told nobody else:** he had set out what a second reading could come to (a second silence, for which an office finds a word; or a plate that lit, which would have made his three days and nine thousand readings worth what a sheet of brass said); he could afford neither answer; it was the second that kept him awake; and when the pen was down he was relieved, on his own account, that he need never learn what the plate would say. "I had kept my hand off a door for a sound reason, and I was thankful of it for a poor one." He answers for the refusal and takes no credit for the gladness. His sentence from forty years of inquiries, "The instrument is not at fault. It has told you where its reach ends," is said aloud here; he wrote it once of a person, in an assessment that a seat declined to receive (Book 6). **His papers:** three sheets under one seal, in oilskin, which would open both gates and bring an officer down the hill; he does not know who reads the town's books at night. He has certified the town four times. In his window: every district he inspected had been told four days before that he was coming.

