+53. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: Oryn's terms restated — the packet gives only two fragments, "three holds owed" and "a stop on my own route"; its sense is given too (she comes east as a healer on her own round, and is on nobody's count). Staged as Oryn's, at the fence, in answer to Teague, with the six and Teague's four in hearing. Her own sentences are not written here — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `"There are three I haven't," said Oryn.\n\n{{JOIN}}\n\n"I'm a stop on my own route."`
+
+54. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "Sixth chair." / "On my terms. Which are the only kind." — the second line is Oryn's (Tier A 25 lists it with her Millrace lines); the plan does not pin the speaker of the first. Staged at the mill's bench at dusk, with Brom's bowls laid along it and Oryn's own bowl set at its end before an upturned keg. Lira could own "Sixth chair." (she told Oryn at this mill that there was a bowl); so could Brom, or Cael. Oryn answers, and sits — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `Lira said, "Sixth chair."\n\nOryn said, "On my terms. Which are the only kind."`
+
+55. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "You listened when it told you the second time." / "Once." / "Once is once more than I priced" — the third line is given without its stop, and no speaker is pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the forearm: Oryn reading what the arm has done since Thornwater, Lira answering in one word, Oryn's reply. That order of speakers is the natural one and is not the plan's — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `Oryn said, "You listened when it told you the second time."\n\n"Once," said Lira.\n\nOryn said, "Once is once more than I priced."`
+
+56. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "It held in a cut" — a fragment, with no speaker pinned. Staged on the plank, with Oryn's hands on the knee and her eyes shut, after Vastin has told her of the wall on the eleventh day. Oryn could own it (reading the joint: what it bore on the road and what it was then asked to bear in a stair); Vastin could own it, of the one time he stood on it with the stick up, two days ago; Cael could, speaking for him. Her verdict follows in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `"It held in a cut," said Oryn.`
+
+57. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "Noted" / "That's new." / "It's a useful word." / "It's his." — four lines, none pinned to a speaker. Staged on the plank as the close of the knee's reading: one of them answers her verdict with the word; she remarks on it; it is defended; and its owner is named. Vastin could own the first and third, Oryn the second, and Vastin or Lira the fourth (with a look toward Cael, who stands at the lean-to's post); the scene ends on it — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `"Noted," Vastin said.\n\nOryn said, "That's new."\n\nVastin said, "It's a useful word."\n\nOryn said, "It's his." She tilted her head toward Cael.`
+
+58. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `That had been settled in the yard, and it was the first thing that the field had cost anybody. The salt cart that had been put out of the mud was going down to the mill for meal, light, behind its two mules; and its carter had said to Teague, cap in hand, that a man's arm was owed something. Teague had looked at an old man with a slat on his leg, and at the length of a ridge.`
+   new: `That had been settled in the yard, and it was the first thing that the field had cost anybody. The salt cart that had been put out of the mud was going down to the mill for meal, light, behind its two mules; but the old man would walk the ridge with the ten.`
+
+59. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `"It's owed a seat," he had said. "For him. I'll not walk ten at the pace of one."`
+   new: `"Ten at the pace of the slowest," Teague had said.`
+
+60. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have had to wait for him, and had shut it, and gone to the tail-board. He had offered his copper, and the carter would not have it; he said that it was the captain's fare, and paid.`
+   new: `The old man had opened his mouth. Then he had looked at the nine who would have to match him, shut it, and set the stick in the road.`
+
+61. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `So a cart went on a furlong ahead, with a brown coat on its tail, and for the fourth day together Cael walked toward a pair of boots that hung.`
+   new: `So the cart went on a furlong ahead, and Cael walked behind the slow planting of the stick.`
+
+62. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `They came to the fork a little after noon, where one pair of ruts kept the ridge. The other went down the western face through a notch that wheels had worn, and the cart had stopped at its head, where the carter was locking a wheel. The old man had got down there, as he got down for every hill.`
+   new: `They came to the fork a little after noon, where one pair of ruts kept the ridge. The other went down the western face through a notch that wheels had worn, and Vastin reached its head last.`
+
+63. `manuscript/chapter-45.md`
+   old: `*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He rides, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*`
+   new: `*Two hundred and ninety-eighth day. The fork above Millrace; the flat rock. A hundred and twenty-eight. Vastin: nineteen of thirty. He walks, on the field's ruling. Karis opened the dividers. I lent them.*`
+
+64. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `[[TIER-B: "Is it a finding?" / "It's a leaf …" / "Then it's not mine." … "Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table." … "Lie still when I say. That's still the term." — elided in three places; the plan says only that Lira offers the leaf and that Oryn does not hear it. Staged in the lean-to after the finding: Lira reminds Oryn that she asked at the fork for whatever walked out of the archive, all of it, and says that there is a paper in Cael's coat which did; Oryn asks whether it is a finding; Lira answers; Oryn declines to have it read at her table, and restates her term. The fair copy stays in Cael's coat throughout and is not unfolded. Oryn's last line may belong here or to the first scene of this chapter, where her term is given in my words — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
+   new: `Oryn said, "Is it a finding?"\n\n"It's a leaf," Lira said.\n\n{{JOIN}}\n\nOryn said, "Then it's not mine."\n\n{{JOIN}}\n\n"Tell me on the road, if there's road. Not at my table."\n\n{{JOIN}}\n\n"Lie still when I say. That's still the term."`
+
+65. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"You went stiff, on the plank, somewhere past the middle of it. She didn't see; she'd her eyes shut. I hadn't."`
+   new: `"You were awake, on the plank, somewhere past the middle of it."`
+
+66. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"Something came," he said. "I didn't ask for it, and I've not got a name for it that I'd put my hand to."`
+   new: `"I was counting," he said. "I couldn't sleep under a healer's hands."`
+
+67. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"Is it bad?"`
+   new: `"Then sleep now."`
+
+68. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"I don't know what it is."`
+   new: `"I will."`
+
+69. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"That's twice now that you've given me a clerk's answer and looked sorry about it." The hay rustled; she had put her back against a wheel. "I'll not dig. I dug at you all the time that we were on a bluff, and all I ever turned up was what you'd have told me in a week if I'd let you alone. You'll tell me when it's a thing."`
+   new: `The hay rustled; she had lain down.`
+
+70. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"On the road."`
+   new: `"Good night."`
+
+71. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"If there's road," said Lira, and he heard her lie down.`
+   new: `"Good night," said Lira.`
+
+72. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `He listened until her breathing had gone long. She had asked him a thing, and had been given less than the whole of it, and had said that she would wait; and he did not know many people who could do the third.`
+   new: `He listened until her breathing had gone long. He had kept the change out of his face and the finding to himself.`
+
+73. `manuscript/chapter-46.md`
+   old: `"Twelve," he said. "And a mule."`
+   new: `There were twelve. And a mule.`

