/bin/zsh -lc "rg -n '\\bthaw\\b' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-*.md | head -n 30
rg -n 'one hundred and twenty-ninth|first snow' editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-*.md | head -n 30" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:227:"He's forty marks in his box against your coming," said the carter. "He told me to say that, and to say that he'd not paid a forty since the thaw." He scratched his neck. "I'm to tell you nothing about the top of his sheet, for there's nothing. The man wasn't down when I left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:"He's done," said the ferryman, to the hoof. "I've known it since the thaw. He stops, and I shout, and he goes on because he's honest, and it's a poor trade for the both of us." He let the foot down at last. "What's that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:"There's none. I've no paper for it and I'm not sending to a plank for some." He scratched his jaw with the stick's end. "You walked my hill for seventy and brought me a no. I'd have paid the seventy three times over in carts, by next thaw, if you'd brought me a yes to please me. I can find a bed for three boxes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"That's been on my oak since the thaw," he said. "It's the carters' well, at the fork, where the south road splits. It went sour. A lad was sent down after a bucket, and they wound him up asleep; he woke, and he's not been right about a ladder since." He drew five strokes on the bark. "Nobody's been down it; they water at a stream a mile on, and curse me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:255:The frost had heaved the top course of the lining at the thaw, and six stones had gone in, and he felt for them with his feet. He stooped for each one, to the shoulder in that water, and got it into the loop, and jerked the rope twice, and it went up past him, dripping, toward the coin of light. He brought up the bucket, and a length of chain; and he brought up something else, which had gone in after water a long while ago and was past being anything, and he sent that up with his face turned to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:"It is. It'll be sour when I'm dead." He laid the knife down. "I put the pair of them on two different stocks with my own hand, the year that I married her, and they've never agreed about anything since. You'll not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket, on the one day. You want to see them both at the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:91:It was not said as a proposal. Cael heard under it a cup of barley on a trestle and a number with her thumb over it, and a small bald man telling her that she wanted to see them both at the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:171:"They'll not hire till the thaw," said Brom. "Nor feed a stranger. I asked him once what a crew does out here when the board shuts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-54.md:133:"I'm a cart's length from the rest of you, on a thing that I can't get off. If it comes on to freeze, I'll be here till the thaw." Brom was turning himself about. "It's a step. I've come down worse with a box on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"She goes on the low side of nothing. You go on the low side of her. She'll not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand, and it's no use arguing; she was like that when I got her. Her near hind's been shod since the Oxhollow road, and it'll want looking at by the thaw. She answers to a click." She showed him, with her tongue. "She's never had a name, and she's not to be given one. It's a beast. You name a thing and then you're sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:287:"Kinds. Forty-one on the Fallow, when the doubtful ones are taken out or let in as this wall bids, and forty-one in the courses here. I have been over the courses of these four walls for six hours, looking for a forty-second, and there is none in them; and there is not one of the forty-one that is wanting." She looked up. "A man with apple trees told me that I wanted to see them both at the thaw. I have seen them both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:49:"That's one," said Oryn. "You'll not close it on a rope before the thaw. Send in the child."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-56.md:143:He had told himself at a grey stone that a captain who asks a question he has no column for has only given himself something more to carry. It had been sound, and he had had three to carry then. He had four now. And he was proposing to stand on some floor beside that boy after the thaw, if the boy went where he seemed to be going. A man does not walk his people up to a thing that he has been too careful to ask the name of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:31:"I'm three weeks late there, and three weeks is three weeks, and there's an old woman at Thornwater whose knee I said I'd see, and I said it after the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-58.md:73:They went up, with Brom on the low side of the mule and three boxes swaying. It was a great whale's back of pale grass, a mile broad on its crown, with the country falling off on either hand. In every fold of its flanks a line of white lay, where the frost had not lifted for a week and would not now lift before the thaw. From above they looked like chalk strokes ruled on a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:The inn's keeper was a slow heavy man with a cloth over his shoulder, who had drawn their jugs for a hundred and twenty days and had never been asked for anything else. She told him what she wanted: a dry floor, and a door that shut, and a table that nobody ate at. It was for paper, she said. She would have it until the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:51:It was a quarter of an hour before the boat came home again. It brought a wet canvas bag, and the bag went up the bank to the plank under the eave, where Pike had set out his book for the last time before the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:85:There was a third that he owed, to a friend on a bluff who lost to him every time he ran it. He had no line for that one yet. It would go by the first boat of the thaw, and he wrote in his own book that it was to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:151:She was a very small old woman. Oryn had mended that knee at a mill, thirteen days before, and had seen its owner go away up a valley on a hurdle between two grandsons. She came over the low place every year before the snow, to her daughter's hearth in this long house; and Oryn had told her, as the hurdle was lifted, that she would see the knee here, after the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:153:"I said after the thaw," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:161:Oryn got down on her heels in the frost, and put both hands round the joint. Her lips went to three. It was all there: the old banks, worn as smooth as a doorstep, and the current going through the place where a ladder had been as though no ladder had ever stood in it. She had promised that knee a look after the thaw, and it had walked over a hill to be looked at before the snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:183:"He goes down to the board at first light, with my bark. He'll not come up again this side of the thaw, if that does what it looks like." The woman jerked her chin at the north. "If you've anything to send to that crew of yours, it's tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:195:So the arm would wait until she could have it between her two hands, and that would not be before the thaw. The girl knew who was to say when. There was nothing in that to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:215:*Route's whole. The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks. After the thaw: bring the map, and lie still when I say. — O.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:225:It was a hand that Cael had never seen. It was square, and stiff, and it pressed, like a person lifting something heavy. He read the four lines out, as they stood, a stop at a time: the route, and a knee, and what three weeks had cost; and then the thaw, and a map, and what he was to do when he was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:235:"After the thaw," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:259:Brom: "After the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:147:"Nor have I. Sixty yards, four-strand." He knocked a last cake of ice into the river. "I'll not want it this side of the thaw. There's one more crossing in her, tomorrow, if that shelf lets me; the post comes down to the far bank for it. Then I take my lines in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:"It's first snow," said Pike. "I rule every heading off at first snow. A crew gets its season said, the once, in a line, and a number set against the line; and the number's what I carry over to the new cloth at the thaw. I've done forty today. This is the last."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-59.md:221:"After the thaw. Whatever's on that bearing. My crew comes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:"They came off the tops with the first snow. Ten days." The factor spoke lower than he had in the yard. "There's a lee under that rock, and they've found it. They've lain on my line ever since, and they've not shifted forty yards. I've sixty head in that byre." It stood directly below the grey backs, four hundred yards down, with the long house beside it. "If those go, they go downhill. They'll go through my beasts and my roof and my wife's mother, and I'll not be standing here to pay anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:85:"I said first snow, near enough. Then I said that I'd say. Then I went north, and it snowed on the pair of us." She nodded at the wool. "How long has that been up?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:85:At the second stride the ground changed under his left boot. Something long and square lay a hand deep, bedded on stone, and it sent his weight away evenly into the bank beneath, as the blue ice had on a good day. It was the baulk that the scow had sat on from the first snow to the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:161:There was a child from the top hold with a chest that had rattled since the first snow, and Oryn was a long while over her, and came to the door when it was done with one hand on the post. There was the heavy carter with the grey beard. He had been brought off the river ice on a hurdle a hundred and eleven days ago, and he had coughed ever since; he sat down before her as a man sits who expects to be charged. And there was the hide-woman, who held out two hands cracked across every knuckle, like old harness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:197:"It's the season's. I rule every heading off at first snow, and I set a number against it, for what that crew brought home out of what it took. I'm not telling you how I get it; it's thirty years of getting it." He loaded the brush. "At the thaw I carry the number over, and they hang in the order of it. A hold reads down from the top until it comes to a crew that it can pay. That's all the cloth is. It doesn't say who's strongest. It says who came back with the goods."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:"Three." Lira was looking at the grey wool across her own breast, and at a knot in it that was not hers. "She said first snow, near enough. Then she said that she'd say when, and then she wrote us a letter with a thaw in it. So mine runs out on the same day as his, and I can't so much as shovel while I wait for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:211:"That's a cold term." Pike set the horn pot down into its hole. "A roof, a well-house and a fence, and the fence is yours. The holds shut their gates and mend what they've got. I've had years with nothing on this oak from first snow to first boat but the nails."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:251:"I've not looked for one. That's blank on purpose." He tapped the space with the feather. "A blank that a man leaves because he'd nothing to put is a hole in his book. One that he leaves because he'll not put a wrong thing is a kind of entry. I read it out as a blank at first snow, twice, in a full room. Nobody scratched."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:297:He had made lists all his life of what he was and what he owed. He had never made one of what he did not know, with the gaps left standing in it on purpose; and he saw who had shown him that a book might be kept so. A one-armed man had turned a ledger round at first snow, in front of fifty people, to show a boy the place where nothing was written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:103:Every crew that had lain up for the cold was there, and a good many carters. The hide-woman had a stool by the hearth, and the three grey men had their board on a keg and were not throwing. It was the hour at which a one-armed man read out what the day had brought to his plank; and the room had gone quiet for it, as it had on a night of first snow, when a crew came in with white on its shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-44.md:151:It was the season's line, with its ruled space at the end. Cael had seen that space left blank on purpose at first snow, and carried blank over a thaw onto a new cloth.

diff --git a/editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-007/plan-audit.md b/editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-007/plan-audit.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..54837a19a9610c50b7f2301957ac51be5bb5827f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/movement-007/plan-audit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,474 @@
+# Verdict: REPAIR
+
+Scope: restore every protected marker with the source speaker; repair the grade so the four ages come from each site's first sheet rather than a running annual series; put Vastin on foot for the road and cut; remove the premature belonging-offer; repair three thaw counts, two edition-vocabulary uses of *bearer*, the Millrace arrival speaker, the hidden-turn leak to Lira, the dawn-count speaker, and stake thirty-two's custody. Everything else can stand with the line fixes below. This is not a request to import source staging.
+
+## 1. Continuity with the closed text
+
+### Findings
+
+- **Vastin must walk the road and the cut.** The draft gives him the dray's tail-board for three days and the salt cart on the 298th (`chapter-42.md:49–61,127–131,185–221,285`; `chapter-43.md:9–13,45,67,125,149,171,219`; `chapter-45.md:187–211`). That contradicts the packet's physical lock, “the road and the cut walked again on a bad knee” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:45`), and the closed approach state in which he walks beside Cael (`STATE_LEDGER.md:361`). His earlier official rides to Treswick and four certifications are recollection, not present travel, and may remain (`chapter-42.md:139–143,203`). Apply fixes 2–12, 15–27 and 58–63.
+- **The factor's seat is a premature Book 9 belonging beat.** The annual seat “for the whole of a cold term” and Vastin's non-answer (`chapter-43.md:25–27`) anticipates the reserved question of where he belongs. The wage to the five is legitimate and Brom may decline it. Apply fixes 13–14.
+- **The grade's derivation is wrong.** The draft makes 61/94/138/206 running series numbers, with one sheet per year and all omitted sheets still in a drawer (`chapter-45.md:79–99,107–109`). The binding plan says **ages by first sheet** (`BOOK_MAP.md:82`; `packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:58`), while closed Movement 3 established only six annual rows (`chapter-11.md:221–247`). The four values may remain, but the paper dates each site from its first sheet. The fifth site's numeric age remains withheld. Apply fixes 38–44.
+- **Stake thirty-two loses custody.** It leaves Vastin's case for the grade (`chapter-45.md:35–39`) and never returns, while the dawn load silently assumes the case remains coherent (`chapter-46.md:293–307`). Return it to the tarred case before departure; it remains undriven. Apply fix 47.
+- **The Millrace count has the wrong speaker and mints a role.** The draft gives “Eleven off the ridge” to a woman with a net (`chapter-45.md:239–247`); the source gives it to the ford-keeper (`source chapter-18.md:5–9`). This is protected-speaker restoration, not imported staging. Apply fixes 48–51.
